window.ATLAS_AGORA = {"questions":[{"id":"lying","title":"Is it ever right to lie?","intro":"Your friend runs into your house and hides in the closet. A minute later a man with a knife knocks and asks where your friend went. You could tell the truth, stay silent, or lie.","sides":{"yes":"Sometimes lying is right","no":"Lying is always wrong"},"compass":"q1","predict":{"prompt":"Kant says you must not lie even to the murderer. Which part of his view do critics attack most often?","options":["That a lie harms humanity in general","That you are to blame for a lie's results but not for the truth's","That you owe the truth to everyone, even a murderer"],"reveal":"The third. Benjamin Constant replied at once that you only owe the truth to people who have a right to it, and a murderer has none. Christine Korsgaard later argued that even Kant's own test of universal law lets you deceive a liar who is using your honesty to do evil.","source":"Cards lying-constant, lying-korsgaard"},"order":["lying-kant-murderer","lying-constant","lying-kant-reply-constant","lying-universal-law","lying-korsgaard","lying-mill","lying-augustine","lying-purpose-reply","lying-silence","lying-white-lies","lying-bok"],"stance":"Before we begin: would you lie to the murderer at the door?"},{"id":"strangers","title":"Must we help strangers far away?","intro":"Walking past a shallow pond, you see a small child drowning. Wading in will ruin your new shoes. Almost everyone says you must save the child. But the price of those shoes, given to the right charity, might save a child's life on the other side of the world.","sides":{"yes":"We must help, even far away","no":"We are not strictly required to"},"compass":"q1","predict":{"prompt":"Peter Singer's argument has three premises. Which one do critics attack most?","options":["Dying from lack of food or medicine is bad","If you can stop something bad without giving up anything comparably important, you ought to","Giving money to aid agencies really can prevent such deaths"],"reveal":"The second. The distance objection, the 'why me?' objection, the demandingness objection and Williams' integrity objection all aim at it. The third premise is attacked too, by critics who doubt that aid works as well as rescuing a child from a pond.","source":"Cards strangers-distance, strangers-why-me, strangers-demandingness, strangers-williams-integrity, strangers-wenar"},"order":["strangers-singer-pond","strangers-distance","strangers-distance-reply","strangers-unger","strangers-why-me","strangers-why-me-reply","strangers-demandingness","strangers-moderate","strangers-williams-integrity","strangers-integrity-reply","strangers-wenar","strangers-evidence-reply"],"stance":"Before we begin: is letting a child far away die as bad as walking past the drowning child?"},{"id":"killing","title":"Is killing worse than letting die?","intro":"A runaway trolley will kill five workers. You can pull a switch to send it onto a side track, where it will kill one. Or you could push a large man off a bridge to stop it. Five lives against one either way, yet most people feel the two cases are very different.","sides":{"yes":"Killing is worse than letting die","no":"Killing is not in itself worse"},"compass":"q3","predict":{"prompt":"In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, what share of professional philosophers said you should push the man off the footbridge to save the five?","options":["About 20%","About 50%","About 75%"],"reveal":"About 22% said push and about 56% said don't push. Yet in the ordinary switch case, about 63% said pull the switch and only about 13% said don't. Same numbers, very different verdicts. That gap is what this debate is trying to explain.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["killing-foot-duties","killing-thomson-bystander","killing-thomson-turning","killing-footbridge","killing-double-effect","killing-loop","killing-rachels","killing-rachels-starving","killing-additive-fallacy","killing-greene","killing-singer-debunk","killing-berker"],"stance":"Before we begin: would you push the man off the footbridge to save the five?"},{"id":"freewill","title":"Do we have free will?","intro":"Every event in your brain may be caused by earlier events, all the way back to before you were born. If so, was your choice of breakfast this morning really up to you? And if not, can anyone deserve praise or blame?","sides":{"yes":"We have free will","no":"We do not (or not the kind that matters)"},"compass":"q8","predict":{"prompt":"Which view of free will is most popular among professional philosophers?","options":["Compatibilism: free will fits with determinism","Libertarianism: free will needs a break in the causal chain","No free will"],"reveal":"Compatibilism, by a wide margin. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey about 59% of faculty accepted or leaned toward compatibilism, about 19% libertarianism and about 11% no free will. The rest gave other answers.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["freewill-consequence","freewill-hume","freewill-conditional-objection","freewill-reactive-attitudes","freewill-frankfurt","freewill-dilemma-defense","freewill-gstrawson","freewill-dennett","freewill-reid","freewill-luck","freewill-pereboom","freewill-hard-line"],"stance":"Before we begin: was your choice of breakfast this morning really up to you?"},{"id":"identity","title":"What makes you the same person over time?","intro":"You share almost no memories with the baby in your old photos, and most of the cells have been replaced. Yet you say 'that's me.' What makes it true? Your memories and personality, your body, or something else?","sides":{"yes":"Psychology: memory and mental continuity","no":"Something else: the body, the animal, or no lasting self"},"compass":"q10","predict":{"prompt":"A teleporter scans you, destroys your body, and builds an exact copy on Mars. What did professional philosophers say happens to you?","options":["Most say you survive","Most say you die","They are split almost evenly"],"reveal":"They are split. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey about 35% said you survive and about 40% said you die; the rest gave other answers. On personal identity in general, about 44% favored a psychological view, about 19% a biological view and about 15% a 'further fact' view.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["identity-locke","identity-reid-officer","identity-continuity-reply","identity-butler","identity-quasi-memory","identity-williams","identity-parfit-fission","identity-olson","identity-transplant","identity-hume-bundle","identity-buddha"],"stance":"Before we begin: is the baby in your old photos really you?"},{"id":"mind","title":"Is the mind just the brain?","intro":"Neuroscientists can watch your brain light up when you taste chocolate. But the taste itself, the rich sweet feeling, seems to be something extra. Is your mind nothing more than a very complicated organ, or is there more to it?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: the mind is physical","no":"No: something is left out"},"compass":"q6","predict":{"prompt":"In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, how did professional philosophers divide on the mind?","options":["Most are physicalists","Most are non-physicalists","Roughly even"],"reveal":"Most are physicalists, but far from all: about 52% accepted or leaned toward physicalism and about 32% toward non-physicalism. The rest gave other answers.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["mind-descartes-conceivability","mind-descartes-divisibility","mind-interaction","mind-causal-closure","mind-nagel-bat","mind-mary","mind-ability","mind-dennett-mary","mind-jackson-recants","mind-zombie","mind-phenomenal-concepts","mind-goff"],"stance":"Before we begin: is the taste of chocolate just something happening in your brain?"},{"id":"evil","title":"Can a good God allow suffering?","intro":"A forest fire traps a young deer. It burns and suffers for days before it dies, and no one ever knows. If an all-powerful, all-knowing, perfectly good God exists, why would such things happen?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: God and suffering are compatible","no":"No: suffering counts against God"},"compass":"q19","predict":{"prompt":"What share of professional philosophers accept or lean toward theism (belief in God)?","options":["About 20%","About 50%","About 75%"],"reveal":"About 19%. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey about 67% accepted or leaned toward atheism. Even so, the logical problem of evil is widely thought to have been answered; the live debate is about evidence.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["evil-mackie","evil-hume-philo","evil-plantinga","evil-mackie-good-choices","evil-natural","evil-natural-reply","evil-hick","evil-rowe","evil-skeptical-theism","evil-moral-paralysis","evil-leibniz"],"stance":"Before we begin: could a perfectly good God have a reason to let the deer suffer in the fire?"},{"id":"realism","title":"Is morality objective?","intro":"\"Torturing a child for fun is wrong.\" Is that true the way \"water is H2O\" is true, whatever anyone thinks? Or is it more like \"broccoli is gross\", an expression of how we feel or what our culture taught us?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: there are objective moral truths","no":"No: morality is not objective"},"compass":"q4","predict":{"prompt":"Do most professional philosophers think there are objective moral truths?","options":["Yes, most are moral realists","No, most are anti-realists","Roughly even"],"reveal":"Yes. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey about 62% accepted or leaned toward moral realism and about 26% toward anti-realism.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["realism-naturalism","realism-moore","realism-synthetic-identity","realism-ayer","realism-frege-geach","realism-mackie-relativity","realism-disagreement-reply","realism-mackie-queerness","realism-companions","realism-street","realism-enoch-third-factor","realism-enoch-indispensability"],"stance":"Before we begin: is 'torturing a child for fun is wrong' true whatever anyone thinks?"},{"id":"meaning","title":"Does life need a meaning to be worth living?","intro":"Suppose you learn for certain that in a few billion years the sun will swell up and scorch the Earth, and nothing anyone ever did will leave a trace. You still love your friends, your music and your morning walk. Is that enough to make your life worth living, or does it need a point that lasts?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: a life worth living needs meaning","no":"No: life can be worth living without it"},"compass":"l1","predict":{"prompt":"Richard Taylor imagines the gods giving Sisyphus, who must roll a stone uphill forever, a deep desire to roll stones. What does Taylor say this changes?","options":["Nothing: his life is just as pointless as before","His life now has meaning for him, though nothing outside him has changed","Nothing yet, but it would if the stones finally built something"],"reveal":"The second. Taylor says meaning comes from within us, from caring about what we do, so the drugged Sisyphus is fulfilled. Susan Wolf replies that something is still missing: a happy stone roller is not living a meaningful life, because what he loves isn't worth loving.","source":"Cards meaning-taylor-sisyphus, meaning-wolf"},"order":["meaning-tolstoy","meaning-nagel-million","meaning-nozick-limits","meaning-nagel-irony","meaning-camus","meaning-camus-smuggles","meaning-taylor-sisyphus","meaning-schopenhauer","meaning-wolf","meaning-rawls-grass","meaning-metz"],"stance":"Before we begin: if nothing you do will last, is your life still worth living?"},{"id":"death","title":"Is death bad for the one who dies?","intro":"Two people die in the same accident, one at 20 and one at 90. Everyone at the first funeral says what a tragedy it is. But the dead feel nothing, want nothing and miss nothing. So who, exactly, has been harmed?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: death harms the one who dies","no":"No: death is nothing to the one who dies"},"compass":"l2","predict":{"prompt":"Lucretius says the endless time after your death mirrors the endless time before your birth, which no one minds. What was Thomas Nagel's best-known reply?","options":["The time before birth was bad for us too; we just don't notice","You could not have been born much earlier and still be you, but you could have died later","Fearing death is natural, so the argument must be wrong"],"reveal":"The second. Being born later takes nothing you could have had, Nagel said, but dying earlier does. In a footnote he admitted a problem Robert Nozick raised: imagine people who grow from spores that could have hatched earlier. Shelly Kagan later pressed the same case.","source":"Cards death-nagel-asymmetry, death-kagan-spores"},"order":["death-epicurus","death-nagel-deprivation","death-rosenbaum","death-feldman","death-lucretius","death-nagel-asymmetry","death-kagan-spores","death-future-bias","death-williams-categorical","death-williams-tedium","death-fischer"],"stance":"Before we begin: is death bad for the person who dies, even though they feel nothing?"},{"id":"animals","title":"Is it wrong to eat animals?","intro":"At a barbecue, a friend hands you a pork sausage. You know that the pig it came from could feel pain and fear, probably lived in a crowded shed, and was killed at about six months old. You also know it tastes good, and that people have eaten meat for as long as there have been people.","sides":{"yes":"Yes: eating animals is wrong","no":"No: it can be permissible"},"compass":"e3","predict":{"prompt":"Roger Scruton says only beings who can take on duties can have rights, so animals have none. Which objection do critics raise most often?","options":["That animals can feel pain","That some humans, like newborn babies, can't take on duties either, yet clearly have rights","That rights are just a legal fiction"],"reveal":"The second. It is called the 'argument from marginal cases', or from species overlap: any line drawn to leave out all animals seems to leave out some humans too. Carl Cohen replies that rights go with the kind of being you are, not your individual abilities, and James Rachels answers that this treats individuals by their group instead of by what they are actually like.","source":"Cards animals-marginal-cases, animals-cohen-kind, animals-rachels-individualism"},"order":["animals-singer-suffering","animals-inefficacy","animals-kagan-threshold","animals-regan-rights","animals-carruthers","animals-rowlands","animals-scruton","animals-marginal-cases","animals-cohen-kind","animals-rachels-individualism","animals-larder","animals-singer-replaceability","animals-salt"],"stance":"Before we begin: would you take the pork sausage at the barbecue?"},{"id":"machines","title":"Can a machine think?","intro":"You spend an evening chatting online with someone funny, well read and quick with a comeback. Afterwards you learn it was a computer program. Was anyone there thinking, or was it only very clever shuffling of words?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: machines could think","no":"No: they only imitate thinking"},"compass":"m6","predict":{"prompt":"In Searle's Chinese Room, a man who knows no Chinese follows a rulebook and gives perfect Chinese answers. What is the best-known reply?","options":["The man would slowly learn Chinese","The man doesn't understand, but the whole system (man, rulebook and papers) does","Computers work faster than any man could"],"reveal":"The second. This is the 'systems reply'. Searle answered at once: let the man memorize the whole rulebook, so that he is the entire system, and he still understands no Chinese. Dennett replies that our intuitions about such an absurdly huge feat can't be trusted.","source":"Cards machines-systems-reply, machines-searle-memorize, machines-dennett-intuition"},"order":["machines-turing-test","machines-blockhead","machines-functionalism","machines-china-brain","machines-chalmers-replacement","machines-searle-room","machines-searle-syntax","machines-systems-reply","machines-searle-memorize","machines-dennett-intuition","machines-lovelace","machines-turing-learning"],"stance":"Before we begin: if a chatbot could talk exactly like a person, would you say it thinks?"},{"id":"skepticism","title":"Can we know anything about the world outside our minds?","intro":"Last night you dreamed you were on a beach, and while the dream lasted you were sure it was real. Right now you seem to be reading on a screen. How do you know this isn't another dream, or a computer feeding signals to a brain in a lab?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: we know about the world","no":"No: we can't rule out deception"},"compass":"k4","predict":{"prompt":"What share of professional philosophers think we really do know about an external world?","options":["Fewer than half","About two-thirds","About four in five"],"reveal":"The third. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey about 80% accepted or leaned toward 'non-skeptical realism' about the external world; only about 5% leaned toward skepticism and about 7% toward idealism. Agreeing that we know is the easy part. Saying how, without begging the question, is where the cards below disagree.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["skepticism-dream","skepticism-coherence","skepticism-stroud","skepticism-ignorance","skepticism-moore","skepticism-begs-question","skepticism-derose","skepticism-putnam","skepticism-recent-vat","skepticism-russell","skepticism-hume","skepticism-vogel"],"stance":"Before we begin: can you be sure you aren't dreaming right now?"},{"id":"god","title":"Does God exist?","intro":"Walking on a beach, you find a watch in the sand. You'd never guess it simply washed up by chance; someone made it. Now look at your own eye, or at a universe whose laws happen to allow stars and life. Does that point to a maker, or is there a better explanation?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: the arguments point to God","no":"No: the arguments fail"},"compass":"k9h","predict":{"prompt":"Paley argued that living things, like watches, must have a designer. What is the most famous objection?","options":["Watches aren't alive, so the comparison fails","Natural selection can produce the look of design without a designer","The universe is too big to have been designed"],"reveal":"The second. Darwin showed how small useful variations, piling up over vast stretches of time, can build organs like the eye. Defenders of design, like Robin Collins, moved the argument to the fine-tuning of the laws of physics, which natural selection can't explain because it only works once life exists.","source":"Cards god-darwin, god-fine-tuning"},"order":["god-first-cause","god-hume-parts","god-leibniz-books","god-van-inwagen","god-paley","god-darwin","god-fine-tuning","god-multiverse","god-hacking","god-hiddenness","god-murray","god-teapot","god-plantinga"],"stance":"Before we begin: does the universe look to you as if someone designed it?"},{"id":"speech","title":"Should hate speech be banned?","intro":"In 1977 a neo-Nazi group announced it would march, in uniform and carrying swastikas, through Skokie, Illinois, a town where many Holocaust survivors lived. The town tried to stop them; the courts said the march was protected speech. In Germany, displaying the swastika like that is a crime. Who got it right?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: some should be banned","no":"No: answer it with more speech"},"compass":"s4","predict":{"prompt":"Jeremy Waldron defends laws against hate speech. What does he say is wrong with it?","options":["It attacks the targeted group's standing as equal members of society","It hurts people's feelings","It is always false"],"reveal":"The first. Waldron insists the law should not protect people from offense, which is just a feeling. What hate speech damages, he says, is a public good: everyone's assurance that they will be treated as equals. Mill had already warned that being offended by someone's views gives you no right to silence them.","source":"Cards speech-waldron, speech-mill-offense"},"order":["speech-waldron","speech-dworkin","speech-waldron-legitimacy","speech-mill-truth","speech-schauer","speech-langton","speech-brandeis","speech-lawrence","speech-fighting-words","speech-feinberg","speech-mill-offense","speech-strossen","speech-other-democracies"],"stance":"Before we begin: should the neo-Nazis have been allowed to march through Skokie?"},{"id":"inequality","title":"Is economic inequality unjust?","intro":"Two children are born on the same day in the same city. One will inherit a fortune and go to the best schools; the other's parents work two jobs to pay the rent. Neither child did anything to earn their start. Is the gap between them unjust, or just unlucky?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: large inequalities are unjust","no":"No: not in itself"},"compass":"s8","predict":{"prompt":"Nozick imagines fans freely paying to watch Wilt Chamberlain play basketball, until Chamberlain is far richer than anyone. What is G.A. Cohen's best-known reply?","options":["The fans were tricked into paying","Chamberlain's wealth gives him power over other people, who never agreed to that","Playing basketball isn't real work"],"reveal":"The second. The fans agreed to give Chamberlain 25 cents each, Cohen says, but not to the power his fortune brings over everyone else, including people who never bought a ticket. Nozick's answer is that keeping any pattern of equality would mean forbidding free exchanges between consenting adults.","source":"Cards inequality-cohen-wilt, inequality-nozick-forbid"},"order":["inequality-rawls-lottery","inequality-nozick-assets","inequality-murphy-nagel","inequality-hayek","inequality-nozick-wilt","inequality-nozick-forbid","inequality-cohen-wilt","inequality-frankfurt","inequality-smith-linen","inequality-anderson","inequality-enough-to-be-equal","inequality-parfit","inequality-temkin"],"stance":"Before we begin: is it unjust that one child is born rich and another poor?"},{"id":"euthanasia","title":"Is euthanasia ever morally permissible?","intro":"A man with advanced cancer is in constant pain that drugs can no longer control. He is clear-headed, and he asks his doctor for a lethal injection. In many countries the law lets the doctor switch off his treatment and let him die, but forbids the injection.","sides":{"yes":"Yes: sometimes it is","no":"No: it is always wrong"},"predict":{"prompt":"David Velleman argues that giving patients the option of euthanasia can harm them, even if no one ever takes it. Why?","options":["Doctors might make mistakes","Once the option exists, patients may feel they have to justify staying alive","It costs hospitals too much"],"reveal":"The second. Before, a sick person could simply go on living without needing a reason; once death is on the menu, staying alive becomes a choice they may be asked to defend, especially if they feel like a burden. Defenders of a right to die, like Dworkin, answer that forcing people to die in ways they find degrading is the greater harm.","source":"Cards euthanasia-velleman, euthanasia-dworkin"},"order":["euthanasia-rachels","euthanasia-double-effect","euthanasia-thomson","euthanasia-dworkin","euthanasia-velleman","euthanasia-aquinas","euthanasia-hume","euthanasia-kant","euthanasia-dignity-lost","euthanasia-slope","euthanasia-battin","euthanasia-slope-real"],"stance":"Before we begin: should the doctor be allowed to give the dying man the injection he asks for?"},{"id":"beauty","title":"Is beauty objective?","intro":"You stop in your tracks at a sunset; your friend glances up and keeps scrolling. You think a song is a masterpiece; your cousin calls it noise. Is one of you right, or is beauty only in the eye of the beholder?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: beauty is really there","no":"No: it's in the beholder"},"compass":"a1","predict":{"prompt":"Hume starts his famous essay by saying that beauty exists only in the mind. Where does he end up?","options":["All tastes are equally good","There is a real standard of taste: the joint verdict of true judges","Beauty is an eternal Form, as Plato said"],"reveal":"The second. Anyone who ranked a hack poet with Milton would be plainly wrong, Hume says, and the standard is set by critics with delicate taste, practice and no prejudice. Jerrold Levinson asks the follow-up question critics still argue about: why should I care what those ideal critics prefer?","source":"Cards beauty-hume-ogilby, beauty-levinson"},"order":["beauty-hume-sentiment","beauty-hume-ogilby","beauty-levinson","beauty-santayana","beauty-kant","beauty-plato","beauty-tastes-vary","beauty-hume-time","beauty-dutton","beauty-sibley","beauty-kolbel"],"stance":"Before we begin: when you and a friend disagree about a song, can one of you be wrong?"},{"id":"disobey","title":"Is it right to break an unjust law?","intro":"In 1955 in Montgomery, Alabama, Rosa Parks refused a bus driver's order to give up her seat to a white passenger, and was arrested for breaking the city's segregation law. Today she is honored as a hero. But if everyone broke whichever laws they judged unjust, could any system of law survive?","stance":"Before we begin: would you ever break a law you thought was unjust?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: sometimes we should disobey","no":"No: we should obey the law"},"compass":"s7","predict":{"prompt":"In Plato's Crito, the Laws of Athens tell Socrates he must not escape his unjust death sentence. But at his trial, in the Apology, Socrates had told the jury something surprising. What?","options":["That he would obey any verdict the court gave","That if they freed him on condition that he stop doing philosophy, he would not obey","That unjust laws are not real laws"],"reveal":"The second. He said he would obey the god rather than the jury, and he reminded them that he had once refused an order from the Thirty Tyrants to arrest an innocent man. Scholars still argue about how this fits with the Crito. One popular answer is that Socrates' basic rule was never to do injustice, not never to break the law.","source":"Plato, Apology 29c–d and 32c–d; cards disobey-crito, disobey-apology"},"order":["disobey-crito","disobey-apology","disobey-king","disobey-hobbes","disobey-rawls","disobey-sincerity","disobey-fairness","disobey-simmons","disobey-klosko","disobey-delmas","disobey-wolff","disobey-raz"]},{"id":"promise","title":"Is it ever right to break a promise?","intro":"You promised your best friend you'd help them move house on Saturday. On the way there, you come across a car crash, and the injured driver needs someone to stay with her until the ambulance comes. Or suppose instead that a friend once lent you his knife, and you promised to give it back whenever he asked. Today he shows up in a rage, wanting it back to hurt someone. Does a promise still bind you?","stance":"Before we begin: would you break a promise to a friend to help a stranger in trouble?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: some promises should be broken","no":"No: a promise must be kept"},"compass":"e4f","predict":{"prompt":"Thomas Hobbes asked about a promise to pay a robber a ransom in return for your life. What did he say?","options":["It is void, because it was made under threat","It binds you, unless the law releases you from it","It binds only if the robber keeps his side first"],"reveal":"The second. Hobbes thought a promise made out of fear is still a real promise: you got what you bargained for, your life. Cicero, writing seventeen centuries earlier, disagreed: a pirate is the common enemy of everyone, he said, and no promise made to him needs to be kept.","source":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 14; Cicero, De Officiis III.107; cards promise-hobbes-fear, promise-cicero-pirates"},"order":["promise-practice","promise-kant","promise-smart","promise-ross-1001","promise-hare","promise-ross-accident","promise-cephalus","promise-conditions","promise-conditions-reply","promise-hobbes-fear","promise-cicero-pirates"]},{"id":"aiart","title":"Is AI art really art?","intro":"In 2022 a picture called 'Théâtre D'opéra Spatial' won first prize for digital art at the Colorado State Fair. The man who entered it, Jason Allen, had made it with the AI program Midjourney, typing prompts, choosing among the results and touching up the winner. Some artists were furious; others shrugged. Was the winning picture art, and if so, who was the artist?","stance":"Before we begin: if a picture made by an AI moved you to tears, would you call it art?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: it can be real art","no":"No: art needs a human maker"},"compass":"a9","predict":{"prompt":"In 2018 Christie's auctioned a blurry portrait made with an AI program by a Paris art collective. The estimate was $7,000–10,000. What did it sell for?","options":["About $10,000","About $43,000","About $432,000"],"reveal":"About $432,000: the 'Portrait of Edmond de Belamy' sold for $432,500, over forty times its high estimate. By the institutional theory of art, that kind of reception by the art world is just what makes something art. Critics replied that the price shows only that people will pay for novelty.","source":"Christie's, New York, 25 October 2018; card aiart-belamy"},"order":["aiart-intentional","aiart-collingwood","aiart-collingwood-reply","aiart-chiang","aiart-camera","aiart-commission","aiart-kelly","aiart-boden","aiart-value","aiart-colton","aiart-whose","aiart-belamy"]},{"id":"children","title":"Is it wrong to have children?","intro":"In 2019 a young man in Mumbai announced that he wanted to sue his parents for having him without his consent. The story went round the world, and most people laughed. But a small group of philosophers think he had a point: every new life will contain pain, grief and death, and no one can agree to that before being born.","stance":"Before we begin: when parents bring a child into the world, are they doing that child a favor?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: having children is wrong","no":"No: it can be good"},"compass":"l8","predict":{"prompt":"David Benatar argues that coming into existence is always a harm. What part of his argument do critics attack most often?","options":["His claim that pain is bad","His claim that the absence of pain is good even when there is no one to enjoy it, while the absence of pleasure is not bad","His claim that most people's lives contain some pain"],"reveal":"The second. Critics like David DeGrazia and Ben Bradley ask why missing pain should count as 'good' for no one while missing pleasure counts as 'not bad'. Benatar replies that this lopsided pairing explains views almost everyone already holds, for example that we must not create a miserable child but need not create a happy one.","source":"Cards children-asymmetry, children-asymmetry-reply, children-asymmetry-defense"},"order":["children-asymmetry","children-asymmetry-reply","children-asymmetry-defense","children-narveson","children-goods","children-pollyanna","children-pollyanna-reply","children-consent","children-hypothetical-consent","children-consent-rejoinder","children-misanthropic","children-mbiti"]},{"id":"torture","title":"Can torture ever be justified?","intro":"Police have captured a terrorist who has hidden a bomb somewhere in a city. It will go off in a few hours and kill thousands of people. He smirks and refuses to say where it is. Causing him severe pain might make him talk. Would it be right to torture him?","stance":"Before we begin: would you torture one guilty man to save thousands of innocent lives?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: in extreme cases","no":"No: never, whatever the stakes"},"compass":"e4r","predict":{"prompt":"The UN Convention Against Torture was adopted in 1984. What does it say about emergencies like a ticking bomb?","options":["Torture is banned except in a declared public emergency","No exceptional circumstances whatsoever can justify torture","Each country may decide its own exceptions"],"reveal":"The second. Article 2 says that no exceptional circumstances whatsoever, whether war, a threat of war, political instability or any other public emergency, may be used to justify torture. Yet some philosophers, like Jeff McMahan, argue that torture can be justified in principle, even though the law should still forbid it.","source":"UN Convention Against Torture (1984), art. 2(2); cards torture-absolute, torture-mcmahan"},"order":["torture-ticking","torture-dreamland","torture-mcmahan","torture-gafgen","torture-absolute","torture-moore","torture-threshold","torture-vague","torture-sussman","torture-steinhoff","torture-dershowitz","torture-luban"]},{"id":"patriotism","title":"Is patriotism a virtue?","intro":"At the World Cup, millions of people cry when their national anthem plays, and cheer for players they have never met just because they wear the same shirt. Is that loyalty a good thing, a kind of love that makes people generous and brave? Or is it a bias, like favoring people of your own race, that has fueled wars?","stance":"Before we begin: do you owe more to people from your own country than to people from anywhere else?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: love of country is good","no":"No: it's a dangerous bias"},"compass":"s12","predict":{"prompt":"In a famous 1984 lecture, Alasdair MacIntyre asked 'Is Patriotism a Virtue?' What did he conclude?","options":["No: it is a kind of prejudice","Yes, on a morality rooted in particular communities, though it remains a permanent source of moral danger","Only if your country is just"],"reveal":"The second. MacIntyre argued that on the 'liberal' view of morality, which asks us to be impartial, patriotism looks like a vice; but if we learn morality within particular communities, loyalty to one's nation is a central virtue. He admitted that, either way, patriotism is a permanent source of moral danger.","source":"Alasdair MacIntyre, 'Is Patriotism a Virtue?', Lindley Lecture, University of Kansas (1984); card patriotism-macintyre"},"order":["patriotism-macintyre","patriotism-nussbaum","patriotism-partiality","patriotism-gomberg","patriotism-gomberg-reply","patriotism-tolstoy","patriotism-orwell","patriotism-keller","patriotism-critical","patriotism-rousseau","patriotism-kateb"]},{"id":"experience","title":"Should you plug into the experience machine?","intro":"Scientists offer you a machine that will give you any experiences you like for the rest of your life: winning an Olympic gold, writing a famous novel, falling in love. In fact you'll be floating in a tank with electrodes on your brain, but you'll forget you ever plugged in, and it will all feel completely real. Would you plug in?","stance":"Before we begin: would you plug into a machine that gave you a perfect life you'd never know was fake?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: plug in","no":"No: reality matters"},"compass":"e4c","predict":{"prompt":"In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, professional philosophers were asked whether they would enter the experience machine. What share said yes?","options":["About 13%","About 40%","About 70%"],"reveal":"About 13%; about 77% said no, and the rest gave other answers. But experiments by Felipe De Brigard suggest that people's answers depend a lot on which life they think they already have: told they were already plugged in, many people chose to stay.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org; card experience-debrigard"},"order":["experience-hedonism","experience-nozick","experience-debrigard","experience-kagan","experience-crisp","experience-philebus","experience-mill","experience-fake-discovery","experience-aristotle","experience-chalmers","experience-not-shared"]},{"id":"timetravel","title":"Could anyone travel back in time?","intro":"You build a time machine, set it for 1950, and find your grandfather as a young man, before he has met your grandmother. It would be easy to make sure they never meet. But then you would never be born, so you could never travel back to stop them. Does that show that travel into the past is impossible?","stance":"Before we begin: do you think travel into the past is possible, at least in principle?","sides":{"yes":"Yes: it's possible in principle","no":"No: the paradoxes rule it out"},"compass":"k7","predict":{"prompt":"In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, professional philosophers were asked whether time travel is metaphysically possible. How did they answer?","options":["A large majority said possible","A large majority said impossible","They were split almost exactly in half"],"reveal":"Split almost exactly: about 42% accepted or leaned toward 'possible' and about 41% toward 'impossible'; the rest gave other answers. Since David Lewis's 1976 paper, most philosophers agree the grandfather paradox doesn't prove time travel contradictory. The disagreement is about whether the price, like strings of lucky coincidences, is too high.","source":"2020 PhilPapers Survey, target faculty (Bourget & Chalmers, 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey', Philosophers' Imprint 23, 2023); survey2020.philpeople.org"},"order":["timetravel-grandfather","timetravel-lewis-can","timetravel-vihvelin","timetravel-horwich","timetravel-smith","timetravel-novikov","timetravel-branches","timetravel-not-past","timetravel-godel","timetravel-hawking","timetravel-tourists","timetravel-bootstrap","timetravel-lewis-loop"]}],"cards":[{"id":"lying-kant-murderer","q":"lying","side":"no","title":"The murderer at the door","claim":"You may not lie, even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding.","premises":["Being truthful in statements you cannot avoid making is a strict duty you owe to everyone, whatever harm follows.","A lie always wrongs humanity in general, because it weakens the trust that all statements and agreements depend on.","If you lie, you are to blame for whatever follows from the lie; if you tell the truth, what the murderer then does is his doing, not yours."],"conclusion":"Even to save a friend, lying to the murderer is wrong.","plain":"Kant says honesty is a wall you may never knock a hole in, even when a killer is knocking on the door.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, 'On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy' (1797), Ak. 8:425–430","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"replies","target":"lying-constant"}]},{"id":"lying-constant","q":"lying","side":"yes","title":"No right to the truth","claim":"You only owe the truth to people who have a right to it, and a murderer has none.","premises":["A duty is always owed to someone who has a matching right.","So the duty to tell the truth is owed only to those who have a right to the truth.","No one has a right to truth they will use to harm others, and the murderer means to kill."],"conclusion":"Lying to the murderer breaks no duty.","plain":"Truth is like a key you hand to people you can trust; you don't owe it to a burglar.","author":"constant","source":"Benjamin Constant, Des réactions politiques (On Political Reactions, 1797), ch. 8","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-kant-murderer","premise":1}]},{"id":"lying-kant-reply-constant","q":"lying","side":"no","title":"Truth is not property","claim":"Talk of a 'right to the truth' makes no sense; truthfulness is a duty about what you say, not a gift to the hearer.","premises":["Truth is not a possession that some people have a right to and others don't.","The duty of truthfulness concerns your own statements, and it protects the trust everyone relies on, not just this one hearer.","So the murderer's lack of rights does not cancel your duty."],"conclusion":"Constant's 'no right to the truth' escape fails.","plain":"Kant says honesty is not a present you give only to nice people; it's a rule about what comes out of your own mouth.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, 'On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy' (1797), Ak. 8:425–427","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-constant","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"lying-kant-murderer"}]},{"id":"lying-universal-law","q":"lying","side":"no","title":"The lie that defeats itself","claim":"A policy of lying when convenient cannot be made into a universal law, so lying is forbidden.","premises":["Act only on a policy (a 'maxim') you could will everyone to follow.","If everyone lied whenever it suited them, no one would believe anyone's words.","Then no lie could work, so the policy of lying undermines itself."],"conclusion":"Lying fails the test of universal law.","plain":"If everyone used counterfeit money, money would be worthless and the trick would stop working; lies are the same.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), Ak. 4:402–403 and 4:422 (the lying promise)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"lying-kant-murderer"}]},{"id":"lying-korsgaard","q":"lying","side":"yes","title":"Deceiving the deceiver","claim":"Kant's own universal-law test permits lying to the murderer, and in an unjust world we may use it.","premises":["The murderer is himself lying: he hides his purpose so that you will answer honestly.","A policy of 'lie to murderers who deceive you to find their victims' could be universal and still work, because his trick depends on you not knowing who he is.","Kant's stricter ideal (never treat anyone merely as a tool) cannot always be met when others are acting evilly; then we may act to resist evil."],"conclusion":"Lying to the murderer at the door is permissible.","plain":"When someone tries to use your honesty as a weapon, you are allowed to take the weapon away.","author":"korsgaard","source":"Christine Korsgaard, 'The Right to Lie: Kant on Dealing with Evil', Philosophy & Public Affairs 15 (1986): 325–349","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-universal-law","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-kant-murderer","premise":1}]},{"id":"lying-mill","q":"lying","side":"yes","title":"Trust is precious, but not sacred","claim":"Truthfulness is a hugely useful rule, but when a lie prevents a great harm, lying is right.","premises":["An act is right if it produces the most happiness overall.","Trust between people is enormously valuable, so even small lies usually cost a lot.","But some cases, like hiding information from someone set on wrongdoing, are recognized exceptions where the harm prevented outweighs the damage to trust."],"conclusion":"Lying is sometimes right.","plain":"Honesty is a great rule of thumb, but a rule of thumb shouldn't get your friend killed.","author":"mill","source":"John Stuart Mill, Utilitarianism (1861), ch. 2","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-kant-murderer","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-augustine","premise":3}]},{"id":"lying-augustine","q":"lying","side":"no","title":"Words were made for truth","claim":"Every lie misuses speech and harms the liar's soul, so no lie is ever right.","premises":["Words exist so that we can show others what is in our minds.","A lie uses words to show the opposite of what is in your mind, against their purpose.","Saving a body's life never justifies harming your own soul."],"conclusion":"No lie is ever right, not even one that saves a life.","plain":"Using words to lie is like using a lifeboat to sink someone: it turns a tool against its own purpose.","author":"augustine","source":"Augustine, Enchiridion, ch. 22; De Mendacio (On Lying, c. 395); followed by Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II q.110 a.3","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"lying-kant-murderer"}]},{"id":"lying-purpose-reply","q":"lying","side":"yes","title":"Speech has many jobs","claim":"The 'purpose of speech' argument proves too much.","premises":["We use words for jokes, stories, games, bluffs in poker and surprise parties, not only to report our thoughts.","None of these is wrong just because words aren't reporting our minds.","So 'words were made for truth' cannot show that every lie is wrong."],"conclusion":"Augustine's argument fails; a lie's wrongness must depend on something else, like harm or broken trust.","plain":"A novel is full of false sentences, and nobody calls the novelist a sinner.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see SEP, 'The Definition of Lying and Deception' (James Edwin Mahon)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-augustine","premise":2}]},{"id":"lying-silence","q":"lying","side":"no","title":"You don't have to answer","claim":"The case is a false choice: you can refuse to answer or evade without lying.","premises":["Kant's duty covers only statements you cannot avoid making.","At the door you can stay silent, refuse, shut the door, or call for help.","So protecting your friend does not require a lie."],"conclusion":"The murderer case does not show that lying is ever necessary, so it does not show lying is ever right.","plain":"If a stranger asks for your password, you don't need to make one up; you just say 'no'.","author":"anon","source":"Kantian defense resting on Kant, 'On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy' (1797), Ak. 8:426; discussed in Korsgaard, 'The Right to Lie' (1986)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-mill","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"lying-kant-murderer"}]},{"id":"lying-white-lies","q":"lying","side":"yes","title":"Kind little lies","claim":"Small 'white lies' that spare feelings and harm no one are often the right thing to do.","premises":["Telling Grandma you love her knitted sweater spares her feelings and costs nothing real.","Everyday social life runs more gently with such small courtesies.","An act that helps someone and harms no one is not wrong."],"conclusion":"White lies are often right.","plain":"Saying 'I love it!' about a gift you'll never wear is like social bubble wrap.","author":"anon","source":"Common view; examined in Sissela Bok, Lying: Moral Choice in Public and Private Life (1978), ch. 5","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"lying-mill"}]},{"id":"lying-bok","q":"lying","side":"neutral","title":"Liars misjudge the cost","claim":"Some lies can be justified, but liars badly underestimate the harm, so a strong presumption against lying is needed.","premises":["A liar sees the lie's benefits clearly but tends to miss its costs: to the deceived, to trust, and to the liar's own habits.","Small lies tend to need more lies to cover them, and get discovered.","A good test: would the lie still look justified if you had to defend it openly to reasonable people, including those deceived?"],"conclusion":"Lying is not always wrong, but it is right far less often than people think.","plain":"A lie is like borrowing on a credit card: it feels free now, and the bill comes later.","author":"anon","source":"Sissela Bok, Lying: Moral Choice in Public and Private Life (1978), chs. 2, 5 and 7","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-white-lies","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"lying-mill","premise":3}]},{"id":"strangers-singer-pond","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"The drowning child","claim":"If you would ruin your shoes to save a child in a pond, you must give to save children far away.","premises":["Suffering and death from lack of food, shelter and medical care are bad.","If we can prevent something bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it.","By giving to aid agencies, we can prevent such suffering and death without sacrificing anything comparably important."],"conclusion":"We ought to give, and not giving is wrong, not merely less generous.","plain":"A child far away is just as much a child as the one in the pond in front of you.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality', Philosophy & Public Affairs 1 (1972): 229–243","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"strangers-distance","q":"strangers","side":"no","title":"Nearness matters","claim":"We have stronger duties to people near us than to distant strangers.","premises":["Most people feel a strong duty to the child in front of them and a much weaker one to a child on another continent.","Morality is a set of practices among people who live together and depend on each other.","So nearness, community and relationship can make a real moral difference."],"conclusion":"The pond case does not carry over to distant strangers.","plain":"Your duty is like a candle's light: brightest close by, dimmer far away.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see Frances Kamm, 'Faminine Ethics: The Problem of Distance in Morality and Singer's Ethical Theory', in Jamieson (ed.), Singer and His Critics (1999)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-singer-pond","premise":2}]},{"id":"strangers-distance-reply","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"Distance is not a moral fact","claim":"How far away someone is makes no moral difference to whether we should help them.","premises":["A child's need is just as great whether she is ten meters away or ten thousand kilometers away.","Distance once mattered because we could not help far-off people; now aid agencies can reach them.","A difference that does not change the need or our ability to help cannot change the duty."],"conclusion":"Distance does not weaken the duty to help.","plain":"If you could save the drowning child by pressing a button from across the world, would it be fine not to press it?","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972), pp. 231–232","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-distance","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"strangers-singer-pond"}]},{"id":"strangers-unger","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"The envelope and the sedan","claim":"We judge the vivid case harshly and the faraway case gently, but the differences are psychological, not moral.","premises":["A man refuses to drive a bleeding stranger to hospital because the blood will ruin his car's expensive upholstery. This seems monstrous.","A woman throws away a charity's letter asking for a small donation that would save several children's lives. This seems fine to most people.","The differences between the cases, such as how close and how conspicuous the victim is, change what grabs our attention, not what we owe."],"conclusion":"Ignoring the envelope is seriously wrong too.","plain":"Our conscience reacts to what's in front of our eyes, like a smoke alarm that only works in one room.","author":"unger","source":"Peter Unger, Living High and Letting Die: Our Illusion of Innocence (1996), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"strangers-singer-pond"},{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-distance","premise":1}]},{"id":"strangers-why-me","q":"strangers","side":"no","title":"Why me, when millions could help?","claim":"Unlike the pond, distant poverty is a problem millions of people could help solve, so it isn't my job alone.","premises":["At the pond, only you can save the child.","Global poverty could be fixed if each well-off person gave a little.","So my duty is at most to do my part, not to make up for everyone else."],"conclusion":"The pond case does not show I must give a lot.","plain":"When a whole crowd could carry the piano, it's unfair to make one person lift it alone.","author":"anon","source":"Objection considered in Peter Singer, 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972), pp. 232–233","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-singer-pond","premise":2}]},{"id":"strangers-why-me-reply","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"The crowded pond","claim":"Other people failing to help does not excuse you.","premises":["Imagine several adults stand by the pond and none of them moves.","You would still be obliged to wade in; their failure doesn't make the child's death acceptable.","Distant poverty is like a crowded pond where most bystanders do nothing."],"conclusion":"You must help, even if others don't do their part.","plain":"If ten people walk past a drowning child, that's ten failures, not a reason for an eleventh.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972), pp. 232–233","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-why-me","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"strangers-singer-pond"}]},{"id":"strangers-demandingness","q":"strangers","side":"no","title":"Morality can't demand everything","claim":"Singer's principle would make us give until we are nearly as poor as those we help, which is too much.","premises":["Followed strictly, the principle requires giving until giving more would cost you as much as it helps others.","That would leave almost no room for hobbies, savings, or treats for your own children.","A moral theory that demands so much from ordinary decent people is probably mistaken."],"conclusion":"Singer's principle is too strong.","plain":"If every coffee you buy is a moral failure, something has gone wrong with the rules.","author":"anon","source":"The demandingness objection; see SEP, 'Consequentialism' (Walter Sinnott-Armstrong); Singer discusses it in 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972), p. 241","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-singer-pond","premise":2}]},{"id":"strangers-moderate","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"Even the mild version bites","claim":"A weaker principle, giving up nothing 'morally significant', still requires the comfortable to give a great deal.","premises":["Swap the strong principle for a moderate one: prevent bad things if you can without sacrificing anything morally significant.","Designer clothes, newer phones and fancy meals are not morally significant.","Comfortable people spend a lot on such things."],"conclusion":"Even on the moderate principle, the well-off must give much more than they do.","plain":"Even if you keep your house and your hobbies, the money spent on extras could still save lives.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, 'Famine, Affluence, and Morality' (1972), pp. 231 and 241; The Life You Can Save (2009)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"replies","target":"strangers-singer-pond"},{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-demandingness","premise":3}]},{"id":"strangers-williams-integrity","q":"strangers","side":"no","title":"The integrity objection","claim":"A morality that treats your own life projects as just one input among millions alienates you from what makes your life yours.","premises":["People live by deep commitments, like a craft, a cause, or their family. These give them reasons to go on living at all.","An impartial calculation that can override these at any moment treats the person as a mere channel for producing good outcomes.","A moral theory that cannot make room for a person's integrity misunderstands what persons are."],"conclusion":"We are not required to sacrifice our ground projects to help distant strangers.","plain":"A violinist told to sell her violin for charity loses more than an object; she loses part of who she is.","author":"williams","source":"Bernard Williams, 'A Critique of Utilitarianism', in J.J.C. Smart and B. Williams, Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973), §5","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-singer-pond","premise":2}]},{"id":"strangers-integrity-reply","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"Integrity isn't a trump card","claim":"Personal projects matter, but not enough to let a child die in front of you, so they can't excuse letting children die far away.","premises":["No one thinks your passion for painting excuses walking past the drowning child.","So personal projects can be outweighed when lives are at stake.","A sensible consequentialist can leave plenty of room for projects and still require serious giving."],"conclusion":"The integrity objection softens Singer's demand but doesn't defeat it.","plain":"Your dreams matter, but they don't give you the right to look away.","author":"anon","source":"Peter Railton, 'Alienation, Consequentialism, and the Demands of Morality', Philosophy & Public Affairs 13 (1984): 134–171","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-williams-integrity","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"strangers-singer-pond"}]},{"id":"strangers-wenar","q":"strangers","side":"no","title":"Poverty is no pond","claim":"Giving money to fight distant poverty is far less certain to help than wading into a pond.","premises":["At the pond, you know exactly what your action will do.","Aid passes through charities, governments and markets, and can have side effects, from waste to propping up bad rulers.","When it's unclear whether giving helps, the pond analogy loses much of its force."],"conclusion":"The duty to give is weaker, or at least more complicated, than Singer suggests.","plain":"Saving the child is a sure thing; donating can be more like throwing a rope into fog.","author":"anon","source":"Leif Wenar, 'Poverty Is No Pond: Challenges for the Affluent', in Illingworth, Pogge and Wenar (eds.), Giving Well (2011)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-singer-pond","premise":3}]},{"id":"strangers-evidence-reply","q":"strangers","side":"yes","title":"Give where the evidence is","claim":"Some aid is uncertain, but the best charities have strong evidence, so the duty to give remains.","premises":["Charities differ hugely in how much good each dollar does.","Some interventions, such as malaria nets and deworming, have been closely studied and are cheap per life saved or improved.","Uncertainty about some aid is a reason to choose carefully, not a reason to give nothing."],"conclusion":"We still must give, and give wisely.","plain":"If some ropes don't reach the drowning, you look for the ones that do.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, The Life You Can Save (2009); The Most Good You Can Do (2015)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"strangers-wenar","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"strangers-singer-pond"}]},{"id":"killing-foot-duties","q":"killing","side":"yes","title":"Not harming beats helping","claim":"Our duty not to harm people is stronger than our duty to help them.","premises":["We may not kill one healthy patient to use his organs to save five, though five lives outweigh one.","The best explanation is that the duty not to harm (a 'negative' duty) is stricter than the duty to aid (a 'positive' duty).","A trolley driver who steers from five to one may do so because either way he would be harming; he is choosing between negative duties."],"conclusion":"Killing is worse than letting die.","plain":"Pushing someone into a river is worse than failing to pull someone out, even if the result is the same.","author":"foot","source":"Philippa Foot, 'The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect', Oxford Review 5 (1967): 5–15","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"killing-thomson-bystander","q":"killing","side":"neutral","title":"The bystander at the switch","claim":"A bystander may divert the trolley, even though for him it's killing one versus letting five die, so Foot's explanation can't be the whole story.","premises":["Unlike the driver, a bystander who does nothing kills no one; he only lets the five die.","Most people still think the bystander may pull the switch and kill one.","If killing were always worse than letting die, the bystander would have to refrain."],"conclusion":"Something other than killing versus letting die explains the trolley cases.","plain":"If you're just walking by the tracks, pulling the lever turns you from a witness into a killer, and most people still say pull it.","author":"thomson","source":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'Killing, Letting Die, and the Trolley Problem', The Monist 59 (1976): 204–217; 'The Trolley Problem', Yale Law Journal 94 (1985): 1395–1415","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-foot-duties","premise":2}]},{"id":"killing-thomson-turning","q":"killing","side":"yes","title":"Thomson changes her mind","claim":"On reflection, the bystander should not divert the trolley after all.","premises":["If the bystander could also send the trolley onto himself instead, he presumably wouldn't be required to sacrifice himself.","If he wouldn't pay that cost himself, he may not make someone else pay it.","So turning the trolley onto the one is impermissible for the bystander."],"conclusion":"The bystander case no longer counts against the killing/letting-die distinction.","plain":"You can't volunteer someone else for a sacrifice you wouldn't make yourself.","author":"thomson","source":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'Turning the Trolley', Philosophy & Public Affairs 36 (2008): 359–374","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-thomson-bystander","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"killing-foot-duties"}]},{"id":"killing-footbridge","q":"killing","side":"yes","title":"The footbridge","claim":"Pushing a man off a bridge to stop the trolley is wrong, though the numbers match the switch case.","premises":["Pushing the large man off the footbridge would stop the trolley and save five, with one death.","Almost everyone judges this wrong.","The outcomes are the same as in the switch case, so something about how the death is brought about must matter."],"conclusion":"Numbers aren't everything: how you kill, and whether you kill, matters.","plain":"Pulling a lever and shoving a person with your own hands feel like completely different acts.","author":"thomson","source":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'The Trolley Problem', Yale Law Journal 94 (1985): 1395–1415","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"killing-foot-duties"}]},{"id":"killing-double-effect","q":"killing","side":"neutral","title":"The doctrine of double effect","claim":"Causing harm as a side effect can be permissible when intending the same harm as a means would not be.","premises":["One act can have two effects: one you intend, and one you foresee but do not intend.","Someone defending himself may intend only to save his own life; the attacker's death is a side effect.","Harm used as a means to your goal is worse than harm foreseen as a side effect, so long as the good is proportionate."],"conclusion":"In the footbridge case the man is used as a means; in the switch case the death is a side effect.","plain":"A bomber who aims at a factory and knows civilians nearby will die is judged differently from one who aims at the civilians.","author":"aquinas","source":"Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.64, a.7; applied to trolley-style cases by Foot (1967)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"killing-footbridge"},{"type":"replies","target":"killing-thomson-bystander"}]},{"id":"killing-loop","q":"killing","side":"neutral","title":"The loop","claim":"When the side track loops back, the one man's body is needed to stop the trolley, yet diverting still seems permissible.","premises":["Suppose the side track loops back to the five, and only the one man's body on it will stop the trolley.","Now his death is part of the plan, a means of saving the five, not just a side effect.","Yet many people judge that diverting is still permissible, just as in the plain switch case."],"conclusion":"The intending/foreseeing difference doesn't explain our verdicts.","plain":"Add a small loop of track and the 'side effect' becomes the 'means', but the lever pull feels no different.","author":"thomson","source":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'The Trolley Problem', Yale Law Journal 94 (1985): 1395–1415","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-double-effect","premise":3}]},{"id":"killing-rachels","q":"killing","side":"no","title":"Smith and Jones","claim":"When everything else is equal, killing is no worse than letting die.","premises":["Smith drowns his young cousin in the bath to inherit money.","Jones plans the same, but sees the cousin slip and drown, and just watches, ready to push him under if needed.","Smith and Jones are equally bad, and the only difference is killing versus letting die."],"conclusion":"The difference between killing and letting die is not itself morally important, so active euthanasia is no worse than withholding treatment.","plain":"Standing by while your cousin drowns, when you wanted him dead, is just as evil as holding him under.","author":"rachels","source":"James Rachels, 'Active and Passive Euthanasia', New England Journal of Medicine 292 (1975): 78–80","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-foot-duties","premise":2}]},{"id":"killing-rachels-starving","q":"killing","side":"no","title":"Starving is like killing","claim":"Letting people starve when we could easily save them is about as bad as killing them.","premises":["The usual reasons killing seems worse, like bad motives or certain results, are often absent or reversed in real cases.","When we compare cases where all such factors are equal, our sense of a big difference fades.","Well-off people let many die of hunger who could be saved cheaply."],"conclusion":"Letting the starving die is far worse than we usually admit.","plain":"If you could save someone from starving for the cost of lunch and don't, that's not so different from harming them.","author":"rachels","source":"James Rachels, 'Killing and Starving to Death', Philosophy 54 (1979): 159–171","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"killing-rachels"}]},{"id":"killing-additive-fallacy","q":"killing","side":"yes","title":"The additive fallacy","claim":"A factor can matter morally even if it makes no visible difference in one pair of cases.","premises":["In Smith and Jones, both men are already as bad as they can be because of their murderous motives.","A factor's effect can be hidden when other factors already max out our judgment, just as adding salt to a dish that's already ruined changes nothing you can taste.","So one pair of cases can't show that killing versus letting die never matters."],"conclusion":"Rachels' argument does not show the distinction is unimportant.","plain":"A loud speaker on top of a jet engine doesn't make the noise seem louder, but that doesn't mean the speaker is silent.","author":"kagan","source":"Shelly Kagan, 'The Additive Fallacy', Ethics 99 (1988): 5–31","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-rachels","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"killing-foot-duties"}]},{"id":"killing-greene","q":"killing","side":"no","title":"Pushing moral buttons","claim":"Our 'don't push' reaction tracks morally irrelevant features like using your own muscles.","premises":["Experiments show people disapprove of the footbridge sacrifice much more when it involves 'personal force', like pushing with your hands, than when it is done by a switch that drops the man through a trapdoor.","Whether harm is done with your muscles or with a switch is not morally important.","So the intuition behind the footbridge verdict is an unreliable alarm, not a moral insight."],"conclusion":"The footbridge case gives no good evidence that killing is worse than letting die.","plain":"Our brains flinch at shoving someone like a smoke alarm going off at burnt toast: loud, but not always right.","author":"greene","source":"Joshua Greene et al., 'Pushing Moral Buttons: The Interaction between Personal Force and Intention in Moral Judgment', Cognition 111 (2009): 364–371; Greene, Moral Tribes (2013), ch. 9","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-footbridge","premise":3}]},{"id":"killing-singer-debunk","q":"killing","side":"no","title":"Evolutionary leftovers","claim":"Our resistance to up-close killing is an instinct shaped by evolution, not a guide to what's right.","premises":["Up-close violence existed in our ancestors' world; harming by remote switch did not.","So we evolved strong alarms against personal violence but not against impersonal harm.","An instinct shaped this way has no special claim to track moral truth."],"conclusion":"We should trust reasoning about outcomes over the 'don't push' instinct.","plain":"Our gut reacts to ancient dangers, like a dog barking at the vacuum cleaner.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, 'Ethics and Intuitions', The Journal of Ethics 9 (2005): 331–352","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"killing-greene"}]},{"id":"killing-berker","q":"killing","side":"yes","title":"Brain scans can't settle ethics","claim":"The debunking argument gets all its force from a moral premise that neuroscience cannot prove.","premises":["Data about which brain systems fire tells us how we judge, not whether the judgment is correct.","The real work is done by the claim that personal force is morally irrelevant, which is itself a moral claim.","Defenders of the footbridge verdict can reply that the deeper feature is using a person as a tool, which personal force often accompanies."],"conclusion":"The footbridge intuition survives the scientific challenge.","plain":"A scan can show where your thoughts happen, not whether they're right, just as a map of a courtroom can't tell you the verdict.","author":"berker","source":"Selim Berker, 'The Normative Insignificance of Neuroscience', Philosophy & Public Affairs 37 (2009): 293–329","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"killing-greene","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"killing-footbridge"}]},{"id":"freewill-consequence","q":"freewill","side":"no","title":"The consequence argument","claim":"If determinism is true, no one could ever have done otherwise.","premises":["If determinism is true, every act is a consequence of the laws of nature together with events in the distant past.","We have no choice about what happened before we were born, and no choice about the laws of nature.","If you have no choice about something, and it guarantees that X happens, then you have no choice about X either."],"conclusion":"If determinism is true, we have no choice about anything we do, so no free will.","plain":"If the dominoes were set up before you were born, your fall was decided long before you 'chose' it.","author":"vaninwagen","source":"Peter van Inwagen, An Essay on Free Will (1983), ch. 3","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"freewill-hume","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"Freedom is doing what you will","claim":"Liberty just means acting as you choose, and that is perfectly compatible with causal necessity.","premises":["Being free means having the power to act or not act according to what you will.","Only a prisoner in chains lacks this, and determinism doesn't put us in chains.","Our actions follow from our characters and motives in regular ways; that's what makes them ours, and lets us predict and judge them."],"conclusion":"Determinism and free will are compatible.","plain":"You're free when nothing stops you doing what you want, even if your wants have causes.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), §8 'Of Liberty and Necessity'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-consequence","premise":3}]},{"id":"freewill-conditional-objection","q":"freewill","side":"no","title":"But could you have chosen otherwise?","claim":"Hume's freedom, 'you'd have acted differently if you had chosen differently', is too thin.","premises":["The compatibilist says: you could have done otherwise means you would have, if you had chosen otherwise.","But someone with a crippling phobia of snakes 'would' touch the snake if they chose to, yet they cannot choose to.","So the 'if you had chosen' test can be passed by someone who plainly had no real alternative."],"conclusion":"Classical compatibilism doesn't give us the freedom we care about.","plain":"Saying 'you could have left the cage if you'd wanted to' is no comfort if you were built unable to want to.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection to the conditional analysis; see SEP, 'Compatibilism' (Michael McKenna and D. Justin Coates), §3","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-hume","premise":1}]},{"id":"freewill-reactive-attitudes","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"We can't stop resenting","claim":"Holding people responsible rests on attitudes like resentment and gratitude, which no theory about determinism could make us give up.","premises":["Responsibility shows up in our reactive attitudes: resentment, gratitude, indignation, love.","These attitudes are part of human relationships, not conclusions we drew from a theory about causes.","So learning that determinism is true would give us no reason to abandon them."],"conclusion":"Moral responsibility is safe, whatever physics says.","plain":"If someone stomps on your foot on purpose, you'll be angry, and no lecture on physics will change that.","author":"pfstrawson","source":"P.F. Strawson, 'Freedom and Resentment', Proceedings of the British Academy 48 (1962): 1–25","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"replies","target":"freewill-hume"},{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-conditional-objection","premise":3}]},{"id":"freewill-frankfurt","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"Black and Jones","claim":"You can be morally responsible even if you couldn't have done otherwise.","premises":["Black secretly controls a device that would force Jones to choose to do X if Jones showed any sign of choosing otherwise.","Jones chooses X all by himself, so the device never acts.","Jones is responsible, even though he could not have done otherwise."],"conclusion":"Responsibility doesn't require alternative possibilities, so the consequence argument does not threaten it.","plain":"If you walk into a room on your own, you chose it, even if someone had secretly locked the door behind you.","author":"frankfurt","source":"Harry Frankfurt, 'Alternate Possibilities and Moral Responsibility', Journal of Philosophy 66 (1969): 829–839","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-consequence"}]},{"id":"freewill-dilemma-defense","q":"freewill","side":"no","title":"Black's sign problem","claim":"Frankfurt cases either assume determinism or leave Jones a real alternative.","premises":["Black must rely on some earlier sign to know what Jones is about to choose.","If that sign guarantees the choice, determinism is already assumed, which begs the question.","If it doesn't guarantee the choice, Jones could still have chosen otherwise, at least for a moment."],"conclusion":"Frankfurt cases don't show that responsibility is possible without alternatives.","plain":"Either Black's crystal ball is perfect, which assumes the future is fixed, or it isn't, and Jones still had a choice.","author":"anon","source":"David Widerker, 'Libertarianism and Frankfurt's Attack on the Principle of Alternative Possibilities', Philosophical Review 104 (1995): 247–261; see SEP, 'Moral Responsibility'","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-frankfurt","premise":1}]},{"id":"freewill-gstrawson","q":"freewill","side":"no","title":"The basic argument","claim":"No one can be ultimately responsible for what they do, whether or not determinism is true.","premises":["You do what you do because of the way you are, at least in mental respects.","To be truly responsible for what you do, you'd have to be truly responsible for the way you are.","To be responsible for the way you are, you'd have to have made yourself that way, using an earlier self you'd also need to be responsible for, and so on forever."],"conclusion":"True, ultimate moral responsibility is impossible.","plain":"To deserve credit for your choices you'd have to have built yourself from scratch, and nobody can lift themselves by their own bootstraps.","author":"gstrawson","source":"Galen Strawson, 'The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility', Philosophical Studies 75 (1994): 5–24","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"freewill-dennett","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"The freedom worth wanting","claim":"We don't need magical self-creation; evolved capacities for reflection and self-control are free will enough.","premises":["Determinism isn't fatalism: what happens still depends on what we think and decide.","Humans have evolved powers to foresee, deliberate, resist temptation and change their own habits over time.","Ultimate self-creation from nothing is a freedom no one could have, and no one needs, to deserve praise and blame."],"conclusion":"We have all the free will worth wanting.","plain":"A good chess program 'decides' its moves even though it's a machine; we're far more flexible than that.","author":"dennett","source":"Daniel Dennett, Elbow Room: The Varieties of Free Will Worth Wanting (1984); Freedom Evolves (2003)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-gstrawson","premise":2},{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-consequence"}]},{"id":"freewill-reid","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"We are the cause","claim":"Free agents are themselves the first causes of their choices, not links in a chain of events.","premises":["We have a natural, unshakable conviction that we are the authors of our deliberate actions.","To be the author of an act is to have power over it: to be able to do it or not.","This power belongs to the agent herself, not to prior events or motives, which do not act but only influence."],"conclusion":"We have libertarian free will: our choices start with us.","plain":"You are not a domino in the line; you're the finger that pushes the first one.","author":"reid","source":"Thomas Reid, Essays on the Active Powers of Man (1788), Essay IV","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-gstrawson","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-consequence"}]},{"id":"freewill-luck","q":"freewill","side":"neutral","title":"Replaying the tape","claim":"If our choices are undetermined, they seem to be matters of luck, which threatens free will too.","premises":["Suppose a choice is undetermined, and God 'replays' the moment a thousand times with everything exactly the same.","Sometimes the person would lie and sometimes tell the truth, in some random-looking proportion.","Then which way she chose in the actual case looks like a matter of chance, not of her control."],"conclusion":"Indeterminism doesn't obviously secure free will. Free will remains a mystery.","plain":"If your choice could come out differently with nothing different about you, it starts to look like a coin toss.","author":"vaninwagen","source":"Peter van Inwagen, 'Free Will Remains a Mystery', Philosophical Perspectives 14 (2000): 1–19","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-reid","premise":3}]},{"id":"freewill-pereboom","q":"freewill","side":"no","title":"The four-case argument","claim":"A person manipulated by scientists isn't responsible, and an ordinary determined person differs in no relevant way.","premises":["Case 1: neuroscientists control Plum's brain moment by moment so that he kills, though he reasons and wants just as a normal person does. He isn't responsible.","Cases 2 and 3: the control happens earlier, by programming at birth or by strict upbringing. There is no relevant difference from Case 1.","Case 4: Plum is an ordinary person in a deterministic world. There is no relevant difference from Case 3."],"conclusion":"If determinism is true, ordinary people aren't responsible either.","plain":"Whether the puppet strings are pulled by scientists or by the Big Bang, they're still strings.","author":"pereboom","source":"Derk Pereboom, Living Without Free Will (2001), ch. 4","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-hume"},{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-dennett"}]},{"id":"freewill-hard-line","q":"freewill","side":"yes","title":"Plum is responsible","claim":"Start from the ordinary case: if ordinary determined people are responsible, so is manipulated Plum.","premises":["We have good reason to think ordinary people in Case 4 can be responsible.","Plum in Case 1 meets every condition an ordinary responsible person meets: he reasons, cares and acts on his own values.","Our sense that he is not responsible comes from imagining his own mind being bypassed, which the case says doesn't happen."],"conclusion":"The argument can be run backward: Plum is responsible in all four cases.","plain":"If Plum thinks, wants and chooses just like you, the story of how he got that way may not excuse him.","author":"anon","source":"Michael McKenna, 'A Hard-line Reply to Pereboom's Four-Case Manipulation Argument', Philosophy and Phenomenological Research 77 (2008): 142–159","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"freewill-pereboom","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"freewill-hume"}]},{"id":"identity-locke","q":"identity","side":"yes","title":"You are as far as your memory reaches","claim":"A person stays the same as far back as their consciousness, mainly memory, can reach.","premises":["A person is a thinking being that can consider itself as the same self at different times and places.","What links you to a past action is being able to remember it from the inside, as your own.","If a prince's consciousness entered a cobbler's body, everyone would see that it is the prince who acts there."],"conclusion":"Personal identity consists in continuity of consciousness, not in sameness of body or soul-stuff.","plain":"If your memories moved into another body, you'd go with them.","author":"locke","source":"John Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (2nd ed., 1694), II.xxvii.9 and 15","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"identity-reid-officer","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"The brave officer","claim":"Locke's memory theory contradicts itself.","premises":["A boy is flogged for stealing apples. As a young officer, he remembers the flogging. As an old general, he remembers being the officer but has forgotten the flogging.","By Locke's theory, the general is the officer, and the officer is the boy, but the general is not the boy.","But identity is transitive: if A is B and B is C, then A must be C."],"conclusion":"Memory cannot be what personal identity consists in.","plain":"If A is B and B is C, A has to be C, but Locke's theory breaks that rule.","author":"reid","source":"Thomas Reid, Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man (1785), Essay III, ch. 6","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":2}]},{"id":"identity-continuity-reply","q":"identity","side":"yes","title":"Overlapping chains","claim":"What matters isn't direct memory of every past moment but an overlapping chain of psychological links.","premises":["The general remembers the officer's experiences, and the officer remembered the boy's.","Identity can be grounded in a continuous chain of overlapping connections, like a rope whose fibers don't run its full length.","Such continuity is transitive even when direct connections fade."],"conclusion":"A revised psychological theory survives Reid's objection.","plain":"No single thread runs the length of a rope, yet it's one rope.","author":"parfit","source":"Derek Parfit, Reasons and Persons (1984), Part III, ch. 10; see SEP, 'Personal Identity' (Eric Olson)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-reid-officer","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"identity-locke"}]},{"id":"identity-butler","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"Memory assumes identity","claim":"Memory can't make you the same person, because real memory already presupposes that you are.","premises":["A genuine memory of doing something is a memory of something you yourself did.","So to tell real memory from false 'memory', we must already know whether the rememberer is the same person.","A theory that defines identity by memory therefore goes in a circle."],"conclusion":"Memory is evidence of identity, not what identity consists in.","plain":"You can't use a ruler to prove the ruler is the right length.","author":"anon","source":"Joseph Butler, 'Of Personal Identity', Dissertation I appended to The Analogy of Religion (1736)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":2}]},{"id":"identity-quasi-memory","q":"identity","side":"yes","title":"Quasi-memory","claim":"We can define a memory-like link that does not presuppose identity, and build the theory on that.","premises":["A 'quasi-memory' is an apparent memory caused in the right way by someone's past experience, whether or not that someone was you.","Ordinary memories are quasi-memories in which, as it happens, the experiencer and rememberer are the same.","Identity can be defined by chains of quasi-memory and other psychological links without circularity."],"conclusion":"Butler's circularity objection can be avoided.","plain":"Define the bridge first, then say a person is whatever is joined by bridges like that.","author":"shoemaker","source":"Sydney Shoemaker, 'Persons and Their Pasts', American Philosophical Quarterly 7 (1970): 269–285; Derek Parfit, Reasons and Persons (1984), ch. 11","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-butler","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"identity-locke"}]},{"id":"identity-williams","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"Who gets tortured tomorrow?","claim":"Told the same story in a different way, a 'mind swap' looks like something terrible happening to you in your own body.","premises":["Suppose you're told tomorrow your memories and character will be wiped and replaced, and then your body will be tortured. You'd fear the torture.","That is exactly the 'body swap' that, told the other way, seems to move you to another body.","Fear of the torture suggests that you stay with your body, whatever happens to your psychology."],"conclusion":"Bodily continuity, not psychology, may be what makes you you.","plain":"If someone says 'we'll erase your mind, then hurt your body', you don't relax and think 'that won't be me'.","author":"williams","source":"Bernard Williams, 'The Self and the Future', Philosophical Review 79 (1970): 161–180","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":3}]},{"id":"identity-parfit-fission","q":"identity","side":"neutral","title":"Splitting in two","claim":"Cases of fission show that identity is not what matters in survival.","premises":["Suppose each half of your brain is transplanted into a different body, and both resulting people have your memories and character.","You can't be both (they'll lead different lives), and there's no reason you'd be one rather than the other.","Yet this is about as good for you as ordinary survival; it's nothing like death."],"conclusion":"What matters is psychological connection and continuity (Parfit's 'Relation R'), not identity itself.","plain":"Like an amoeba splitting, you'd carry on twice over, even if neither half is strictly 'you'.","author":"parfit","source":"Derek Parfit, Reasons and Persons (1984), Part III, chs. 12–13; teletransporter cases in ch. 10","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":1},{"type":"supports","target":"identity-continuity-reply"}]},{"id":"identity-olson","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"You are an animal","claim":"You are a human animal, so you persist as long as that living organism does.","premises":["There is a human animal sitting in your chair, and it is thinking your thoughts.","If you were not that animal, there would be two thinkers in your chair, and you could never know which one you were.","Animals persist through biological continuity, even through memory loss or a vegetative state, and each of us was once a fetus without any psychology."],"conclusion":"Your identity over time is the life of a biological organism.","plain":"You're not a ghost riding a body; you're the body, a living animal that thinks.","author":"olson","source":"Eric Olson, The Human Animal: Personal Identity without Psychology (1997); 'An Argument for Animalism', in Martin and Barresi (eds.), Personal Identity (2003)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":1}]},{"id":"identity-transplant","q":"identity","side":"yes","title":"Where the brain goes","claim":"In a brain transplant, you go where your brain goes, not where your animal body stays.","premises":["Suppose your cerebrum is moved into another body, and the person who wakes has your memories, values and plans.","Almost everyone says that person is you.","But the animal left behind, with a working brainstem, is still alive."],"conclusion":"You are not identical to the animal; psychology decides where you go.","plain":"If your brain woke up in a new body, you'd say 'I've had a transplant', not 'someone else has my brain'.","author":"shoemaker","source":"Sydney Shoemaker, Self-Knowledge and Self-Identity (1963), pp. 23–24 (Brown and Brownson); see SEP, 'Animalism' (Eric Olson)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-olson","premise":3}]},{"id":"identity-hume-bundle","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"A bundle of perceptions","claim":"When you look within, you find only passing perceptions, never a lasting self.","premises":["Whenever I look into myself, I always stumble on some particular perception: heat, cold, love, pain.","I never observe a self apart from these perceptions.","Our belief in a single lasting self comes from the imagination smoothing over related, changing perceptions."],"conclusion":"There is no lasting self; the mind is a bundle of perceptions, and personal identity is a kind of useful fiction.","plain":"A self is like a theater play with no theater: just scenes following one another.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature (1739), Book I, Part iv, §6 'Of Personal Identity'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":1},{"type":"supports","target":"identity-parfit-fission"}]},{"id":"identity-buddha","q":"identity","side":"no","title":"Not-self","claim":"None of the parts that make up a person is a permanent self, and there's nothing beyond them that is.","premises":["A person is made of five changing aggregates: body, feelings, perceptions, mental formations and consciousness.","Each is impermanent and not fully under our control, so none is a lasting self.","Clinging to the idea of a lasting self leads to suffering."],"conclusion":"There is no permanent self that stays the same over time.","plain":"You're less like a stone and more like a river: always the same name, never the same water.","author":"buddha","source":"Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta (Discourse on the Not-Self), Saṃyutta Nikāya 22.59","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"identity-hume-bundle"},{"type":"attacks","target":"identity-locke","premise":1}]},{"id":"mind-descartes-conceivability","q":"mind","side":"no","title":"I can conceive myself without a body","claim":"Because I can clearly conceive mind and body apart, they really are distinct.","premises":["I clearly and distinctly understand myself as a thinking thing, without needing to think of a body.","I clearly understand body as an extended, non-thinking thing.","Whatever I can clearly conceive apart, God could make exist apart, so they are really distinct."],"conclusion":"My mind is distinct from my body and could exist without it.","plain":"If you can imagine being a thinking thing with no body at all, maybe that's because you really are one.","author":"descartes","source":"René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Meditation VI (AT VII 78)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"mind-descartes-divisibility","q":"mind","side":"no","title":"You can't cut a mind in half","claim":"Bodies can be divided; minds cannot. So they are different things.","premises":["Any body, including the brain, can be divided into parts.","My mind cannot be divided: I can't find half a mind; it is one thing that wills, understands and feels.","If one thing has a property the other lacks, they are not identical."],"conclusion":"The mind is not the body.","plain":"You can slice a cake into pieces, but you can't slice a thought.","author":"descartes","source":"René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Meditation VI (AT VII 85–86)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"mind-descartes-conceivability"}]},{"id":"mind-interaction","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"How can a ghost push a body?","claim":"If the mind is non-physical, it's a mystery how it could ever move the body.","premises":["Pushing and moving seem to require contact, and contact requires being physical and having extension.","Descartes' mind is non-physical and unextended.","Yet our minds clearly move our bodies when we decide to act."],"conclusion":"Mind-body dualism cannot explain how mind and body interact, which favors the view that the mind is physical.","plain":"A ghost's hand passes through the door; how could it ever push your arm?","author":"elisabeth","source":"Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia, letter to Descartes, 6/16 May 1643 (AT III 661); see SEP, 'Elisabeth of Bohemia'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-descartes-conceivability"}]},{"id":"mind-causal-closure","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"The causal closure argument","claim":"Since mental events cause physical events, and physics leaves no gaps, mental events must be physical.","premises":["Every physical effect has a sufficient physical cause (physics is 'causally closed').","Mental events, like deciding to raise your arm, cause physical effects.","Physical effects aren't systematically caused twice over, once physically and once by a separate mind."],"conclusion":"Mental events are physical events.","plain":"If the brain already fully explains your arm moving, a separate ghost would have nothing left to do.","author":"anon","source":"SEP, 'Physicalism' (Daniel Stoljar); David Papineau, Thinking about Consciousness (2002), ch. 1 and appendix","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"mind-interaction"},{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-descartes-conceivability"}]},{"id":"mind-nagel-bat","q":"mind","side":"no","title":"What is it like to be a bat?","claim":"Physical science, which is objective, seems to leave out the subjective 'what it is like' of experience.","premises":["An organism is conscious if there is something it is like to be that organism, like a bat sensing by sonar.","Facts about what it is like are tied to a particular point of view.","Physical science describes things from no point of view, so it seems to leave these facts out."],"conclusion":"We have no idea, at present, how physicalism could be true. (Nagel stops short of saying it is false.)","plain":"You could learn everything about a bat's brain and still not know what echolocation feels like.","author":"nagel","source":"Thomas Nagel, 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?', Philosophical Review 83 (1974): 435–450","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-causal-closure"}]},{"id":"mind-mary","q":"mind","side":"no","title":"Mary's room","claim":"A scientist who knows every physical fact about color still learns something new when she first sees red.","premises":["Mary has lived in a black-and-white room and knows every physical fact about color vision.","When she leaves and sees a red tomato, she learns something new: what seeing red is like.","If she knew all the physical facts and still learned a new fact, not all facts are physical."],"conclusion":"Physicalism is false.","plain":"Reading everything about strawberries isn't the same as tasting one.","author":"jackson","source":"Frank Jackson, 'Epiphenomenal Qualia', Philosophical Quarterly 32 (1982): 127–136","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"mind-nagel-bat"}]},{"id":"mind-ability","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"Mary gains know-how, not facts","claim":"What Mary learns is a set of abilities, not new information.","premises":["There is knowing that something is so, and knowing how to do something.","On leaving the room, Mary gains new abilities: to imagine red, remember it and recognize it.","Gaining abilities is not learning new facts, so her new knowledge doesn't show there are non-physical facts."],"conclusion":"Mary's room doesn't refute physicalism.","plain":"Reading about riding a bike gives you facts; riding it gives you a skill, not a secret fact.","author":"lewis","source":"David Lewis, 'What Experience Teaches', Proceedings of the Russellian Society 13 (1988): 29–57; Laurence Nemirow, 'Physicalism and the Cognitive Role of Acquaintance' (1990)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-mary","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"mind-causal-closure"}]},{"id":"mind-dennett-mary","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"The blue banana","claim":"If Mary truly knew all the physical facts, she wouldn't be surprised at all.","premises":["We can't really imagine knowing all physical facts; we picture someone who merely knows a lot.","Someone who knew all of it would know exactly how her brain would react to red, and could not be tricked by a blue-painted banana.","So the intuition that she learns something new is a failure of imagination."],"conclusion":"Mary learns nothing new, and physicalism is safe.","plain":"Show the all-knowing Mary a blue banana and she says, 'Nice try, that's blue.'","author":"dennett","source":"Daniel Dennett, Consciousness Explained (1991), ch. 12","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-mary","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"mind-causal-closure"}]},{"id":"mind-jackson-recants","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"Jackson changes his mind","claim":"The inventor of Mary's room came to think the argument must go wrong somewhere.","premises":["If Mary's new knowledge were of non-physical properties, those properties would have no physical effects.","But our knowledge of and talk about experiences are physical effects of them.","So we couldn't know about non-physical qualia at all, and the intuition must be mistaken."],"conclusion":"We should accept physicalism and look for the flaw in the knowledge argument.","plain":"If the redness of red couldn't make you say 'red!', how would you ever know about it?","author":"jackson","source":"Frank Jackson, 'Mind and Illusion', in O'Hear (ed.), Minds and Persons, Royal Institute of Philosophy Supplement 53 (2003): 251–271","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-mary","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"mind-causal-closure"}]},{"id":"mind-zombie","q":"mind","side":"no","title":"The zombie argument","claim":"A physical duplicate of you with no inner experience is conceivable, so consciousness is not physical.","premises":["We can conceive of a 'zombie': a being physically identical to you, atom for atom, with no conscious experience at all.","If such a zombie is conceivable, it is possible.","If a zombie is possible, consciousness is something over and above the physical."],"conclusion":"Physicalism is false.","plain":"Picture your exact twin who acts like you but has the lights off inside; if that's possible, the lights are something extra.","author":"chalmers","source":"David Chalmers, The Conscious Mind (1996), ch. 3","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-causal-closure"},{"type":"supports","target":"mind-mary"}]},{"id":"mind-phenomenal-concepts","q":"mind","side":"yes","title":"Two names, one thing","claim":"Conceivability doesn't prove possibility: we have two different concepts for one physical thing.","premises":["People once could conceive of water without H2O, yet water just is H2O.","We think about experiences with special 'phenomenal concepts' from the inside, and about brain states with scientific concepts.","Two concepts can pick out the same thing without our being able to tell a priori."],"conclusion":"Zombies and Mary show only a gap between concepts, not between things.","plain":"'Morning star' and 'evening star' sound like two things but are both Venus; 'pain' and 'neural state X' may be the same.","author":"anon","source":"Brian Loar, 'Phenomenal States', Philosophical Perspectives 4 (1990): 81–108; see SEP, 'Qualia: The Knowledge Argument' (Martine Nida-Rümelin)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-zombie","premise":2},{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-mary","premise":3}]},{"id":"mind-goff","q":"mind","side":"neutral","title":"Consciousness all the way down","claim":"Physics tells us what matter does, not what it is; maybe consciousness is the inner nature of matter itself.","premises":["Physics describes matter only by its structure and behavior, in mathematical terms.","It says nothing about the intrinsic nature of the stuff that has this structure.","Consciousness is the one intrinsic nature we know directly, so the simplest view is that matter's intrinsic nature is, at bottom, a form of consciousness."],"conclusion":"Panpsychism: neither standard physicalism nor dualism, but consciousness as a basic feature of the physical world.","plain":"Physics is like a rulebook for chess pieces that never says what the pieces are made of; maybe it's experience.","author":"goff","source":"Philip Goff, Galileo's Error: Foundations for a New Science of Consciousness (2019), chs. 1 and 5","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"mind-nagel-bat"},{"type":"attacks","target":"mind-interaction"}]},{"id":"evil-mackie","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"The logical problem of evil","claim":"An all-powerful, wholly good God and the existence of evil are logically inconsistent.","premises":["God is omnipotent: there are no limits to what he can do.","God is wholly good, and a good being eliminates evil as far as it can.","Evil exists."],"conclusion":"These claims can't all be true, so the traditional God does not exist.","plain":"If a lifeguard is perfectly kind and perfectly strong, why is anyone drowning?","author":"mackie","source":"J.L. Mackie, 'Evil and Omnipotence', Mind 64 (1955): 200–212","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"evil-hume-philo","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"Epicurus' old questions","claim":"Is God willing but unable to prevent evil? Then he's not all-powerful. Able but unwilling? Then he's not good.","premises":["If God is willing to prevent evil but not able, he is weak.","If he is able but not willing, he is malevolent.","If he is both able and willing, where does evil come from?"],"conclusion":"Nature as we find it gives no reason to infer a perfectly good, all-powerful designer.","plain":"Either God can't stop the suffering, or won't, and neither fits a perfect God.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), Parts X–XI (Philo's speeches)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"evil-mackie"}]},{"id":"evil-plantinga","q":"evil","side":"yes","title":"The free will defense","claim":"It's possible that God couldn't create a world with free creatures and no evil, so God and evil are consistent.","premises":["A world with creatures who freely choose good is more valuable than a world of puppets.","God cannot cause creatures to freely do only good; if he causes it, it isn't free.","It's possible that every free person God could create would go wrong at least once ('transworld depravity')."],"conclusion":"It's possible that God has a good reason to allow evil, so the logical problem fails.","plain":"You can't make someone freely love you; giving them freedom means risking that they won't.","author":"plantinga","source":"Alvin Plantinga, God, Freedom, and Evil (1974), Part I, section a","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-mackie","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"evil-mackie-good-choices"}]},{"id":"evil-mackie-good-choices","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"Why not free and always good?","claim":"If it's possible for one person to freely choose good on one occasion, God could make people who freely choose good every time.","premises":["There's nothing contradictory about a person freely choosing good on every occasion.","An omnipotent God can make anything that is not contradictory.","So God could have made free creatures who always freely choose good."],"conclusion":"Free will does not explain why God allows evil.","plain":"If you can freely pick the right answer once, why couldn't God make someone who always freely picks it?","author":"mackie","source":"J.L. Mackie, 'Evil and Omnipotence', Mind 64 (1955): 200–212, at p. 209","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-plantinga","premise":2}]},{"id":"evil-natural","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"Earthquakes aren't choices","claim":"Free will may explain cruelty, but not earthquakes, cancer or disease.","premises":["Much suffering is caused by nature, not by anyone's free choice.","The free will defense only explains evil that comes from free choices.","So the defense leaves natural evil unexplained."],"conclusion":"Natural evil remains a problem for belief in a good God.","plain":"Nobody chose the tsunami.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see SEP, 'The Problem of Evil' (Michael Tooley)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-plantinga"}]},{"id":"evil-natural-reply","q":"evil","side":"yes","title":"It only has to be possible","claim":"Against the logical problem, the defense only needs a possible explanation of natural evil, not a believable one.","premises":["The logical problem claims God and evil are flatly inconsistent.","To defeat it, we only need a possible story in which both are true.","It's possible that natural evil results from the free choices of non-human persons, or is needed for a stable world where free choices matter."],"conclusion":"Natural evil doesn't revive the logical problem.","plain":"To show two things can fit together, you only need one way they could fit, even an odd one.","author":"plantinga","source":"Alvin Plantinga, God, Freedom, and Evil (1974), Part I, section a (on natural evil)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-natural","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"evil-plantinga"}]},{"id":"evil-hick","q":"evil","side":"yes","title":"A vale of soul-making","claim":"God's goal is not a pleasure paradise but the growth of good character, which requires a world with real hardship.","premises":["God's aim is to bring people to moral and spiritual maturity, not to keep them comfortable.","Virtues like courage, compassion and patience can only grow in a world with real danger, pain and need.","A world where God visibly fixed every harm would leave no room for freely chosen goodness."],"conclusion":"Suffering, including natural evil, can serve God's good purpose.","plain":"A gym with no weights won't build muscle; a world with no hardship won't build character.","author":"hick","source":"John Hick, Evil and the God of Love (1966), Part IV","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-natural","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"evil-plantinga"}]},{"id":"evil-rowe","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"The fawn in the forest fire","claim":"Some suffering seems pointless, which makes it likely that there is no God.","premises":["A fawn is trapped in a forest fire, badly burned, and suffers for days before dying. No one sees it, and it seems to serve no greater good.","Probably there exist such cases of intense suffering that an all-powerful, all-knowing being could have prevented without losing any greater good.","A wholly good, all-knowing being would prevent such suffering unless it could not do so without losing a greater good or allowing something worse."],"conclusion":"Probably, no all-powerful, all-knowing, wholly good being exists.","plain":"No one learns courage from a fawn dying alone in a fire, so what's it for?","author":"rowe","source":"William Rowe, 'The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism', American Philosophical Quarterly 16 (1979): 335–341","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-hick","premise":2}]},{"id":"evil-skeptical-theism","q":"evil","side":"yes","title":"We wouldn't see the reasons","claim":"Not seeing a reason for suffering is weak evidence that there is none, given how limited our minds are compared with God's.","premises":["You can rely on 'I don't see an X, so there's probably none' only if you'd expect to see an X if it were there.","If God exists, his reasons might well be far beyond our understanding, like a parent's reasons beyond a baby's.","So failing to see a reason for the fawn's suffering doesn't make it likely there is none."],"conclusion":"Rowe's key premise isn't supported.","plain":"A toddler can't see why the doctor gives a painful shot, but that doesn't mean there's no reason.","author":"anon","source":"Stephen Wykstra, 'The Humean Obstacle to Evidential Arguments from Suffering', International Journal for Philosophy of Religion 16 (1984): 73–93; see SEP, 'Skeptical Theism'","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-rowe","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"evil-hick"}]},{"id":"evil-moral-paralysis","q":"evil","side":"no","title":"Then why stop any suffering?","claim":"If we can never judge whether suffering serves a hidden good, we'd have no reason to prevent suffering ourselves.","premises":["Skeptical theism says we can't tell whether a given evil serves some greater good beyond our grasp.","But then when we see a child being hurt, we also couldn't tell whether stopping it would block a greater good.","That would undermine ordinary moral reasoning, which is unacceptable."],"conclusion":"Skeptical theism proves too much.","plain":"If we can't tell when suffering is pointless, should we stop pulling children out of fires?","author":"anon","source":"The moral skepticism objection; see SEP, 'Skeptical Theism' (Trent Dougherty)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-skeptical-theism","premise":3}]},{"id":"evil-leibniz","q":"evil","side":"yes","title":"The best of all possible worlds","claim":"A perfect God would create the best possible world, and the best world may contain evils that make the whole better.","premises":["A perfectly good, wise and powerful God would choose the best of all possible worlds.","The best world as a whole may include some evils, because removing them would remove greater goods tied to them.","We see only a tiny part of the whole, so we can't judge that a world without these evils would be better."],"conclusion":"This world, with its evils, may be the best possible.","plain":"A dark shadow in a painting can make the whole picture more beautiful.","author":"leibniz","source":"G.W. Leibniz, Theodicy (1710), Part I, §§8–10","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-rowe","premise":2},{"type":"attacks","target":"evil-mackie","premise":2}]},{"id":"realism-naturalism","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"Goodness is natural","claim":"Moral facts are objective facts about the natural world, such as facts about what helps or harms creatures like us.","premises":["Whether an act causes suffering or promotes flourishing is a plain fact about the world.","Moral words like 'good' and 'wrong' track such facts.","So moral claims can be true or false, independently of what anyone thinks."],"conclusion":"Morality is objective, and part of the natural world.","plain":"'Cruelty is wrong' could be as factual as 'smoking is unhealthy'.","author":"anon","source":"Moral naturalism; see SEP, 'Moral Naturalism' (James Lenman) and Richard Boyd, 'How to Be a Moral Realist' (1988)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"realism-moore","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"The open question","claim":"'Good' can't mean any natural property, because it always makes sense to ask whether that property is good.","premises":["Take any natural definition of good, say, 'what we desire to desire.'","It still makes sense to ask, 'Is what we desire to desire good?' That question is open.","If the definition were right, the question would be as empty as 'Is a bachelor unmarried?'"],"conclusion":"Goodness is a simple, non-natural property, and we know it by intuition. Morality is objective but not natural.","plain":"Whatever you say goodness 'is', a thoughtful person can still ask, 'but is THAT good?'","author":"moore","source":"G.E. Moore, Principia Ethica (1903), ch. 1, §13","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-naturalism","premise":2}]},{"id":"realism-synthetic-identity","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"Water was always H2O","claim":"A property can be identical to a natural property even if the words don't mean the same.","premises":["'Is water H2O?' was once an open question, yet water is H2O.","Identities can be discovered by investigation rather than by analyzing word meanings.","So the openness of 'Is pleasure good?' doesn't show goodness isn't a natural property."],"conclusion":"Moore's argument does not refute moral naturalism.","plain":"You can wonder whether lightning is electricity even though it is.","author":"anon","source":"Standard reply to Moore; see SEP, 'Moral Naturalism' (James Lenman), drawing on Hilary Putnam, 'The Meaning of \"Meaning\"' (1975)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-moore","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"realism-naturalism"}]},{"id":"realism-ayer","q":"realism","side":"no","title":"Boo and hooray","claim":"Moral statements don't state facts at all; they express feelings.","premises":["A statement is meaningful as a factual claim only if it can be checked by experience or is true by definition.","'Stealing is wrong' can't be checked by experience, and it isn't true by definition.","So it states no fact. It expresses disapproval, like saying 'Stealing!' in a horrified tone."],"conclusion":"There are no moral facts, objective or otherwise; moral talk expresses emotion.","plain":"'Stealing is wrong' works like 'Boo, stealing!'","author":"ayer","source":"A.J. Ayer, Language, Truth and Logic (1936), ch. 6","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-moore"},{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-naturalism","premise":3}]},{"id":"realism-frege-geach","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"The Frege–Geach problem","claim":"Moral sentences work in logic just like factual ones, which expressivism can't explain.","premises":["'If lying is wrong, then getting your brother to lie is wrong' makes sense, and you can reason from it.","Inside the 'if', 'lying is wrong' expresses no disapproval; you might not disapprove of lying at all.","Yet it must mean the same inside the 'if' as outside, or the argument 'lying is wrong; if lying is wrong, then...; so...' would equivocate."],"conclusion":"Moral sentences have meanings beyond expressing feelings, which fits realism better than emotivism.","plain":"'If boo lying, then boo getting your brother to lie' is nonsense, but the moral version makes perfect sense.","author":"anon","source":"Peter Geach, 'Assertion', Philosophical Review 74 (1965): 449–465, building on Frege; see SEP, 'Moral Cognitivism vs. Non-Cognitivism' (Mark van Roojen)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-ayer","premise":3}]},{"id":"realism-mackie-relativity","q":"realism","side":"no","title":"The argument from relativity","claim":"Moral codes differ so widely between cultures that they're best explained as ways of life, not perceptions of objective truth.","premises":["Moral codes vary greatly between societies and eras.","This variation is better explained by people adopting the customs of their way of life than by some of them perceiving objective moral truths better than others.","If the best explanation of moral belief doesn't need objective values, we have reason to doubt they exist."],"conclusion":"There are no objective values.","plain":"If morality were like math, why do cultures disagree about it so much more than about 2+2?","author":"mackie","source":"J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), ch. 1, §8","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-naturalism","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-moore"}]},{"id":"realism-disagreement-reply","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"Disagreement doesn't mean no fact","claim":"Much moral disagreement comes from disagreement about other facts, and disagreement exists in science too.","premises":["People have disagreed for centuries about history, physics and medicine, yet there are facts in those areas.","Many moral disputes rest on non-moral disagreements, like what happens after death or what animals can feel.","Underneath, there is wide agreement on basics, like not harming the innocent and keeping faith with others."],"conclusion":"The argument from relativity does not undermine moral objectivity.","plain":"People once disagreed about whether the Earth moves; that didn't make the answer a matter of opinion.","author":"anon","source":"Standard realist reply; see SEP, 'Moral Realism' (Geoff Sayre-McCord) and SEP, 'Moral Anti-Realism' (Richard Joyce)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-mackie-relativity","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"realism-naturalism"}]},{"id":"realism-mackie-queerness","q":"realism","side":"no","title":"The argument from queerness","claim":"Objective values would be utterly unlike anything else in the universe, and we'd need a strange sense to detect them.","premises":["Objective values would have to be things that by their nature tell you what to do, with 'to-be-doneness' built in.","Nothing else in the universe is like that; such things would be very strange ('queer').","Knowing them would need a special faculty of moral intuition, unlike our other ways of knowing."],"conclusion":"There are no objective values; ordinary moral thought rests on an error.","plain":"A fact that bossed you around all by itself would be the weirdest thing in the universe.","author":"mackie","source":"J.L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), ch. 1, §9","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-moore"},{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-naturalism"}]},{"id":"realism-companions","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"Companions in guilt","claim":"Reasons for belief are just as 'queer' as moral reasons; if you reject one, you lose the other.","premises":["Facts about what we ought to believe, such as 'you ought to believe what the evidence supports', also tell us what to do.","If such facts are too queer to exist, then there are no facts about what we ought to believe either.","But then even the anti-realist's argument couldn't give us a reason to believe its conclusion."],"conclusion":"Since epistemic facts exist, the queerness objection fails, and moral facts can exist too.","plain":"If 'you should follow the evidence' is a real fact, then 'should' facts aren't so weird after all.","author":"anon","source":"Terence Cuneo, The Normative Web: An Argument for Moral Realism (2007)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-mackie-queerness","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"realism-moore"}]},{"id":"realism-street","q":"realism","side":"no","title":"The Darwinian dilemma","claim":"Evolution shaped our values; realists can't explain how those values could track independent moral truths.","premises":["Evolution strongly shaped our basic values: caring for kin, returning favors, punishing cheats.","Either these evolutionary pressures track independent moral truths, or they don't.","If they don't, our moral beliefs are probably off-track. If they do, the realist must claim a coincidence that the science doesn't need: the best explanation is simply that these values helped survival."],"conclusion":"Realism leaves our moral knowledge a mystery; anti-realism explains it easily.","plain":"If evolution built your moral compass to help you survive, why would it happen to point at objective truth?","author":"street","source":"Sharon Street, 'A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value', Philosophical Studies 127 (2006): 109–166","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-moore"},{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-naturalism"}]},{"id":"realism-enoch-third-factor","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"Survival is (mostly) good","claim":"Evolution could track moral truths without coincidence, because survival and flourishing are themselves good.","premises":["Evolution pushed us toward beliefs that helped survival and reproduction.","It is an objective moral truth that survival and well-being are, by and large, good.","So aiming at survival also tended to aim us at moral truth; a third factor links them."],"conclusion":"The Darwinian dilemma can be answered by a realist.","plain":"An archer aiming at the bull's-eye for prize money still hits the bull's-eye.","author":"enoch","source":"David Enoch, Taking Morality Seriously (2011), ch. 7; 'The Epistemological Challenge to Metanormative Realism', Philosophical Studies 148 (2010): 413–438","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-street","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"realism-moore"}]},{"id":"realism-enoch-indispensability","q":"realism","side":"yes","title":"You can't deliberate without it","claim":"When we decide what to do, we must assume there's a fact about what we should do.","premises":["When you deliberate, you aren't just predicting what you'll do or checking what you like; you're trying to find what you should do.","Deliberation is something we cannot avoid as rational agents.","We are justified in believing what is indispensable to an unavoidable project, as scientists believe in what their best explanations require."],"conclusion":"We are justified in believing in objective normative facts.","plain":"When you agonize over a hard choice, you act as if there's a right answer out there to find.","author":"enoch","source":"David Enoch, Taking Morality Seriously: A Defense of Robust Realism (2011), ch. 3","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"realism-mackie-queerness"},{"type":"supports","target":"realism-moore"}]},{"id":"meaning-tolstoy","q":"meaning","side":"yes","title":"What will come of it all?","claim":"If death wipes out everything I do, my life has no point, and a pointless life cannot be lived once you see it clearly.","premises":["Everything I do, and everyone I love, will be ended by death and in time forgotten.","If nothing I do leads to anything that lasts, then nothing I do has a point.","A life in which nothing has a point can only be lived while you are distracted from that fact; once you see it, going on feels impossible."],"conclusion":"Without a meaning that death cannot destroy, life is not worth living.","plain":"Building sandcastles is fun until you notice the tide takes every one.","author":"tolstoy","source":"Leo Tolstoy, A Confession (1882), chs. IV and V","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"meaning-nagel-million","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"In a million years","claim":"That our deeds won't matter in a million years doesn't show they don't matter now.","premises":["If nothing we do now will matter in a million years, then by the same reasoning nothing that happens in a million years matters now, including the fact that our deeds are forgotten.","Lasting longer doesn't make something matter: if a 70-year life were pointless, an endless one would be pointless too.","So permanence cannot be what gives our actions a point."],"conclusion":"The fact that nothing we do will last does not show that our lives are pointless.","plain":"A great meal isn't worthless just because by tomorrow it will be gone.","author":"nagel","source":"Thomas Nagel, 'The Absurd', The Journal of Philosophy 68 (1971): 716–727, section I","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-tolstoy","premise":2}]},{"id":"meaning-nozick-limits","q":"meaning","side":"yes","title":"Tied to something bigger","claim":"The worry isn't how long things last; it's that a limited life seems to need a link to something beyond its limits to have a point.","premises":["A life gets meaning by connecting with something valuable beyond itself: other people, a cause, a tradition, an achievement.","But each of those things is limited too, so the question of what gives it a point comes up again.","The questions only stop with something unlimited, which nothing finite can supply."],"conclusion":"Lasting longer is not the issue, but a purely finite life may still lack the meaning it needs.","plain":"A rope tied to another rope is only as safe as whatever the last rope is tied to.","author":"nozick","source":"Robert Nozick, Philosophical Explanations (1981), ch. 6, 'Philosophy and the Meaning of Life'","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-nagel-million","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"meaning-tolstoy"}]},{"id":"meaning-nagel-irony","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"Irony, not despair","claim":"Our lives look absurd when we step back from them, but that is no reason to think they aren't worth living.","premises":["Reasons inside a life don't need further reasons behind them: taking an aspirin for a headache needs no cosmic backing.","Life seems absurd because we can step back and see our serious concerns as arbitrary, while still taking them seriously.","If from that distant view nothing matters, then that doesn't matter either."],"conclusion":"We can live our absurd lives with irony, instead of heroism or despair.","plain":"You can laugh at how seriously you take a board game and still enjoy playing to win.","author":"nagel","source":"Thomas Nagel, 'The Absurd', The Journal of Philosophy 68 (1971): 716–727, sections I, II and VI","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-nozick-limits","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-camus","premise":3}]},{"id":"meaning-camus","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"Imagine Sisyphus happy","claim":"Life has no meaning the universe gives it, and it can be lived all the better for that.","premises":["We demand meaning and the world gives no answer: that clash is what Camus calls the absurd.","Escaping the absurd, whether by ending one's life or by a leap of faith into a meaning we can't know, is running from the truth rather than facing it.","Living with eyes open, in revolt against the absurd, with freedom and passion, lets us make the most of the one life we have."],"conclusion":"Life is worth living without a meaning; one must imagine Sisyphus happy.","plain":"A game with no prize at the end can still be worth playing with everything you've got.","author":"camus","source":"Albert Camus, The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), 'An Absurd Reasoning', 'Absurd Freedom' and 'The Myth of Sisyphus'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-tolstoy","premise":3}]},{"id":"meaning-camus-smuggles","q":"meaning","side":"yes","title":"Revolt is a meaning","claim":"Camus doesn't show life is worth living without meaning; he gives life a new meaning and calls it something else.","premises":["Camus says a life of revolt, freedom and passion is better than one of escape or giving up.","Saying one way of living is better is saying it is worth caring about and gives the life a point.","So Camus's rebel lives a meaningful life after all, just not one with a cosmic meaning."],"conclusion":"Camus supports the view that a life needs meaning, only meaning of a down-to-earth kind.","plain":"Saying 'there's no point, so my point is to defy that' is still having a point.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; on Camus as a nihilist, see Thaddeus Metz, 'The Meaning of Life', Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, section 4, 'Nihilism'","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-camus","premise":3}]},{"id":"meaning-taylor-sisyphus","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"Give Sisyphus the wish","claim":"If the gods gave Sisyphus a deep desire to roll stones, his endless task would be what he wants, and his life would be worth living to him.","premises":["Sisyphus, rolling a stone uphill forever only to see it roll back, is the picture of a life with no outside point.","If the gods planted in him a strong wish to roll stones, he would now be doing exactly what he wants, and nothing outside him would need to change.","Our lives are like his: we build, it crumbles, our children start again. What makes them worth living is our own will to do what we do."],"conclusion":"Life needs no meaning from outside; the point of living is in the living.","plain":"A child stacking blocks doesn't need the tower to last; the stacking is the point.","author":"richard_taylor","source":"Richard Taylor, Good and Evil (1970), ch. 18, 'The Meaning of Life'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-tolstoy","premise":2}]},{"id":"meaning-schopenhauer","q":"meaning","side":"yes","title":"Between pain and boredom","claim":"Getting what you want can't make life worth living, because wanting is a kind of pain and having brings boredom.","premises":["All wanting comes from lack, and lack is a kind of suffering.","When a want is satisfied, a new one takes its place, or else we sink into boredom.","So a life run on wanting alone swings like a pendulum between pain and boredom."],"conclusion":"Doing what you want, by itself, cannot make life worth living.","plain":"Scratching an itch feels good only because the itch hurt, and then you wait for the next one.","author":"schopenhauer","source":"Arthur Schopenhauer, The World as Will and Representation, vol. 1 (1819), §57","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-taylor-sisyphus","premise":2}]},{"id":"meaning-wolf","q":"meaning","side":"yes","title":"Fulfilled is not enough","claim":"A life of happy but pointless activity lacks something we rightly want; meaning comes when you love something that is really worth loving.","premises":["Picture someone who spends every day on the couch watching sitcoms, or the drugged Sisyphus who loves rolling stones.","Even if they feel fulfilled, we pity them, and we would not want their lives for our children.","What is missing is meaning: active, loving engagement with something that is actually worthwhile, beyond yourself."],"conclusion":"Feeling satisfied is not enough; a good life also needs meaning, and that depends on what you care about being worth caring about.","plain":"Loving a fake diamond can make you happy, but you'd still want it to be real.","author":"wolf","source":"Susan Wolf, Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010), Lecture 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-taylor-sisyphus","premise":3}]},{"id":"meaning-rawls-grass","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"The grass counter","claim":"If someone truly wants to spend life counting blades of grass, that is what is good for them, and outsiders have no standing to call it wasted.","premises":["Imagine a brilliant person whose only pleasure is counting blades of grass on lawns and in parks.","If, after careful thought, this is what he really wants, then counting grass is his good.","When we pity him, we are judging his life by our own tastes, not by what makes it go well for him."],"conclusion":"Whether a life is worth living for the one who lives it depends on what they care about, not on an outside ranking of worthy activities.","plain":"Who are you to tell a happy grass counter his life is wasted?","author":"rawls","source":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §65","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-wolf","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"meaning-taylor-sisyphus"}]},{"id":"meaning-metz","q":"meaning","side":"no","title":"One good among several","claim":"Even if meaning is a real good, it is only one of several, so a life can lack it and still be worth living.","premises":["Philosophers who study meaning treat it as a distinct good, separate from happiness and from being morally good.","A life can be rich in one good and poor in another: a happy life can lack meaning, and a meaningful life can be miserable.","Missing one good makes a life less good overall, not worthless."],"conclusion":"A life without meaning can still be worth living, if it has enough of the other goods.","plain":"A dinner with no dessert can still be a good dinner.","author":"metz","source":"Thaddeus Metz, 'The Meaning of Life', Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (2007, rev. 2026), section 1; Meaning in Life: An Analysis (2013)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-wolf","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"meaning-tolstoy","premise":3}]},{"id":"death-epicurus","q":"death","side":"no","title":"Where death is, I am not","claim":"Death is nothing to us: when we exist, death is not here, and when death is here, we do not exist.","premises":["Something can be good or bad for you only if you can feel it: all good and bad lies in experience.","Death is the end of all experience; the dead feel nothing.","So death is not bad for the living, since it hasn't happened to them, nor for the dead, since they no longer exist."],"conclusion":"Death is not bad for the one who dies, and it is foolish to fear it.","plain":"You can't be hurt by a party you'll never know you missed, because there will be no 'you' to miss it.","author":"epicurus","source":"Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus (c. 300 BCE), 124–125, preserved in Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, Book X","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"death-nagel-deprivation","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"What death takes away","claim":"Death is bad for you not because it feels bad, but because it takes away all the good life you would otherwise have had.","premises":["You can be harmed by things you never experience: being betrayed behind your back, or a brilliant adult reduced by injury to the mind of a contented baby.","Whether something is a misfortune depends on comparing your life with how it could have gone, not on how you feel at a given moment.","Death takes away all the goods of life you would have had if you had lived on."],"conclusion":"Death is bad for the one who dies, because it deprives them of the goods of life.","plain":"Someone who steals a winning lottery ticket you never knew you had has still taken something from you.","author":"nagel","source":"Thomas Nagel, 'Death', Noûs 4 (1970): 73–80; reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-epicurus","premise":1}]},{"id":"death-rosenbaum","q":"death","side":"no","title":"Betrayal is not like death","claim":"Nagel's examples happen to people who exist and could find out; death is something no one can ever experience at all.","premises":["A secret betrayal happens while you are alive, and you could still come to learn of it and suffer.","There is never a time when you exist and are dead, so death is not something you could ever come to experience.","So the betrayal case doesn't show that something you can never experience at all can be bad for you."],"conclusion":"Nagel's examples don't refute Epicurus; being dead is still nothing to the dead.","plain":"A rumour about you can still reach your ears; your own death never can.","author":"anon","source":"Stephen E. Rosenbaum, 'How to Be Dead and Not Care: A Defense of Epicurus', American Philosophical Quarterly 23 (1986): 217–225","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-nagel-deprivation","premise":1},{"type":"supports","target":"death-epicurus"}]},{"id":"death-feldman","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"Your whole life goes worse","claim":"An event is bad for you if your life as a whole goes worse with it than without it, and that needs no feeling and no special time.","premises":["Something is bad for you if your life as a whole contains less good with it than it would have contained without it.","If you die at 20 instead of 80, your whole life contains far less good than it otherwise would.","This comparison is true at every time, so there is no puzzle about when death harms you or how you could feel it."],"conclusion":"Death is usually bad for the one who dies, even though they never experience being dead.","plain":"A film cut off halfway is a worse film, even though no frame in it shows anything going wrong.","author":"feldman","source":"Fred Feldman, 'Some Puzzles About the Evil of Death', The Philosophical Review 100 (1991): 205–227; Confrontations with the Reaper (1992)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-epicurus","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"death-rosenbaum","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"death-nagel-deprivation"}]},{"id":"death-lucretius","q":"death","side":"no","title":"The mirror of time","claim":"The endless time after your death is just like the endless time before your birth, and no one thinks that was bad for them.","premises":["Before you were born there were endless ages in which you did not exist, and they did you no harm.","The time after your death is a mirror image of that time: once again, you will not exist.","It is unreasonable to be troubled by one of two matching states and not the other."],"conclusion":"Being dead is no more bad for you than not yet being born.","plain":"You don't mourn the centuries you missed before your birth, so why mourn the ones after your death?","author":"lucretius","source":"Lucretius, On the Nature of Things (De Rerum Natura, c. 55 BCE), Book III, lines 832–842 and 972–977","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"death-epicurus"},{"type":"attacks","target":"death-nagel-deprivation","premise":2}]},{"id":"death-nagel-asymmetry","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"The mirror is broken","claim":"Dying early takes away life you could have had; being born late takes nothing, since you could not have been born much earlier.","premises":["The time after your death is time you would have been alive in, if you had not died.","You could not have been born much earlier: someone born from different cells at a different time would not be you.","So your late birth takes nothing from you that you could have had, while your death does."],"conclusion":"The two times are not mirror images, so we can rightly mind death without minding our birth date.","plain":"Missing a train that left before you were invited isn't a loss; being thrown off the train halfway is.","author":"nagel","source":"Thomas Nagel, 'Death', Noûs 4 (1970): 73–80; reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-lucretius","premise":2}]},{"id":"death-kagan-spores","q":"death","side":"no","title":"People from spores","claim":"Nagel's reply rests on a fact about how humans happen to begin, and the mirror puzzle survives without it.","premises":["Imagine beings who grow from spores that can lie dormant for ages; a given spore could have hatched much earlier than it did.","For such a being, late birth takes away life it could have had, just as early death does.","Yet it still seems strange for it to grieve over its late hatching, so Nagel's point about our origins can't be what makes death bad."],"conclusion":"Nagel's asymmetry doesn't answer Lucretius; the mirror argument still needs a reply.","plain":"If you could have been born a century earlier, would you really feel cheated that you weren't?","author":"kagan","source":"Shelly Kagan, Death (2012), ch. 10, 'The Badness of Death'; the spores case is Robert Nozick's, reported in Thomas Nagel, 'Death', note 3 of the reprint in Mortal Questions (1979)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-nagel-asymmetry","premise":2},{"type":"supports","target":"death-lucretius"}]},{"id":"death-future-bias","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"We look forward, not back","claim":"We rightly care more about what lies ahead of us than what lies behind, so minding death but not prenatal time is no mistake.","premises":["Most people would rather have had ten hours of pain yesterday than face one hour of pain tomorrow.","This bias toward the future is deep in how planning, acting beings relate to time, not a slip in reasoning.","If that bias is reasonable, it is reasonable to mind losing future life more than missing past life."],"conclusion":"The two times can match and still differ in how much we should care about them, so death can be bad even if being unborn wasn't.","plain":"You'd rather the dentist's drilling be behind you than ahead of you, even if it lasted longer.","author":"parfit","source":"Derek Parfit, Reasons and Persons (1984), §64 (the bias toward the future); applied to Lucretius in Shelly Kagan, Death (2012), ch. 10","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-lucretius","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"death-nagel-asymmetry"}]},{"id":"death-williams-categorical","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"Reasons to go on","claim":"Some of our desires give us reason to keep living, so death works against our interests whether or not we will feel it.","premises":["Some desires depend on being alive: if I'm alive, I want to eat when hungry.","Others are categorical: they give me a reason to stay alive, such as finishing my novel or seeing my children grow up.","Death defeats categorical desires, and that is a way of being against my interests that Epicurus overlooks."],"conclusion":"Death can be bad for the one who dies, since it cuts off the projects that give them reason to live.","plain":"Dying mid-novel is bad for the novelist even though they'll never feel the book left unfinished.","author":"williams","source":"Bernard Williams, 'The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality' (1973), in Problems of the Self (1973), ch. 6","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-epicurus","premise":1},{"type":"supports","target":"death-nagel-deprivation"}]},{"id":"death-williams-tedium","q":"death","side":"no","title":"The Makropulos case","claim":"Death is not always a loss: an endless life would grow unbearably dull, so dying in good time is no harm.","premises":["In Karel Čapek's play, Elina Makropulos drinks an elixir and lives to 342; by then nothing interests her and she refuses another dose.","A character who stayed the same would, given endless time, use up everything that could hold her interest.","So more life is not always better: after enough of it, there are no goods left for death to take."],"conclusion":"Death need not be bad for the one who dies; in time it would be a release, not a loss.","plain":"Even your favourite song becomes torture if it plays for a thousand years.","author":"williams","source":"Bernard Williams, 'The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality' (1973), in Problems of the Self (1973), ch. 6","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-nagel-deprivation","premise":3}]},{"id":"death-fischer","q":"death","side":"yes","title":"Pleasures that don't wear out","claim":"An endless life need not become boring, because many goods can be enjoyed again and again.","premises":["Some pleasures are used up once had, like finishing a puzzle; others are repeatable, like a good meal, a favourite piece of music, or time with friends.","Repeatable pleasures, spaced out sensibly, stay worth having however often we return to them.","An endless life with a good mix of such goods would not run out of things worth living for."],"conclusion":"Death would still take away goods even from a very long life, so it can still be bad for the one who dies.","plain":"You don't get bored of eating dinner just because you had dinner yesterday.","author":"fischer","source":"John Martin Fischer, 'Why Immortality Is Not So Bad', International Journal of Philosophical Studies 2 (1994): 257–270","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"death-williams-tedium","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"death-nagel-deprivation"}]},{"id":"animals-singer-suffering","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"Equal pain, equal weight","claim":"Animal suffering counts as much as similar human suffering, and factory farming inflicts huge suffering for trivial gains.","premises":["The capacity to suffer is what gives a being interests that count morally.","Equal suffering deserves equal consideration whatever the species; to discount it just because the sufferer isn't human is 'speciesism', a prejudice like racism.","Buying factory-farmed meat pays for great suffering in exchange for minor human interests like taste and convenience."],"conclusion":"Buying and eating factory-farmed meat is wrong.","plain":"If a pig's pain feels as bad to the pig as yours does to you, 'but it tastes nice' is a very weak excuse for causing it.","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, Animal Liberation (1975), chs. 1 and 3","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"animals-inefficacy","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"My burger changes nothing","claim":"One shopper's purchases are far too small to change how many animals are raised and killed.","premises":["The meat industry responds to demand in huge batches, not one animal at a time.","So whether I buy this one chicken makes no difference to how many chickens suffer.","An act that makes no difference to anyone's suffering cannot be wrong because of that suffering."],"conclusion":"Arguments from suffering do not show that it is wrong for me to buy meat.","plain":"If the supermarket orders chickens by the truckload, skipping one drumstick looks like refusing to drink from the ocean to lower the tide.","author":"anon","source":"The 'causal inefficacy' objection; stated and answered in Shelly Kagan, 'Do I Make a Difference?', Philosophy & Public Affairs 39 (2011): 105–141","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-singer-suffering","premise":3}]},{"id":"animals-kagan-threshold","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"One purchase may tip the order","claim":"Even if most purchases change nothing, the one that crosses the butcher's threshold changes a lot, so each purchase makes a difference on average.","premises":["Suppose a butcher orders 25 more chickens each time 25 are sold.","Most purchases trigger nothing, but one in every 25 triggers a whole new order of 25 chickens.","So each purchase has a 1-in-25 chance of causing 25 chickens to be raised: on average, about one chicken's worth of suffering."],"conclusion":"Buying meat does make a difference in expectation, so the suffering argument still applies to you.","plain":"Like one vote in a close election, a single purchase usually changes nothing, but when it does, it changes a lot.","author":"kagan","source":"Shelly Kagan, 'Do I Make a Difference?', Philosophy & Public Affairs 39 (2011): 105–141; earlier, Peter Singer, 'Utilitarianism and Vegetarianism', Philosophy & Public Affairs 9 (1980): 325–337","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-inefficacy","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"animals-singer-suffering"}]},{"id":"animals-regan-rights","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"Subjects of a life","claim":"Animals like pigs and cows have rights, so using them for food is wrong, however kindly they are raised.","premises":["Many animals are 'subjects of a life': they have beliefs, desires, memory, feelings and a welfare that matters to them.","Every subject of a life has equal inherent value and a right not to be treated merely as a resource for others.","Raising and killing animals for food treats them merely as resources."],"conclusion":"Eating animals violates their rights, even on the best farms.","plain":"We wouldn't excuse farming humans by pointing out that the cages were roomy; the wrong is in using them at all.","author":"anon","source":"Tom Regan, The Case for Animal Rights (1983), chs. 7–9","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"animals-carruthers","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"Morality is a human contract","claim":"Moral rights come from an agreement among rational agents, and animals can't take part in it.","premises":["Moral rules are best understood as the rules rational agents would agree on to live together.","Only those who can make and keep such an agreement are directly protected by it.","Animals cannot make or keep agreements, so they have no direct moral rights; cruelty to them is wrong only because of what it shows and does to people."],"conclusion":"Animals do not have rights, and killing them for food does not wrong them.","plain":"Rights are like the rules of a club, and only members who can sign the rules are covered by them.","author":"carruthers","source":"Peter Carruthers, The Animals Issue: Moral Theory in Practice (1992)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-regan-rights","premise":2}]},{"id":"animals-rowlands","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"A veil that hides your species","claim":"A fair contract, made without knowing who you'll turn out to be, would protect animals too.","premises":["Rawls says fair rules are those we'd choose behind a 'veil of ignorance', not knowing our race, sex, talents or class, since these are undeserved.","Which species you belong to, and whether you are rational, is just as undeserved.","So the veil should hide those facts as well, and anyone choosing behind it would refuse rules that let some beings be farmed and killed for others' taste."],"conclusion":"Properly understood, the contract approach supports protecting animals rather than excluding them.","plain":"If you didn't know whether you'd be born the farmer or the pig, you wouldn't vote for factory farms.","author":"rowlands","source":"Mark Rowlands, 'Contractarianism and Animal Rights', Journal of Applied Philosophy 14 (1997): 235–247; Animal Rights: A Philosophical Defence (1998)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-carruthers","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"animals-regan-rights"}]},{"id":"animals-scruton","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"Duties to animals, not rights","claim":"Animals have no rights, but we owe them care; meat from well-kept animals meets that duty.","premises":["Rights belong to members of a moral community, who can claim them and respect the claims of others.","Animals can't recognize duties or make claims, so they have no rights, though we have real duties not to be cruel and to care for those in our keeping.","Raising animals in good conditions and killing them quickly honors those duties; factory farming does not."],"conclusion":"Eating meat from humanely raised animals is permissible.","plain":"A good shepherd owes his sheep a decent life, but not a vote.","author":"scruton","source":"Roger Scruton, Animal Rights and Wrongs (1996); 'The Conscientious Carnivore', in Steve F. Sapontzis (ed.), Food for Thought: The Debate over Eating Meat (2004)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"animals-marginal-cases","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"What about newborns?","claim":"Any line that shuts out all animals also shuts out some humans.","premises":["Newborn babies and people with severe cognitive disabilities can't recognize duties or make claims.","Yet they clearly have rights: it would be monstrous to eat them or use them in painful experiments.","So the capacity to take part in a moral community can't be what grounds rights, and some animals have richer mental lives than some humans."],"conclusion":"Scruton's reason for denying animals rights fails.","plain":"A baby can't sign a contract or keep a promise, and nobody thinks that makes her fair game.","author":"anon","source":"The 'argument from marginal cases' (or from species overlap); see Peter Singer, Animal Liberation (1975), ch. 1, and SEP, 'The Moral Status of Animals' (Lori Gruen)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-scruton","premise":2},{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-carruthers","premise":3}]},{"id":"animals-cohen-kind","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"Rights go with the kind","claim":"Rights belong to every member of a kind whose normal members are moral agents, including babies.","premises":["Humans are the kind of being that, normally, can make moral claims and recognize duties.","Rights belong to members of such a kind, whatever the abilities of any particular individual.","No animal belongs to a kind like that."],"conclusion":"Babies have rights and pigs don't; the marginal cases argument fails.","plain":"A newborn is a person at the start of the road, not a different kind of traveller.","author":"anon","source":"Carl Cohen, 'The Case for the Use of Animals in Biomedical Research', New England Journal of Medicine 315 (1986): 865–870","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-marginal-cases","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"animals-scruton"}]},{"id":"animals-rachels-individualism","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"Judge the individual","claim":"How a being may be treated should depend on what it is like, not on which group it belongs to.","premises":["When we decide how to treat someone, the relevant facts are that individual's own capacities and interests.","Treating an individual according to what is typical of its group, regardless of what it is actually like, is the mistake behind prejudice.","Cohen's 'kind' view does exactly that: it grants rights for abilities a particular baby may never have, and denies them to animals whatever their abilities."],"conclusion":"Membership in a species can't by itself justify eating animals but not humans.","plain":"We don't decide whether a particular dog can guard sheep by asking what dogs in general are good at; we look at the dog.","author":"rachels","source":"James Rachels, Created from Animals: The Moral Implications of Darwinism (1990), ch. 5 ('moral individualism')","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-cohen-kind","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"animals-marginal-cases"}]},{"id":"animals-larder","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"The logic of the larder","claim":"Farm animals exist only because we eat them, and a good life followed by a quick death is better than no life at all.","premises":["If no one ate pork, almost no pigs would be bred; the demand for meat is the reason these animals exist.","A well-raised farm animal has a life that is good for it, cut short by a quick death.","A life that is mostly good is better for the animal than never existing."],"conclusion":"Eating meat from well-treated animals does them no wrong overall; it gives them their lives.","plain":"No bacon, no pigs: the pig, you might say, has more interest than anyone in the demand for bacon.","author":"anon","source":"Leslie Stephen, Social Rights and Duties (1896), vol. 1; named and criticized in Henry Salt, 'The Logic of the Larder', in The Humanities of Diet (1914)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"animals-singer-replaceability","q":"animals","side":"no","title":"Even Singer allows replacement","claim":"On Singer's own utilitarian view, painlessly killing an animal without a sense of its future may do no wrong if a new happy animal replaces it.","premises":["For a utilitarian, what matters is the total amount of happiness and suffering in the world.","Some animals may lack any awareness of themselves as having a future, so killing them painlessly frustrates no plans or hopes of theirs.","If each such animal killed is replaced by another with an equally good life, the total is unchanged."],"conclusion":"Humane farming of such animals is not necessarily wrong, by the very theory behind the case against factory farms.","plain":"If one happy hen is swapped for another just as happy, the happiness in the world hasn't gone down. (Singer himself doubts this applies to pigs or cows.)","author":"singer","source":"Peter Singer, Practical Ethics (1979; 2nd ed. 1993), ch. 5","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"animals-larder"},{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-singer-suffering"}]},{"id":"animals-salt","q":"animals","side":"yes","title":"No one is owed existence","claim":"Bringing an animal into being is no favor to it, so it can't pay for killing it later.","premises":["A being that doesn't exist can't be benefited or deprived: there is no one there to be better or worse off.","So the farmer who breeds a pig does the pig no favor that could be 'repaid' by its death.","And if creating a life were a gift that excused killing, the same excuse would apply to breeding humans for food."],"conclusion":"The logic of the larder fails.","plain":"You can't do someone a kindness by creating them so that you can eat them later.","author":"anon","source":"Henry Salt, 'The Logic of the Larder', in The Humanities of Diet (1914)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"animals-larder","premise":3}]},{"id":"machines-turing-test","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"The imitation game","claim":"If a machine can converse so well that we can't tell it from a person, we should say it thinks.","premises":["'Can machines think?' is too vague to answer directly; a fairer question is whether a machine could do as well as a human at a game of written conversation, judged by someone who can't see who is who.","We judge that other people think from how they behave, above all from what they say.","A machine that conversed indistinguishably from a human would give us the same evidence, and refusing it the same verdict would be prejudice."],"conclusion":"A machine that passes this test should be counted as thinking.","plain":"You've never seen inside your friends' heads; you believe they think because of how they talk to you.","author":"turing","source":"Alan Turing, 'Computing Machinery and Intelligence', Mind 59 (1950): 433–460, §§1–2 and §6 (the argument from consciousness)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"machines-blockhead","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"The giant lookup table","claim":"A machine could pass the test with no thinking at all, so passing proves nothing.","premises":["Imagine a machine that stores every sensible conversation of up to an hour in a vast table and simply looks up its next reply.","It would pass the Turing test.","But it obviously doesn't think; it is a jukebox of canned answers."],"conclusion":"Behavior alone can't settle whether something thinks; what matters is how it works inside.","plain":"A parrot with a big enough memory would still be a parrot.","author":"block","source":"Ned Block, 'Psychologism and Behaviorism', Philosophical Review 90 (1981): 5–43","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-turing-test","premise":3}]},{"id":"machines-functionalism","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"Minds are what they do","claim":"A mental state is defined by its job, not by what it is made of, so a machine doing the same jobs would have a mind.","premises":["A mental state such as pain is defined by its role: what typically causes it, how it interacts with other states, and what it leads you to do.","The same role can be played by very different materials; an octopus's nervous system is built quite differently from ours, yet octopuses can surely feel pain.","So a machine whose parts played the same roles as our mental states would have those states."],"conclusion":"There is no reason in principle why a machine couldn't think.","plain":"A clock can be made of brass gears or silicon chips; what makes it a clock is that it keeps time.","author":"putnam","source":"Hilary Putnam, 'The Nature of Mental States' (1967; first published as 'Psychological Predicates'), in Mind, Language and Reality (1975)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"machines-china-brain","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"The nation of China","claim":"A system can have exactly the right functional organization and still obviously have no mind.","premises":["Imagine the population of China, each person with a two-way radio, copying the functional organization of a human brain for an hour.","By functionalism, this system would then be in pain whenever its pattern matched the pattern of pain.","But it seems absurd that the nation as a whole would feel anything."],"conclusion":"Having the right organization is not enough for a mind, so functionalism is false.","plain":"A stadium crowd doing 'the wave' moves like water, but it isn't wet.","author":"block","source":"Ned Block, 'Troubles with Functionalism', Minnesota Studies in the Philosophy of Science 9 (1978): 261–325","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-functionalism","premise":3}]},{"id":"machines-chalmers-replacement","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"Replace one neuron at a time","claim":"If your neurons were swapped one by one for chips that do the same job, your mind would not fade away.","premises":["Replace your neurons one at a time with silicon chips that do exactly the same job.","Your behavior could never change: at every stage you would still say 'I see red' and 'I feel fine'.","If your experience faded while you kept sincerely insisting it hadn't, you'd be badly out of touch with your own mind, which is very implausible."],"conclusion":"Organization, not material, is what matters, and our intuitions about strange systems like Block's nation are unreliable.","plain":"If the ship of your brain is rebuilt plank by plank in silicon, there's no plank at which the lights should go out.","author":"chalmers","source":"David Chalmers, The Conscious Mind (1996), ch. 7 ('fading qualia')","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-china-brain","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"machines-functionalism"}]},{"id":"machines-searle-room","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"The Chinese Room","claim":"Running a program, however good, is never enough for understanding.","premises":["A man who knows no Chinese sits in a room with a rulebook for matching Chinese symbols to other symbols; his answers are as good as a native speaker's.","He understands no Chinese; he just moves symbols around by their shapes.","A computer running a program does nothing more than this."],"conclusion":"No machine understands anything just by running the right program.","plain":"Following a recipe in a language you can't read might produce a perfect cake, but you still won't know what you cooked.","author":"searle","source":"John Searle, 'Minds, Brains, and Programs', Behavioral and Brain Sciences 3 (1980): 417–457","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"machines-searle-syntax","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"Syntax is not meaning","claim":"Programs work only on the shapes of symbols, and shapes alone never add up to meaning.","premises":["A program is defined purely by rules for manipulating symbols by their form (syntax).","Minds have meaning (semantics): our thoughts are about things.","Syntax by itself is not sufficient for semantics."],"conclusion":"Programs are not sufficient for minds.","plain":"A spell-checker can tell 'cat' from 'cta' without having any idea what a cat is.","author":"searle","source":"John Searle, 'Is the Brain's Mind a Computer Program?', Scientific American 262 (January 1990): 26–31","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"machines-searle-room"}]},{"id":"machines-systems-reply","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"The whole room understands","claim":"The man is only one part of the system; the system as a whole could understand Chinese.","premises":["In the story the man plays the role of a computer's processor, not of the whole computer.","No single neuron in your brain understands English either, yet you do.","So the man's not understanding doesn't show that the whole system, man plus rulebook plus papers, doesn't."],"conclusion":"The Chinese Room doesn't show that a program can't understand.","plain":"Asking whether the man understands Chinese is like asking whether one ant knows where the nest is.","author":"anon","source":"The 'systems reply', stated and answered in John Searle, 'Minds, Brains, and Programs' (1980), §I; see SEP, 'The Chinese Room Argument' (David Cole)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-searle-room","premise":3}]},{"id":"machines-searle-memorize","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"Swallow the room","claim":"Let the man memorize the rulebook and work in his head; he is then the whole system, and still understands nothing.","premises":["Let the man learn all the rules by heart and do every step in his head, even outdoors.","Now there is nothing in the system that isn't in him.","He still has no idea what any of the Chinese symbols mean."],"conclusion":"The systems reply fails: if he doesn't understand, neither does the system.","plain":"Memorizing a phone book of Chinese answers doesn't teach you Chinese; it just makes you a walking phone book.","author":"searle","source":"John Searle, 'Minds, Brains, and Programs', Behavioral and Brain Sciences 3 (1980): 417–457, reply to 'the systems reply'","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-systems-reply","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"machines-searle-room"}]},{"id":"machines-dennett-intuition","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"Don't trust the picture","claim":"Searle's story works by making an unimaginably vast process sound simple; our intuitions about it are unreliable.","premises":["A program that could really hold a Chinese conversation would be enormously complex; done by hand, one answer might take years or more.","Picturing a man calmly shuffling a few cards hides this complexity, and with it everything that might make understanding possible.","An 'obvious' intuition about a badly misdescribed case is no evidence."],"conclusion":"The Chinese Room, and its memorized version, don't show that the system lacks understanding.","plain":"Describe a brain as 'a few cells sending sparks' and it, too, will seem to understand nothing.","author":"dennett","source":"Daniel Dennett, Consciousness Explained (1991), ch. 14; Douglas Hofstadter's reflections on Searle in Hofstadter and Dennett (eds.), The Mind's I (1981)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-searle-memorize","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"machines-systems-reply"}]},{"id":"machines-lovelace","q":"machines","side":"no","title":"Lady Lovelace's objection","claim":"A machine originates nothing; it only does what we already know how to order it to do.","premises":["A machine can do only whatever we know how to order it to perform.","Real thinking involves originating something: new ideas, not just following instructions.","So whatever 'thinking' a machine shows is really its programmer's."],"conclusion":"Machines cannot think for themselves.","plain":"A music box plays a lovely tune, but the tune was the composer's, not the box's.","author":"anon","source":"Ada Lovelace, 'Notes by the Translator' to L.F. Menabrea, 'Sketch of the Analytical Engine' (1843), Note G; discussed in Turing (1950), §6","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"machines-turing-learning","q":"machines","side":"yes","title":"Machines that learn","claim":"Machines often surprise their makers, and a machine that learns can end up doing things no one told it to.","premises":["Even simple machines regularly do things their builders didn't foresee.","A machine can be built like a child's mind and educated, changing its own rules through experience.","What such a machine ends up doing is not something anyone ordered it to do, any more than a teacher dictates a pupil's later ideas."],"conclusion":"Lovelace's objection doesn't show that machines can't originate anything.","plain":"Teachers don't own their students' discoveries, even though they taught them everything they started with.","author":"turing","source":"Alan Turing, 'Computing Machinery and Intelligence', Mind 59 (1950): 433–460, §6 ('Lady Lovelace's Objection') and §7 ('Learning Machines')","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"machines-lovelace","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"machines-turing-test"}]},{"id":"skepticism-dream","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"Am I dreaming?","claim":"Since I can't tell for certain that I'm not dreaming, I can't know that my experiences show me the real world.","premises":["I have often dreamed things that felt every bit as real as what I am experiencing now.","There are no sure signs by which I can tell being awake from dreaming.","If I can't rule out that I'm dreaming, I can't know that things are as they appear."],"conclusion":"My senses don't give me knowledge of the world outside my mind.","plain":"Every dream feels real from the inside, which is exactly why you never notice you're in one.","author":"descartes","source":"René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Meditation I (AT VII 19)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"skepticism-coherence","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"Dreams don't hang together","claim":"There is a mark of waking life: it connects up with everything else in my life, and dreams don't.","premises":["Dreams are never linked by memory with the rest of my life the way waking experiences are.","When I can trace where something came from and how it fits with the rest of my life, without any break, I can be sure I'm awake.","So I can tell waking from dreaming after all."],"conclusion":"The dream argument fails, and the senses, used carefully, can be trusted.","plain":"In dreams, people appear from nowhere and vanish; in waking life you can always ask, 'how did I get here?' and get an answer.","author":"descartes","source":"René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Meditation VI (AT VII 89–90)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-dream","premise":2}]},{"id":"skepticism-stroud","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"You could dream the test","claim":"Any test for being awake could itself be dreamed, so no test can settle it.","premises":["To use a test, such as checking that everything hangs together, I'd have to know I really carried out the test and really got the result.","But I could just be dreaming that I checked and that everything fit.","So the test helps only if I already know I'm not dreaming, which is the very thing in question."],"conclusion":"Descartes' reply doesn't escape the dream argument.","plain":"Pinching yourself proves nothing if you might be dreaming the pinch.","author":"anon","source":"Barry Stroud, The Significance of Philosophical Scepticism (1984), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-coherence","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"skepticism-dream"}]},{"id":"skepticism-ignorance","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"The brain in a vat","claim":"I can't know I'm not a brain in a vat, so I can't know even that I have hands.","premises":["If I were a brain in a vat, wired to a computer that fed me experiences, everything would seem exactly as it does now.","So I don't know that I'm not a brain in a vat.","If I don't know that, then I don't know that I have hands, since knowing I have hands would rule it out."],"conclusion":"I don't know that I have hands, nor most other things about the world.","plain":"If a perfect fake would look exactly like the real thing, looking can't tell you which one you've got.","author":"anon","source":"The 'argument from ignorance'; Peter Unger, Ignorance: A Case for Scepticism (1975); named and analyzed in Keith DeRose, 'Solving the Skeptical Problem', Philosophical Review 104 (1995): 1–52; ancestor: Descartes' evil demon, Meditation I (AT VII 22–23)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"skepticism-moore","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"Here is one hand","claim":"I know I have hands far more surely than I know any premise of a skeptical argument, so it's the argument that must go.","premises":["I can prove there are things outside me: here is one hand, and here is another.","I know that I have hands more certainly than I know that any premise in the skeptic's argument is true.","When a valid argument clashes with something more certain than its premises, it is reasonable to reject a premise rather than the certainty."],"conclusion":"The skeptic's argument shows only that one of its premises is false; I do know there is an external world.","plain":"If a clever proof concludes you don't have hands, you should go looking for the mistake in the proof.","author":"moore","source":"G.E. Moore, 'Proof of an External World', Proceedings of the British Academy 25 (1939): 273–300; 'Four Forms of Scepticism', in Philosophical Papers (1959)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"skepticism-begs-question","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"Moore assumes the answer","claim":"Moore's proof simply assumes what the skeptic doubts, so it can't answer the skeptic.","premises":["The skeptic asks how you can know anything about the world given that you might be dreaming or deceived.","Moore answers by taking for granted that he knows he has hands.","An argument that assumes the point in dispute can't settle the dispute."],"conclusion":"Moore's 'proof' misses the skeptic's challenge.","plain":"Proving your bank is honest by showing the bank's own statement only works if you already trust the bank.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see Barry Stroud, The Significance of Philosophical Scepticism (1984), ch. 3","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-moore","premise":2}]},{"id":"skepticism-derose","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"Knowledge shifts with the stakes","claim":"The word 'know' sets higher or lower standards in different conversations, so both Moore and the skeptic are right in their own contexts.","premises":["Whether 'S knows that p' is true depends on the standards in play where it is said, as with words like 'tall' or 'flat'.","In everyday contexts the standards are moderate, and I truly 'know' I have hands.","Mentioning brains in vats raises the standards very high; only then does 'I don't know I have hands' come out true."],"conclusion":"The skeptic's argument is persuasive but doesn't threaten our everyday claims to know.","plain":"A table can be 'flat' for a picnic but not 'flat' for a physicist; that doesn't make you wrong about the picnic.","author":"derose","source":"Keith DeRose, 'Solving the Skeptical Problem', Philosophical Review 104 (1995): 1–52","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-ignorance","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"skepticism-moore"}]},{"id":"skepticism-putnam","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"The vat refutes itself","claim":"If I were a brain in a vat, I couldn't even think the thought 'I am a brain in a vat'.","premises":["Words refer to things only through some causal contact with them.","A brain that had always been in a vat would never have been in contact with real brains or vats, so its words 'brain' and 'vat' would refer to images or signals, not to real brains and vats.","So if I were such a brain, my sentence 'I am a brain in a vat' would be false."],"conclusion":"Whenever I say 'I am a brain in a vat', it is false; the skeptic's scenario undermines itself.","plain":"A person who'd only ever seen paintings of trees would have a word for painted trees, not for real ones.","author":"putnam","source":"Hilary Putnam, Reason, Truth and History (1981), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"skepticism-recent-vat","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"Envatted last night","claim":"Putnam's argument only works for a brain that was always in a vat, and leaves other skeptical scenarios untouched.","premises":["Someone kidnapped last night and put in a vat would have learned 'brain' and 'vat' in the real world, so their words still refer to real brains and vats.","Such a person could truly think 'I am a brain in a vat', and would be deceived about almost everything around them.","I can't rule out that this happened to me, or that I am dreaming."],"conclusion":"Putnam's argument doesn't defeat skepticism about the external world.","plain":"A trick that fails only on people who were born in the trap won't help someone who was caught yesterday.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see Anthony Brueckner, 'Brains in a Vat', Journal of Philosophy 83 (1986): 148–167, and SEP, 'Skepticism and Content Externalism' (Brueckner)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-putnam","premise":2},{"type":"supports","target":"skepticism-ignorance"}]},{"id":"skepticism-russell","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"The best explanation","claim":"We can't prove the world exists, but it's by far the best explanation of our experience.","premises":["We cannot strictly prove that anything exists outside our own experiences.","But the hypothesis that real objects cause our experiences explains them far better: the cat that was in one corner and is now hungry in another must have existed in between.","It is reasonable to believe the simplest and best explanation, especially when it agrees with our instinctive beliefs."],"conclusion":"We are justified in believing in an external world.","plain":"If your keys keep turning up where you left them, the simplest explanation is that they were there all along.","author":"russell","source":"Bertrand Russell, The Problems of Philosophy (1912), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"skepticism-hume","q":"skepticism","side":"no","title":"Experience is silent","claim":"We can never check whether our perceptions are caused by objects, so no explanation of them can be tested.","premises":["We only ever meet our own perceptions, never objects apart from them.","To learn that one thing causes another, we must observe both together; we can never observe objects and perceptions side by side.","So whether perceptions come from external objects is a question that experience cannot answer."],"conclusion":"Belief in the external world rests on natural instinct, not reasoning.","plain":"You can't check whether a photo matches the scene if you're only ever allowed to see photos.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), §12, Part 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-russell","premise":2}]},{"id":"skepticism-vogel","q":"skepticism","side":"yes","title":"Real worlds explain better","claim":"Explanation doesn't need us to watch the cause directly, and the real-world hypothesis beats the skeptic's rivals on simplicity.","premises":["Scientists rightly believe in atoms they never see, because atoms best explain what they do see; the same kind of reasoning is open to us.","A skeptical rival, like a computer feeding a vat, would have to copy the whole structure of the real-world story, such as rules of geometry for where things can be, and add more on top.","Other things equal, the simpler explanation is more likely to be true."],"conclusion":"We are justified in believing in the external world by inference to the best explanation.","plain":"A forger who copies every detail of a real painting hasn't made a simpler picture, just the same one plus a forger.","author":"anon","source":"Jonathan Vogel, 'Cartesian Skepticism and Inference to the Best Explanation', Journal of Philosophy 87 (1990): 658–666","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"skepticism-hume","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"skepticism-russell"}]},{"id":"god-first-cause","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"The first cause","claim":"The chain of causes in the world must start somewhere, with a cause that is not itself caused.","premises":["In the world, things are caused to exist by other things; nothing causes itself.","A chain of causes can't go back forever, because without a first cause there would be no middle causes and no present effects.","So there must be a first cause that is not caused by anything else."],"conclusion":"There is a first, uncaused cause, which everyone calls God.","plain":"A row of dominoes can't be falling unless something knocked over the first one.","author":"aquinas","source":"Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae I, q.2, a.3 (the Second Way)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"god-hume-parts","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Explain the parts, explain the whole","claim":"If every member of a chain of causes is explained by the one before it, nothing is left over to explain.","premises":["In an endless chain, every member has a cause: the one before it.","Once each part of a collection is explained, it is unreasonable to ask for a further cause of the whole collection.","So an endless chain needs no first cause outside it."],"conclusion":"The first-cause argument fails; the universe may simply be an endless series, or 'just there'.","plain":"If you've explained why each of twenty people came to the party, you don't owe a separate explanation of why the crowd came.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), Part IX; compare Bertrand Russell in the BBC radio debate with F.C. Copleston (1948)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-first-cause","premise":2}]},{"id":"god-leibniz-books","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"The endless copied book","claim":"Even an endless series leaves unexplained why that series exists at all, rather than another or none.","premises":["Suppose a geometry book has always existed, each copy made from an earlier one.","That explains each copy, but not why there is this book at all, or why it says what it says.","The same goes for the whole series of states of the world: everything has a sufficient reason, so its reason must lie outside the series, in a being that exists necessarily."],"conclusion":"There must be a necessary being beyond the world, which is God.","plain":"Explaining each link of an endless chain still doesn't explain why there's a chain hanging there at all.","author":"leibniz","source":"G.W. Leibniz, 'On the Ultimate Origination of Things' (1697); Monadology (1714), §§32–38","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-hume-parts","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"god-first-cause"}]},{"id":"god-van-inwagen","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Too much explanation","claim":"Leibniz's principle that everything has a sufficient reason proves too much: it makes everything necessary.","premises":["Take the sum of all the facts that merely happen to be true. By the principle, something explains it, and that explanation must be a necessary truth.","Whatever follows from a necessary truth is itself necessary.","So every fact would be necessary, and nothing could have been otherwise, which is absurd and leaves no room for God to choose freely."],"conclusion":"The principle of sufficient reason is false, so the argument from contingency fails.","plain":"If every 'why?' must have a must-be answer, then nothing could ever have been different, not even what you had for breakfast.","author":"vaninwagen","source":"Peter van Inwagen, Metaphysics (1993), the chapter 'Necessary Being: The Cosmological Argument'; see SEP, 'Principle of Sufficient Reason' (Yitzhak Melamed and Martin Lin)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-leibniz-books","premise":3}]},{"id":"god-paley","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"The watch on the heath","claim":"Living things fit together for a purpose far better than any watch, so they too must have a designer.","premises":["When we find something whose parts fit together for a purpose, like a watch, we rightly infer a designer, even if we never saw it made.","Living things, like the eye, show such fitting of parts to purpose, only far more intricately.","Nothing but a designing mind can produce such fit."],"conclusion":"Nature had a designer: God.","plain":"If a watch needs a watchmaker, an eye, which is far cleverer, needs one even more.","author":"anon","source":"William Paley, Natural Theology (1802), chs. 1–3","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"god-darwin","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Design without a designer","claim":"Natural selection explains how living things come to fit their purposes, with no designer needed.","premises":["Living things vary, and some variations help their bearers survive and reproduce.","Over vast spans of time, the useful variations pile up, step by small step, building organs as complex as the eye.","So there is a natural process that produces the fit of parts to purpose."],"conclusion":"Paley's argument fails: apparent design doesn't require a designer.","plain":"A river doesn't plan to carve a canyon, but given enough time, it does.","author":"anon","source":"Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1859), ch. 4 ('Natural Selection') and ch. 6 ('Organs of extreme perfection and complication')","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-paley","premise":3}]},{"id":"god-fine-tuning","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"A universe tuned for life","claim":"The laws of physics are set just right for life, which is far more likely if there is a designer.","premises":["If certain physical constants, such as the strength of gravity, were even slightly different, there could be no stars, no chemistry and no life.","Natural selection can't explain this, because it only works once life already exists.","A life-permitting universe is far more probable if there is a designer than if the values were set by chance."],"conclusion":"The fine-tuning of the universe is strong evidence for a designer.","plain":"If a safe's dial is set to the one combination in billions that opens it, you suspect someone set it.","author":"anon","source":"Robin Collins, 'The Teleological Argument: An Exploration of the Fine-Tuning of the Universe', in W.L. Craig and J.P. Moreland (eds.), The Blackwell Companion to Natural Theology (2009)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"replies","target":"god-paley"}]},{"id":"god-multiverse","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Many universes, one lucky","claim":"If there are many universes with different constants, some are bound to allow life, and we could only find ourselves in one of those.","premises":["Some theories in physics suggest there may be a huge number of universes, with differently set constants.","Among enough universes, some will be life-permitting by chance.","Observers can only ever exist in a life-permitting universe, so it's no surprise that ours is one."],"conclusion":"Fine-tuning doesn't need a designer to explain it.","plain":"If millions of people buy lottery tickets, someone is bound to win, and only the winner asks 'why me?'","author":"anon","source":"Standard reply; see SEP, 'Fine-Tuning' (Simon Friederich)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-fine-tuning","premise":3}]},{"id":"god-hacking","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"The inverse gambler's fallacy","claim":"Other universes don't make it any more likely that this universe is fine-tuned.","premises":["Our evidence is that this particular universe permits life.","Whether other universes exist makes no difference to how probable it is that this one came out life-permitting.","Thinking otherwise is like the gambler who sees a double six and concludes that the dice must have been rolled many times before."],"conclusion":"The multiverse doesn't explain away the fine-tuning of our universe.","plain":"Walking in on a double six doesn't tell you the dice were rolled many times before you arrived.","author":"anon","source":"Roger White, 'Fine-Tuning and Multiple Universes', Noûs 34 (2000): 260–276; the fallacy was named by Ian Hacking, 'The Inverse Gambler's Fallacy: The Argument from Design. The Anthropic Principle Applied to Wheeler Universes', Mind 96 (1987): 331–340","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-multiverse","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"god-fine-tuning"}]},{"id":"god-hiddenness","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Why would God hide?","claim":"A perfectly loving God would make sure that anyone open to a relationship could find him; yet many sincere seekers don't.","premises":["A perfectly loving God would always be open to a personal relationship with every creature able to have one.","So there would be no 'nonresistant' nonbelievers: no one open to God would fail to believe God exists, since belief is needed for that relationship.","But there are nonresistant nonbelievers: people who sincerely look for God and don't find him."],"conclusion":"There is no perfectly loving God.","plain":"A loving parent doesn't hide from a child who is calling for them.","author":"schellenberg","source":"J.L. Schellenberg, Divine Hiddenness and Human Reason (1993)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"god-murray","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"Hidden to leave us free","claim":"If God's presence were overwhelming, we would obey out of fear or self-interest, not freely and from love.","premises":["A relationship of love requires that we freely choose it, and choose to be good.","If God were obvious, the threat of punishment and the hope of reward would be so vivid that our choices would be coerced.","So a loving God has good reason to stay partly hidden."],"conclusion":"Divine hiddenness is compatible with a loving God.","plain":"Few people speed with a police car right behind them, but that doesn't make them good drivers.","author":"anon","source":"Michael Murray, 'Coercion and the Hiddenness of God', American Philosophical Quarterly 30 (1993): 27–38","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-hiddenness","premise":2}]},{"id":"god-teapot","q":"god","side":"no","title":"Russell's teapot","claim":"The burden of proof lies on the believer, and without good evidence the reasonable response is disbelief.","premises":["Whoever claims that something exists carries the burden of showing it.","If I claimed a tiny china teapot orbits the sun between Earth and Mars, too small for any telescope to see, no one could disprove me, yet it would be unreasonable to believe me.","The arguments for God fail to meet the burden of proof."],"conclusion":"It is reasonable to disbelieve in God, not merely to suspend judgment.","plain":"'You can't prove there isn't one' is no reason to believe in an invisible dragon in the garage.","author":"russell","source":"Bertrand Russell, 'Is There a God?' (1952), in The Collected Papers of Bertrand Russell, vol. 11 (1997)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"god-plantinga","q":"god","side":"yes","title":"Belief without arguments","claim":"Belief in God can be rational without arguments, just as belief in other minds or the past is.","premises":["Many rational beliefs aren't based on arguments: that other people have minds, that the world existed five minutes ago, that I had breakfast.","Such 'properly basic' beliefs are rational when they arise in the right circumstances from working faculties, unless we have reason to doubt them.","Belief in God can arise in the same way, for instance in sensing God's presence when admiring nature or feeling guilt."],"conclusion":"The believer need not carry a burden of proof to be rational.","plain":"You don't need a proof that your friends aren't robots before you can reasonably trust they're people.","author":"plantinga","source":"Alvin Plantinga, 'Reason and Belief in God', in A. Plantinga and N. Wolterstorff (eds.), Faith and Rationality (1983)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"god-teapot","premise":1}]},{"id":"speech-waldron","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"The harm is to dignity","claim":"Public hate speech attacks a group's standing as equal members of society, and the law may protect that standing.","premises":["In a decent society, everyone can go about their lives with the assurance that they will be treated as equals; this assurance is a public good.","Hate speech posted or broadcast in public, such as signs saying members of a religion are terrorists and should not be served, attacks that assurance and the targeted group's social standing.","The law may protect public goods and people's basic standing, as libel laws protect individuals' reputations."],"conclusion":"Carefully drawn laws against group defamation and hate speech are justified.","plain":"A sign in a shop window saying 'your kind not welcome' doesn't just express an opinion; it changes what the street is like for everyone it targets.","author":"waldron","source":"Jeremy Waldron, The Harm in Hate Speech (2012), chs. 3–5","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"speech-dworkin","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"Laws need everyone's voice","claim":"Banning hate speech undermines the legitimacy of the very laws that protect minorities.","premises":["Laws are legitimately imposed on everyone only if everyone had a fair chance to voice their views, including views against those laws.","Hate speech laws silence some people's views about whether laws like anti-discrimination laws should exist.","So those laws can't be legitimately enforced against the people who were silenced."],"conclusion":"We must tolerate hate speech so that we can legitimately enforce laws against discrimination itself.","plain":"You can't fairly hold someone to the result of a vote they weren't allowed to argue in.","author":"dworkin","source":"Ronald Dworkin, Foreword to Ivan Hare and James Weinstein (eds.), Extreme Speech and Democracy (2009)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"speech-waldron-legitimacy","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"Say it civilly","claim":"Hate speech laws restrict only the most vicious manner of speaking, so everyone can still argue against any law.","premises":["Well-drawn hate speech laws don't forbid opposing anti-discrimination laws; they forbid abusive, degrading attacks on groups.","A person can still voice even ugly views about policy in non-degrading terms.","So any loss of legitimacy is small, and is outweighed by the protection the laws give."],"conclusion":"Dworkin's legitimacy argument doesn't defeat hate speech laws.","plain":"A rule against shouting slurs in a debate isn't a rule against taking a side in the debate.","author":"waldron","source":"Jeremy Waldron, The Harm in Hate Speech (2012), ch. 7 ('Ronald Dworkin and the Legitimacy Argument')","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-dworkin","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"speech-waldron"}]},{"id":"speech-mill-truth","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"Silencing robs everyone","claim":"Silencing any opinion, even a false and hateful one, costs us the truth or our grip on it.","premises":["A silenced opinion may be true, and no authority is infallible.","Even a false opinion does good when challenged: it forces us to know why the truth is true, instead of holding it as 'dead dogma'.","Most often, rival opinions each hold part of the truth."],"conclusion":"Opinions should be answered, not silenced.","plain":"A muscle you never use goes weak; so does a belief you never have to defend.","author":"mill","source":"John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"speech-schauer","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"The marketplace fails","claim":"There's little reason to think truth wins in a free contest of ideas, least of all against hate.","premises":["History shows that falsehoods often spread and endure, helped by repetition, fear and prejudice.","Nothing guarantees that the 'collision' with error leads people to truth rather than to error.","So the argument from truth can't show that every kind of speech must be protected."],"conclusion":"Mill's argument doesn't rule out restricting hate speech.","plain":"A rumor can run round the world while the truth is still putting its shoes on.","author":"anon","source":"Frederick Schauer, Free Speech: A Philosophical Enquiry (1982), ch. 2 ('The Argument from Truth')","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-mill-truth","premise":2}]},{"id":"speech-langton","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"Speech that silences","claim":"Some speech stops others' speech from working, so restricting it can increase free speech overall.","premises":["Speech doesn't just express ideas; it does things, such as ranking people as inferior.","When a group is constantly degraded in public, its members' words are dismissed or not heard as they mean them, and many stop speaking up.","So unrestricted hate speech can reduce the real freedom of speech of its targets."],"conclusion":"Some restrictions on hate speech can protect free speech itself.","plain":"If one person in a meeting keeps mocking another, the mocked person may technically be free to talk but won't really be heard.","author":"anon","source":"Rae Langton, 'Speech Acts and Unspeakable Acts', Philosophy & Public Affairs 22 (1993): 293–330 (on pornography); applied to racist speech in Mari Matsuda, 'Public Response to Racist Speech: Considering the Victim's Story', Michigan Law Review 87 (1989): 2320–2381","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"speech-brandeis","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"More speech, not silence","claim":"When there is time to answer bad speech, the remedy is more speech, not enforced silence.","premises":["If there is time to expose falsehood and fallacy through discussion, people can answer harmful speech themselves.","Repression breeds fear and hatred, and leaves bad ideas unanswered instead of refuted.","Only an immediate danger, with no time to reply, justifies suppression."],"conclusion":"Answer hate speech with counter-speech; ban only incitement to imminent harm.","plain":"The best response to a bigot with a megaphone is a crowd with bigger megaphones.","author":"anon","source":"Justice Louis Brandeis, concurring, Whitney v. California, 274 U.S. 357 (1927)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-langton","premise":3}]},{"id":"speech-feinberg","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"The offense principle","claim":"The law may sometimes prevent serious offense that people can't reasonably avoid, not only harm.","premises":["Some conduct that does no physical harm still causes intense shock, disgust or humiliation.","When such offense is serious and widespread, hard to avoid, and the conduct has little value or could easily happen elsewhere, the law may prevent it.","Some public hate speech, in the streets people must use, meets these conditions."],"conclusion":"Some public hate speech may be restricted.","plain":"You're free to hold any views you like, but not to pin a hateful poster to your neighbor's front door.","author":"feinberg","source":"Joel Feinberg, Offense to Others (1985), vol. 2 of The Moral Limits of the Criminal Law","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"speech-mill-offense","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"No right not to be offended","claim":"Being offended by someone's opinion gives you no right to silence it.","premises":["The law should only prevent harm to others, not dislike of what they think.","Many people are deeply offended by opinions others hold dear, about religion for example.","If offense justified silencing, almost any opinion could be silenced by those it upsets."],"conclusion":"Offense is not a good reason to restrict speech.","plain":"Your feelings about my opinion are no more yours to control than my wallet is yours to spend.","author":"mill","source":"John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. 1 (the harm principle) and ch. 4","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-feinberg","premise":2}]},{"id":"speech-strossen","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"Who decides what's hateful?","claim":"Hate speech laws are vague, so officials end up using them against the very people they were meant to protect.","premises":["'Hate speech' has no clear definition, so whoever enforces the law decides what it covers.","Those in power tend to use vague speech laws against minorities and dissenters.","Counter-speech, education and laws against real threats and incitement protect minorities better, without the risks."],"conclusion":"Hate speech should not be banned.","plain":"Before you hand someone the power to silence your enemies, imagine your enemies holding it.","author":"anon","source":"Nadine Strossen, HATE: Why We Should Resist It with Free Speech, Not Censorship (2018)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"speech-other-democracies","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"Look at other democracies","claim":"Most mature democracies have hate speech laws and remain free societies.","premises":["Countries like Germany, Canada, Britain and New Zealand restrict hate speech.","They remain open democracies with lively public debate.","So well-drafted hate speech laws need not lead to tyranny or the silencing of dissent."],"conclusion":"The fear of abuse is not a decisive reason against hate speech laws.","plain":"If seatbelt laws were the first step to a police state, half the world would be one by now.","author":"waldron","source":"Jeremy Waldron, The Harm in Hate Speech (2012), chs. 1–2","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-strossen","premise":2}]},{"id":"speech-lawrence","q":"speech","side":"yes","title":"A slur is a slap","claim":"Much hate speech can't be answered with more speech, because it wounds at once and its targets often can't reply.","premises":["A racial slur hurled face to face injures instantly, like a slap, before any reply is possible.","Its targets are often in no position to answer: outnumbered, frightened, or not listened to.","So 'more speech' is no remedy for much hate speech, and it puts the burden of answering on the victims."],"conclusion":"Brandeis's remedy fails for hate speech.","plain":"Telling someone who has just been spat at to 'debate it' misunderstands what happened.","author":"anon","source":"Charles R. Lawrence III, 'If He Hollers Let Him Go: Regulating Racist Speech on Campus', Duke Law Journal (1990): 431–483","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-brandeis","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"speech-langton"}]},{"id":"speech-fighting-words","q":"speech","side":"no","title":"Threats are already illegal","claim":"Face-to-face slurs and threats can already be punished under narrow rules, so they give no reason for broad bans on hateful opinions.","premises":["Even US law allows punishing 'fighting words' said face to face, true threats, and targeted harassment.","The slur hurled at a person in the street falls under these narrow rules.","So the slap-in-the-face case supports only narrow rules like these, not bans on hateful views expressed to the public."],"conclusion":"Lawrence's argument doesn't justify hate speech laws of the European kind.","plain":"A law against punching people doesn't need to be widened into a law against rude books.","author":"anon","source":"Chaplinsky v. New Hampshire, 315 U.S. 568 (1942); Nadine Strossen, HATE: Why We Should Resist It with Free Speech, Not Censorship (2018)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"speech-lawrence","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"speech-brandeis"}]},{"id":"inequality-rawls-lottery","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"The natural lottery","claim":"No one deserves their talents or their family, so inequalities are just only if they help the worst off.","premises":["No one deserves the natural talents they were born with or the family they were born into; these are 'arbitrary from a moral point of view'.","A just society shouldn't let such arbitrary luck decide people's life prospects.","Some inequality can still be allowed, if, for instance, higher pay for doctors makes everyone, including the worst off, better off than they'd be otherwise."],"conclusion":"Economic inequalities are unjust unless they work to the benefit of the least advantaged (the 'difference principle').","plain":"If prizes in a race went to whoever happened to start nearest the finish line, nobody would call the race fair.","author":"rawls","source":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §§12–13 and §17","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"inequality-nozick-assets","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"Entitled to what you have","claim":"Even if no one deserves their talents, people are entitled to them and to what they earn with them.","premises":["Whether or not people's natural talents are arbitrary from a moral point of view, they are entitled to them.","If people are entitled to something, they are entitled to whatever flows from it by fair means, like the fruits of their work.","So the fact that talents are undeserved gives no one else a claim to what the talented earn."],"conclusion":"Rawls's natural lottery doesn't show that inequality is unjust.","plain":"You didn't 'deserve' your kidneys either, but that doesn't mean the state may give one away.","author":"nozick","source":"Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), ch. 7, section II","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-rawls-lottery","premise":2}]},{"id":"inequality-murphy-nagel","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"There is no pre-tax income","claim":"What you 'earn' depends on laws of property, contract and tax, so it can't set the standard for judging those laws.","premises":["Your income and property exist only within a legal system that defines ownership, enforces contracts and collects taxes.","So there is no natural 'pre-tax' amount that is yours before the law decides what is fair.","Asking how much people may keep is really asking which property system is just, and Nozick's entitlements can't answer that, because they presuppose one."],"conclusion":"Entitlement to what you earn can't by itself rule out a system that reduces inequality.","plain":"A football player can't say a rule change 'takes away' goals he would have scored under the old rules; the rules decide what a goal is.","author":"nagel","source":"Liam Murphy and Thomas Nagel, The Myth of Ownership: Taxes and Justice (2002)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-nozick-assets","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"inequality-rawls-lottery"}]},{"id":"inequality-hayek","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"The mirage of social justice","claim":"In a free market no one hands out shares, so the resulting distribution can't be just or unjust.","premises":["Only deliberate actions or decisions can be just or unjust.","In a market economy, who ends up with what results from millions of separate choices, not from anyone's decision.","So the overall distribution is like the weather: it can be lucky or unlucky, but not unjust."],"conclusion":"'Social justice' is a mirage; market inequality is not unjust.","plain":"No one is to blame for a drought, and no one decided that a pop star should earn more than a nurse.","author":"hayek","source":"F.A. Hayek, Law, Legislation and Liberty, vol. 2: The Mirage of Social Justice (1976), ch. 9","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-rawls-lottery","premise":2}]},{"id":"inequality-nozick-wilt","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"Wilt Chamberlain","claim":"If people start from a just distribution and freely exchange, whatever inequality results is just.","premises":["Start with whatever distribution you think is just, say, perfect equality.","A million fans each freely pay 25 cents to watch Wilt Chamberlain play; he ends up with $250,000, far more than anyone else.","If the starting point was just and every step was voluntary, the result is just too."],"conclusion":"Inequality that arises from free exchanges is not unjust; justice depends on how holdings came about, not on their pattern.","plain":"If everyone starts with the same pocket money and lots of them choose to buy your lemonade, nobody has been wronged when you end up richest.","author":"nozick","source":"Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), ch. 7, section I ('How Liberty Upsets Patterns')","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"inequality-nozick-forbid","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"Liberty upsets patterns","claim":"Keeping any pattern of equality would require constant interference with people's free choices.","premises":["People will always want to trade, give gifts and pay for things they like.","Each such free exchange tends to upset whatever pattern of distribution was set up.","So to keep the pattern, the state would have to keep taking from some and giving to others, or forbid such exchanges altogether."],"conclusion":"An equal society would have to forbid capitalist acts between consenting adults.","plain":"Keeping everyone's money equal would be like keeping a beach flat while children are building sandcastles.","author":"nozick","source":"Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), ch. 7, section I","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"inequality-nozick-wilt"}]},{"id":"inequality-cohen-wilt","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"Who agreed to his power?","claim":"The fans agreed to pay Chamberlain, but not to the power his fortune gives him over everyone else.","premises":["A large fortune gives its owner power over others, for example over workers, prices and politics.","The fans agreed only to pay for a game; they didn't agree to create that power, and many people affected by it never agreed to anything.","A series of voluntary exchanges can still produce a result that wrongs third parties or later generations."],"conclusion":"Nozick's argument doesn't show that the resulting inequality is just.","plain":"Each of us may freely sell our house to the same buyer, but no one agreed that he should end up owning the whole town.","author":"ga_cohen","source":"G.A. Cohen, 'Robert Nozick and Wilt Chamberlain: How Patterns Preserve Liberty', Erkenntnis 11 (1977): 5–23; reprinted in Self-Ownership, Freedom, and Equality (1995), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-nozick-wilt","premise":3}]},{"id":"inequality-frankfurt","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"Enough, not equal","claim":"What matters morally is that everyone has enough, not that everyone has the same.","premises":["What is bad about poverty is that the poor have too little to live a decent life, not that others have more.","If everyone had enough, a billionaire's having far more would not wrong anyone.","Fixing on equality distracts people from asking what they themselves actually need."],"conclusion":"Economic inequality is not unjust in itself; deprivation is.","plain":"If everyone at the table has a full plate, it doesn't matter that someone's plate is bigger.","author":"frankfurt","source":"Harry Frankfurt, 'Equality as a Moral Ideal', Ethics 98 (1987): 21–43; On Inequality (2015)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"inequality-smith-linen","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"The linen shirt","claim":"What counts as 'enough' depends on what others have, so 'enough' and 'equal' can't be separated.","premises":["The necessities of life include not just what you need to survive, but what custom makes it shameful to be without.","In Smith's day, a respectable day-laborer would be ashamed to appear in public without a linen shirt, though the ancient Greeks lived comfortably without one.","So as others grow richer, the level of 'enough' rises, and a person can fall below it without getting any poorer."],"conclusion":"Inequality matters even on the 'enough' view, because what's enough is relative to what others have.","plain":"Without a phone today, you can't easily apply for a job; fifty years ago, nobody needed one.","author":"smith","source":"Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations (1776), Book V, ch. 2","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-frankfurt","premise":2}]},{"id":"inequality-anderson","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"Equals, not masters and servants","claim":"The point of equality is a society where people relate as equals, and large economic gaps create domination.","premises":["The goal of equality is not to even out bad luck but to end oppression: a society where no one is master or servant to another.","Large economic gaps turn into power: over workers, over politics, and over who gets respect.","Domination and status hierarchies of this kind are unjust."],"conclusion":"Economic inequalities that create domination are unjust, even if everyone has enough.","plain":"A worker who can't afford to lose her job can't say no to a boss who bullies her, however decent her wage.","author":"anderson_e","source":"Elizabeth Anderson, 'What Is the Point of Equality?', Ethics 109 (1999): 287–337","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"inequality-parfit","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"The levelling down objection","claim":"If inequality were bad in itself, making the better-off worse off would be an improvement, even if it helped no one.","premises":["If inequality is bad in itself, then removing it is better in at least one way, however it is removed.","Imagine removing it by making the better-off worse off, say through a disaster, while helping no one at all.","It is absurd that this would be better in any way."],"conclusion":"Inequality is not bad in itself; what matters is helping the worse off (giving them priority).","plain":"If one twin has a cold and the other doesn't, giving the healthy twin a cold too doesn't improve anything.","author":"parfit","source":"Derek Parfit, 'Equality or Priority?', Lindley Lecture, University of Kansas (1991; published 1995)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"inequality-temkin","q":"inequality","side":"yes","title":"Better in one way","claim":"Levelling down is worse overall, but still better in one respect: it is fairer.","premises":["Something can be better in one respect and worse overall.","When some are worse off than others through no fault of their own, that is unfair, whether or not fixing it helps anyone.","So levelling down makes things better in respect of fairness, even if far worse overall."],"conclusion":"The levelling down objection doesn't show that inequality isn't bad in itself.","plain":"Cancelling a race because one runner was given a head start helps no runner, but it does remove an unfairness.","author":"anon","source":"Larry Temkin, Inequality (1993); 'Egalitarianism Defended', Ethics 113 (2003): 764–782","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-parfit","premise":3}]},{"id":"inequality-enough-to-be-equal","q":"inequality","side":"no","title":"Equal citizens, unequal incomes","claim":"Relational equality only requires everyone to have enough to stand as an equal citizen, which allows large inequalities above that line.","premises":["Anderson's 'democratic equality' asks that everyone have effective access to what they need to function as an equal citizen: health care, education, a voice in politics, freedom from domination.","That is a threshold of sufficiency; it says nothing against inequalities among people who are all above it.","Where money does turn into domination, as in politics or the workplace, it can be curbed directly, by campaign finance rules or labor rights, without evening out incomes."],"conclusion":"Anderson's argument shows that domination is unjust, not that economic inequality as such is.","plain":"If the problem is that the rich can buy elections, the fix is to stop elections being for sale.","author":"anon","source":"Elizabeth Anderson, 'What Is the Point of Equality?', Ethics 109 (1999): 287–337, on democratic equality as a sufficiency standard; see SEP, 'Egalitarianism' (Richard Arneson)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"inequality-anderson","premise":2},{"type":"supports","target":"inequality-frankfurt"}]},{"id":"euthanasia-rachels","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"The slower death","claim":"If doctors may let a dying patient die, they should be allowed to end his life quickly, which is often kinder.","premises":["Doctors are already allowed to withhold treatment so that a hopeless patient dies, and medical bodies accept this.","Letting die can take days of suffering that a lethal injection would spare.","Killing is not in itself worse than letting die: the motive and the result are what matter, and here both are the same."],"conclusion":"If passive euthanasia is permitted, active euthanasia should be too, and it is sometimes more humane.","plain":"If you've already agreed a journey should end, insisting it end in a slow crawl rather than a quick stop helps no one.","author":"rachels","source":"James Rachels, 'Active and Passive Euthanasia', New England Journal of Medicine 292 (1975): 78–80","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"euthanasia-double-effect","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"Intending versus foreseeing","claim":"Stopping treatment aims to end a burden, not a life; a lethal injection aims at death itself.","premises":["There is a moral difference between intending a death and merely foreseeing it as a side effect.","A doctor who withdraws burdensome treatment, or gives morphine to relieve pain, aims at relief; death is a foreseen side effect.","A doctor who gives a lethal injection aims at the patient's death as the very means of ending the suffering."],"conclusion":"Passive euthanasia can be permissible while active euthanasia is not.","plain":"A surgeon who knows an operation may kill is not doing the same thing as a poisoner, even if the patient dies either way.","author":"anon","source":"The doctrine of double effect, from Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.64, a.7; see SEP, 'Doctrine of Double Effect' (Alison McIntyre)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-rachels","premise":3}]},{"id":"euthanasia-thomson","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"Intentions don't change the act","claim":"Whether an act is permissible doesn't depend on what is going on in the doctor's head.","premises":["Two doctors can give the same dose of morphine to the same patient, one aiming at relief and one at death.","If double effect were right, one act would be permissible and the other not, though the patient, the dose and the result are identical.","But what makes an act permissible is what the agent does and why it is justified, not the agent's private aim."],"conclusion":"Double effect can't draw a line between permissible pain relief and impermissible assisted death.","plain":"Whether you may open a door can't depend on whether you're secretly thinking about the draft or the view.","author":"thomson","source":"Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'Physician-Assisted Suicide: Two Moral Arguments', Ethics 109 (1999): 497–518; see also Ronald Dworkin, Thomas Nagel, Robert Nozick, John Rawls, Thomas Scanlon and Judith Jarvis Thomson, 'Assisted Suicide: The Philosophers' Brief', New York Review of Books (27 March 1997)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-double-effect","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"euthanasia-rachels"}]},{"id":"euthanasia-dworkin","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"Your death, your life","claim":"How a life ends is part of the story of that life, so competent people should decide it for themselves.","premises":["People care not only about their experiences but about the shape of their lives as a whole, including how they end.","Forcing someone to die in a way that others approve of but that they see as a betrayal of their own life is a grave form of tyranny.","Competent adults should be free to make such deeply personal decisions according to their own convictions."],"conclusion":"Voluntary euthanasia should be permitted for competent adults who want it.","plain":"People get to write their own life story; it's strange to hand the last chapter to someone else.","author":"dworkin","source":"Ronald Dworkin, Life's Dominion: An Argument About Abortion, Euthanasia, and Individual Freedom (1993), ch. 7","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"euthanasia-velleman","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"A dangerous option","claim":"Merely offering the option of euthanasia can harm the very patients it is meant to help.","premises":["Having more options is not always better: once an option exists, you can be held responsible for not taking it.","Once euthanasia is available, a sick person who goes on living has chosen to, and may feel pressed to justify that choice, especially if they feel a burden to their family.","Many patients would be better off not facing that pressure at all."],"conclusion":"There are strong reasons against establishing a right to die, even if some individuals would benefit.","plain":"Once there's an 'exit' sign, everyone who stays in the room may be asked why they haven't left.","author":"anon","source":"J. David Velleman, 'Against the Right to Die', Journal of Medicine and Philosophy 17 (1992): 665–681","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-dworkin","premise":3}]},{"id":"euthanasia-aquinas","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"Life is not ours to end","claim":"Ending your own life, or helping someone end theirs, goes against nature, the community and God.","premises":["Every living thing naturally loves itself and seeks to keep itself in being.","Each person belongs to a community, and ending a life injures the community.","Life is a gift from God, and only God has authority over life and death."],"conclusion":"Suicide is always wrong, and so is helping someone to it.","plain":"A life, like a gift held in trust, isn't yours to throw away.","author":"aquinas","source":"Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.64, a.5","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"euthanasia-hume","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"Then don't build dams either","claim":"If shortening a life defies God, then so does lengthening one, which is absurd.","premises":["If ending a life interfered with God's providence, so would every human act that changes when a life ends, including medicine.","Nobody thinks it wrong to save a life, or to turn a river from its course; we use our powers to change nature all the time.","So there is no reason to think ending a life of misery usurps God's authority, while lengthening one doesn't."],"conclusion":"The religious argument against ending one's life fails.","plain":"If dying early defies God's plan, then so does every antibiotic.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, 'Of Suicide' (written c. 1755; published 1777)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-aquinas","premise":3}]},{"id":"euthanasia-kant","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"Persons are not means","claim":"Ending a person's life to escape suffering treats the person as a mere means to comfort.","premises":["Persons have dignity, a worth beyond any price, because they are rational beings.","Someone who destroys himself to escape a painful condition uses his person merely as a means to ending his discomfort.","It is always wrong to treat humanity, in yourself or in another, merely as a means."],"conclusion":"Suicide to escape suffering is wrong, and so is helping with it.","plain":"Your life is not a coin you can spend to buy relief; it's the one doing the spending.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), Ak. 4:421–422 and 4:429","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"euthanasia-dignity-lost","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"When illness destroys the person","claim":"Even if dignity matters more than comfort, dying can be permissible when illness is destroying a person's ability to live as a rational agent.","premises":["Respect for dignity forbids trading a person's life for comfort, as Kant says.","But some illnesses destroy the very capacities that give a person dignity: the ability to think, choose and act.","When that is happening, ending one's life does not trade away dignity; it can be a way of respecting it."],"conclusion":"A dignity-based ethic need not forbid all euthanasia.","plain":"Respecting a great building can mean refusing to sell it, but also refusing to watch it crumble into a ruin no one recognizes.","author":"anon","source":"J. David Velleman, 'A Right of Self-Termination?', Ethics 109 (1999): 606–628","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-kant","premise":2}]},{"id":"euthanasia-slope","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"The slippery slope","claim":"Even if some cases are justified, a law allowing euthanasia will lead to mistakes, pressure on the vulnerable, and wider killing.","premises":["Patients' consent can be unclear, mistaken, or shaped by depression, pain or pressure from others.","Once killing is accepted for some, the limits tend to widen, for example to those who cannot consent.","The risk to the vulnerable outweighs the benefit to the few who clearly want to die."],"conclusion":"Euthanasia should not be permitted.","plain":"Once you open a gate for one sheep, it's very hard to stop the rest of the flock following.","author":"anon","source":"Yale Kamisar, 'Some Non-Religious Views Against Proposed \"Mercy-Killing\" Legislation', Minnesota Law Review 42 (1958): 969–1042","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"euthanasia-battin","q":"euthanasia","side":"yes","title":"Look at the evidence","claim":"Where assisted dying is legal, the feared harm to vulnerable groups hasn't shown up.","premises":["Oregon and the Netherlands have allowed assisted dying for years and kept careful records.","Studies of those records found no heightened risk to groups such as the elderly, the poor, women or minorities.","A slippery slope argument must rest on evidence about what actually happens."],"conclusion":"The slippery slope objection does not defeat legal euthanasia.","plain":"If you're told a bridge will collapse, the fact that thousands have crossed it safely counts for something.","author":"anon","source":"Margaret P. Battin et al., 'Legal Physician-Assisted Dying in Oregon and the Netherlands: Evidence Concerning the Impact on Patients in \"Vulnerable\" Groups', Journal of Medical Ethics 33 (2007): 591–597","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-slope","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"euthanasia-dworkin"}]},{"id":"euthanasia-slope-real","q":"euthanasia","side":"no","title":"The rules have widened","claim":"Where euthanasia is legal, it has been extended over time to new groups, such as people with psychiatric illness.","premises":["Euthanasia laws were first defended for clear-headed adults dying in unbearable pain.","In the Netherlands and Belgium, they are now also used for people with psychiatric conditions who are not dying, and Belgium has extended them to minors.","Studies of psychiatric cases find patients who were often socially isolated and doctors who sometimes disagreed about whether the criteria were met."],"conclusion":"The slope is real, so the evidence doesn't settle the worry.","plain":"A path that started at the cliff's edge for emergencies now runs a long way inland.","author":"anon","source":"Scott Y.H. Kim, Raymond G. De Vries and John R. Peteet, 'Euthanasia and Assisted Suicide of Patients With Psychiatric Disorders in the Netherlands 2011 to 2014', JAMA Psychiatry 73 (2016): 362–368","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"euthanasia-battin","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"euthanasia-slope"}]},{"id":"beauty-hume-sentiment","q":"beauty","side":"no","title":"Beauty is a feeling","claim":"Beauty is not a quality in things; it is a feeling in the mind of whoever looks at them.","premises":["Judgments of fact are about something beyond us, so they can be right or wrong.","Feelings don't represent anything beyond themselves, so no feeling is right or wrong; each is simply what it is.","Finding something beautiful is having a feeling of pleasure in it."],"conclusion":"Each mind perceives a different beauty, and none is more correct than another.","plain":"Calling a painting beautiful is like calling a bath relaxing: it tells you about the bather, not the water.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, 'Of the Standard of Taste' (1757), para. 7 (a view Hume states and then qualifies)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"beauty-hume-ogilby","q":"beauty","side":"yes","title":"Ogilby and Milton","claim":"Some judgments of taste are plainly wrong, so there must be a standard of taste.","premises":["Anyone who said the minor poet Ogilby was as great as Milton would be thought absurd, as if he'd said a molehill was as high as a mountain.","So, despite the feelings view, we treat some judgments of taste as correct and others as mistaken.","The standard is the joint verdict of true judges: those with delicate taste, practice, experience in comparing works, and freedom from prejudice."],"conclusion":"There is a real standard of taste, even though beauty involves feeling.","plain":"Anyone can have a feeling about wine, but a trained taster really can tell you something you missed.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, 'Of the Standard of Taste' (1757), paras. 8 and 23","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-hume-sentiment","premise":2}]},{"id":"beauty-levinson","q":"beauty","side":"no","title":"Why care what critics prefer?","claim":"Even if ideal critics agree, that gives the rest of us no reason to share their preferences.","premises":["Hume's ideal critics have different sensibilities from most of us.","That they prefer Milton tells me what they enjoy, not what I will enjoy.","Unless their verdicts give me a reason to prefer what they prefer, they don't set a standard that binds me."],"conclusion":"Hume's 'standard of taste' doesn't show that taste is objective.","plain":"If wine experts love a bitter vintage I can't stand, why should I drink it?","author":"levinson","source":"Jerrold Levinson, 'Hume's Standard of Taste: The Real Problem', Journal of Aesthetics and Art Criticism 60 (2002): 227–238","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-hume-ogilby","premise":3}]},{"id":"beauty-santayana","q":"beauty","side":"no","title":"Pleasure seen as a quality","claim":"Beauty is our own pleasure, which we mistakenly experience as a quality of the object.","premises":["When something pleases us immediately through the senses, we tend to experience the pleasure as part of the object, like its color or shape.","Beauty is exactly this kind of 'objectified' pleasure.","So beauty exists only in our responses, though it seems to be out there."],"conclusion":"Beauty is not an objective property of things.","plain":"A song feels 'sad' the way a room feels 'cozy': we put the feeling there and then find it.","author":"santayana","source":"George Santayana, The Sense of Beauty (1896), Part I, §11","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"beauty-kant","q":"beauty","side":"neutral","title":"Subjective, but speaking for all","claim":"A judgment of beauty rests on a feeling, yet unlike mere liking it claims everyone's agreement.","premises":["If I say a wine is pleasant to me, I don't mind if you disagree; that is mere 'agreeableness'.","But if I call something beautiful, I speak as if beauty were a property of the thing, and I demand that others agree.","This demand rests not on a concept or proof but on feelings that all human minds are capable of sharing."],"conclusion":"Beauty is neither an objective property nor a mere private preference.","plain":"You won't argue with someone who prefers cherry to lime, but you will argue with someone who says the Taj Mahal is ugly.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, Critique of the Power of Judgment (1790), §§6–8 and §40","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-santayana","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-kolbel","premise":2}]},{"id":"beauty-plato","q":"beauty","side":"yes","title":"Beauty itself","claim":"All beautiful things share in one Beauty that is eternal and doesn't depend on any viewer.","premises":["Many different things are beautiful: bodies, souls, customs, laws, kinds of knowledge.","What they have in common must be something beyond any one of them: Beauty itself.","Beauty itself does not come and go, grow or fade, or look beautiful to some and ugly to others."],"conclusion":"Beauty is objective; beautiful things are beautiful by sharing in Beauty itself.","plain":"Many songs can be in the key of C, but the key isn't any one song.","author":"plato","source":"Plato, Symposium 210a–212a (Diotima's ladder of love)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"beauty-tastes-vary","q":"beauty","side":"no","title":"Tastes vary everywhere","claim":"Ideals of beauty vary so much between cultures and eras that they can't be tracking one objective Beauty.","premises":["Ideals of beauty differ hugely across times and places: in body shapes, musical scales, architecture and dress.","If beauty were an objective quality, we'd expect people to converge on it, as they do on shapes and sizes.","The variety is better explained by upbringing and custom."],"conclusion":"Beauty is not an objective quality of things.","plain":"Yesterday's height of fashion is today's embarrassing photo.","author":"anon","source":"Standard argument; see Hume, 'Of the Standard of Taste' (1757), paras. 1–2, and SEP, 'Aesthetic Judgment' (Nick Zangwill)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-plato","premise":3}]},{"id":"beauty-hume-time","q":"beauty","side":"yes","title":"The test of time","claim":"Works that are admired across many ages and nations show that some things please human nature as such.","premises":["Fashions and prejudices pass, but some works are admired again and again by people in very different times and places.","The same Homer who pleased in ancient Athens and Rome is still admired in Paris and London.","Such lasting agreement is best explained by real qualities in the work suited to human nature, not by local custom."],"conclusion":"Variation in taste is mostly on the surface; there are lasting standards of beauty.","plain":"Hit songs come and go, but people keep returning to Bach.","author":"hume","source":"David Hume, 'Of the Standard of Taste' (1757), para. 11","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-tastes-vary","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"beauty-plato"}]},{"id":"beauty-dutton","q":"beauty","side":"yes","title":"A shared human taste","claim":"Beneath the variety, humans everywhere share deep aesthetic preferences, shaped by evolution.","premises":["People in very different cultures show similar preferences, for example for landscapes with open space, trees, water and a path, and for visible skill in art.","These shared tastes are best explained by an evolved human nature, not by local fashion.","So standards of beauty are widely shared, not a matter of individual whim."],"conclusion":"Cultural variety doesn't show that beauty is merely subjective.","plain":"Calendar landscapes look much the same all over the world.","author":"dutton","source":"Denis Dutton, The Art Instinct: Beauty, Pleasure, and Human Evolution (2009), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-tastes-vary","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"beauty-plato"}]},{"id":"beauty-sibley","q":"beauty","side":"yes","title":"Seeing what is there","claim":"Qualities like 'graceful' or 'garish' are perceived in things, like other features, though it takes taste to notice them.","premises":["We describe things as graceful, delicate, balanced or garish, and we argue for these descriptions by pointing to features others have missed.","These qualities depend on ordinary features such as lines, colors and sounds, but not everyone can perceive them without training, just as not everyone can hear a wrong note.","Perceiving something that is there to be noticed is an objective matter, even if not everyone can do it."],"conclusion":"Aesthetic qualities are real features of things.","plain":"Not everyone can hear that a piano is out of tune, but it still is.","author":"sibley","source":"Frank Sibley, 'Aesthetic Concepts', Philosophical Review 68 (1959): 421–450; 'Objectivity and Aesthetics', Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society, Supplementary Volume 42 (1968): 31–54","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"beauty-kolbel","q":"beauty","side":"no","title":"Faultless disagreement","claim":"Two people can disagree about beauty without either making a mistake, which wouldn't be possible if beauty were objective.","premises":["If beauty were an objective feature, then whenever two people disagree about it, at least one would be making a mistake.","But in many disagreements of taste, both people can be fully informed, attentive and sensitive, and neither seems at fault.","So aesthetic claims are not simply true or false of the object; their truth is relative to a standard of taste."],"conclusion":"Beauty is not an objective property.","plain":"If you find a joke hilarious and I don't, we disagree, but it's odd to say one of us got it wrong.","author":"anon","source":"Max Kölbel, 'Faultless Disagreement', Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society 104 (2004): 53–73","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"beauty-sibley","premise":3}]},{"id":"disobey-crito","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"The Laws speak to Socrates","claim":"Socrates must not escape from prison, even though his death sentence is unjust, because a citizen owes the laws obedience.","premises":["By choosing to live in Athens all his life when he was free to leave, Socrates agreed to obey its laws.","The laws made his life possible: under them his parents married, and he was raised and educated, so he owes them more than a child owes a parent.","A city cannot survive if its court verdicts can be set aside by anyone who disagrees with them, so escaping would be doing what he can to destroy the laws."],"conclusion":"A citizen must either persuade the city to change its laws or obey them, even when this means accepting an unjust verdict.","plain":"A guest who enjoys a house for seventy years doesn't get to smash its windows the day he dislikes one of its rules.","author":"socrates","source":"Plato, Crito 50a–54d (the speech of the Laws)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"disobey-apology","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"Socrates would disobey too","claim":"Socrates himself held that some commands must not be obeyed, so his loyalty was to justice, not to obedience as such.","premises":["At his trial Socrates told the jury that if they freed him on condition that he give up philosophy, he would not obey: he would obey the god rather than them.","Earlier, when the Thirty Tyrants ordered him to help arrest an innocent man, Leon of Salamis, he refused and went home, at the risk of his life.","So his deepest rule was never to do injustice; the Crito shows at most that he would not run away from his punishment."],"conclusion":"The Crito does not show that we must obey every law; it allows open refusal to do injustice.","plain":"Someone who says 'I'll go to jail rather than do that' is not someone who thinks you must always do as you're told.","author":"anon","source":"Plato, Apology 29c–d and 32c–d; Richard Kraut, Socrates and the State (1984)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-crito","premise":3}]},{"id":"disobey-king","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"Letter from Birmingham Jail","claim":"We have a moral duty to obey just laws, and an equal moral duty to disobey unjust ones.","premises":["One has a moral responsibility not only to obey just laws but to disobey unjust ones.","A law is unjust if it degrades human personality, if a majority imposes it on a minority without binding itself, or if the minority had no say in making it.","The segregation laws of the American South were unjust by every one of these tests."],"conclusion":"Breaking segregation laws, openly, lovingly and with willingness to accept the penalty, was right.","plain":"A referee who makes one team play with its hands tied isn't running a game anyone owes respect to.","author":"anon","source":"Martin Luther King Jr., 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (16 April 1963)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"disobey-hobbes","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"Who decides what's unjust?","claim":"If each person may disobey whatever their own conscience judges unjust, there is no law at all.","premises":["People disagree deeply and sincerely about what is just.","If each private person is the judge of which laws deserve obedience, the law becomes whatever each person happens to think, which is no law.","Without a common authority whose judgment settles disputes, we slide back toward a war of all against all, which is worse than almost any unjust law."],"conclusion":"Private conscience gives no license to break the law; the doctrine that it does is a disease of the state.","plain":"A football match where each player decides which of the referee's calls to accept soon stops being a match.","author":"hobbes","source":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), chs. 13 and 29","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-king","premise":1}]},{"id":"disobey-rawls","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"Civil disobedience in a nearly just society","claim":"Public, nonviolent law-breaking that appeals to a society's own principles of justice can be justified, and it strengthens rather than dissolves the rule of law.","premises":["Civil disobedience is a public, nonviolent, conscientious breach of law, done openly and with willingness to accept the punishment, in order to change a law or policy.","It appeals not to private conscience or religion but to the shared sense of justice that the majority itself professes.","Limited to clear and serious injustices, and used when normal political appeals have failed, it helps keep a nearly just society on course instead of threatening it."],"conclusion":"In a nearly just democracy, civil disobedience is sometimes justified.","plain":"A whistleblower who reports her company to the regulators isn't attacking the rules; she's calling on the rules everyone claims to follow.","author":"rawls","source":"John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §§55, 57 and 59","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-hobbes","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"disobey-king"}]},{"id":"disobey-sincerity","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"Conscience cuts both ways","claim":"Anyone sure that a law is seriously unjust could claim Rawls's permission, including people fighting for injustice.","premises":["Rawls's conditions depend on the disobedient judging that an injustice is clear and serious.","After the US Supreme Court ordered the end of school segregation in 1954, many officials and citizens sincerely believed this was a grave injustice, and openly defied the law.","A principle that permits King's disobedience seems to permit theirs too, unless someone other than the disobedient decides which injustices count."],"conclusion":"Rawls's conditions don't stop disobedience spreading to anyone who is sure they are right, so a strong presumption in favor of obeying democratic law remains.","plain":"Everyone who runs a red light thinks they had a good reason.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see SEP, 'Civil Disobedience' (Kimberley Brownlee); on the 'massive resistance' to Brown v. Board of Education (1954), see the Southern Manifesto (1956)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-rawls","premise":3}]},{"id":"disobey-fairness","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"Don't be a free rider","claim":"Since we all benefit from others obeying the law, fairness requires us to obey it too, even when we dislike a rule.","premises":["When people run a shared scheme by following rules, those who benefit owe it to the others to follow the rules too.","A legal system is such a scheme: you benefit from safe streets, working roads and from millions of others obeying laws they don't always like.","Breaking a law because you disagree with it takes the benefits of the scheme while refusing its burdens."],"conclusion":"We have a general duty to obey the law, even laws we think mistaken.","plain":"If everyone else waits in the queue, you don't get to skip it just because you think queues are silly.","author":"hart","source":"H.L.A. Hart, 'Are There Any Natural Rights?', Philosophical Review 64 (1955): 175–191; John Rawls, 'Legal Obligation and the Duty of Fair Play' (1964)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"disobey-simmons","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"Benefits you never chose","claim":"Fair play binds only those who accept a scheme's benefits, and most citizens never accept the state's benefits in that sense.","premises":["Fair play binds people who willingly and knowingly accept a scheme's benefits, not those on whom benefits are simply forced.","Most benefits of the state, like national defense or clean air, come to you whether you want them or not, so you never really accept them.","So fair play can't ground a general duty to obey every law."],"conclusion":"There is no general duty to obey the law just because it is the law, though many laws should be obeyed for their own sakes.","plain":"If neighbors set up a street loudspeaker and then tell you it's your turn to run it, enjoying the music through your window doesn't oblige you.","author":"simmons","source":"A. John Simmons, Moral Principles and Political Obligations (1979), ch. 5; the loudspeaker case is from Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), ch. 5","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-fairness","premise":2}]},{"id":"disobey-klosko","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"Goods everyone needs","claim":"For goods that everyone needs, like security, receiving them is enough to owe your share.","premises":["Some goods, like defense and public order, are needed for any decent life, whatever else a person wants.","For such 'presumptively beneficial' goods, it is fair to expect everyone who receives them to do their part, even if they never chose them.","The core work of the state is providing exactly these goods."],"conclusion":"Fair play does ground a duty to obey, at least the laws that provide essential goods.","plain":"You never signed up for the town's flood defenses, but when the river rises you'd be glad of them, and it's fair to ask you to help pay.","author":"anon","source":"George Klosko, 'Presumptive Benefit, Fairness, and Political Obligation', Philosophy & Public Affairs 16 (1987): 241–259; The Principle of Fairness and Political Obligation (1992)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-simmons","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"disobey-fairness"}]},{"id":"disobey-delmas","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"A duty to resist","claim":"The same reasons that tell us to support just laws tell us to resist unjust ones, sometimes even covertly.","premises":["Our duties to support a political order come from justice, fairness and solidarity with others.","An unjust law is not part of a fair scheme, so fairness and solidarity count against obeying it, not for it.","Sometimes resistance only works if it is secret and illegal, as with the Underground Railroad that helped enslaved people escape."],"conclusion":"Disobeying serious injustice is sometimes not just permitted but required, even when it isn't 'civil'.","plain":"If the club's rules say members must bully the new kid, fairness to the club is not a reason to join in.","author":"anon","source":"Candice Delmas, A Duty to Resist: When Disobedience Should Be Uncivil (2018)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-klosko","premise":3},{"type":"supports","target":"disobey-king"}]},{"id":"disobey-wolff","q":"disobey","side":"yes","title":"The duty of autonomy","claim":"No one can have a duty to obey a law simply because it is the law.","premises":["A responsible adult's basic duty is to be autonomous: to decide for herself what she ought to do.","Obeying a law simply because it is the law means doing what you are told, not what you have judged right.","So the duty of autonomy and a duty to obey the state as such cannot both hold."],"conclusion":"No law binds you merely because it is law; you should follow laws only where your own judgment agrees.","plain":"'Because I said so' may work on small children, but it isn't a reason for a grown-up.","author":"rp_wolff","source":"Robert Paul Wolff, In Defense of Anarchism (1970), ch. 1","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"disobey-raz","q":"disobey","side":"no","title":"Deferring can be wise","claim":"Following an authority is often the most rational thing an autonomous person can do.","premises":["Autonomy doesn't mean making every decision alone: it's often reasonable to defer to a doctor, a pilot or a map.","An authority is legitimate for you when you would act better on the reasons you already have by following its rules than by deciding each case yourself.","Many laws, such as traffic rules and tax rules, coordinate millions of people in ways no individual could, so following them is what a reasonable person would choose."],"conclusion":"Wolff's argument fails: autonomy and authority can fit together, at least for many laws. (Raz himself denies a fully general duty to obey every law.)","plain":"Driving on the agreed side of the road isn't giving up your judgment; it's using it.","author":"raz","source":"Joseph Raz, The Morality of Freedom (1986), chs. 2–3 (the 'normal justification thesis')","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"disobey-wolff","premise":2}]},{"id":"promise-practice","q":"promise","side":"no","title":"The point of a promise","claim":"A promise is meant to bind you even when breaking it would turn out better, so 'it would be better on the whole' is no excuse.","premises":["Promising is a practice with rules, and its whole point is to let others rely on you, even when keeping your word turns out to be inconvenient.","The rules of the practice do not allow 'breaking it would do more good' as a defense; that is exactly the kind of excuse the practice rules out.","Someone who breaks promises whenever a fresh calculation favors it hasn't understood what a promise is."],"conclusion":"You may not break a promise just because breaking it would have better results.","plain":"A promise that you'll keep 'unless something better comes up' is not a promise; it's a weather forecast.","author":"rawls","source":"John Rawls, 'Two Concepts of Rules', Philosophical Review 64 (1955): 3–32, §III","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"promise-kant","q":"promise","side":"no","title":"A policy that destroys itself","claim":"A policy of making promises and breaking them when convenient could not be followed by everyone, so it is wrong.","premises":["Act only on a policy you could will everyone to follow.","If everyone made promises they meant to break whenever it suited them, no one would believe a promise, and promising would become impossible.","So the policy of breaking promises for convenience undermines itself."],"conclusion":"Breaking your word for your own advantage is wrong.","plain":"If every IOU might be ignored, an IOU would be worth less than the paper it's written on.","author":"kant","source":"Immanuel Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), Ak. 4:402–403 and 4:422 (the false promise)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"supports","target":"promise-practice"}]},{"id":"promise-smart","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"The desert island promise","claim":"When breaking a promise does more good and can't damage anyone's trust, you should break it.","premises":["Alone on a desert island, you promise a dying man to give his fortune to a cause he cared about; no one else will ever know of the promise.","Once rescued, you find the money would do far more good elsewhere, for example by saving lives.","Keeping promises matters because breaking them usually damages trust; here no trust can be damaged, so only the good done is left to weigh."],"conclusion":"You should put the money where it does the most good.","plain":"A rule of thumb about keeping your word is a good rule of thumb, but it is still only a thumb.","author":"smart","source":"J.J.C. Smart, 'Extreme and Restricted Utilitarianism', Philosophical Quarterly 6 (1956): 344–354; 'An Outline of a System of Utilitarian Ethics', in Smart and Williams, Utilitarianism: For and Against (1973)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-practice","premise":2}]},{"id":"promise-ross-1001","q":"promise","side":"no","title":"A thousand and one","claim":"A promise creates an obligation to a particular person, which a slightly better outcome cannot cancel.","premises":["Suppose keeping a promise to A would produce 1,000 units of good, and breaking it to help B would produce 1,001.","On the 'only results matter' view, you ought to break the promise, but hardly anyone believes that.","The best explanation is that a promise gives the person you promised a special claim on you, not just one more source of good results."],"conclusion":"The good done by breaking a promise doesn't by itself justify breaking it; the person you promised has been wronged.","plain":"Swapping a promise for one extra crumb of good feels less like wise accounting and more like betrayal.","author":"ross","source":"W.D. Ross, The Right and the Good (1930), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-smart","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"promise-practice"}]},{"id":"promise-hare","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"Good habits, odd cases","claim":"Our strong feeling that promises must be kept is a valuable habit for real life, but it can't settle made-up cases.","premises":["The firm conviction that promises must be kept is a good disposition to have, because in daily life we can't trust our own quick calculations.","Cases like '1,000 versus 1,001' are artificial: in real life you never know the gains so precisely, and the cost to trust is never zero.","So the intuition is right for ordinary life but proves nothing about what is truly best in the imagined case."],"conclusion":"Ross's example doesn't refute the view that promises bind because of their effects.","plain":"A smoke alarm should go off at burnt toast; that doesn't mean every burnt slice is a fire.","author":"hare","source":"R.M. Hare, Moral Thinking: Its Levels, Method, and Point (1981), chs. 2–3","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-ross-1001","premise":2}]},{"id":"promise-ross-accident","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"Duties can be outweighed","claim":"Keeping promises is a real duty, but a stronger duty can override it.","premises":["The duty to keep promises is a 'prima facie' duty: it holds unless a weightier duty conflicts with it in the situation.","If I promised to meet a friend for some trivial purpose, I would be justified in breaking it to prevent a serious accident or help its victims.","Our considered convictions about such cases are the data of ethics, as perceptions are the data of science."],"conclusion":"Some promises should be broken, when keeping them would mean neglecting a stronger duty.","plain":"Nobody blames you for missing the movie you promised to see if you were pulling someone out of a canal.","author":"ross","source":"W.D. Ross, The Right and the Good (1930), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"promise-cephalus","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"The madman's weapon","claim":"Paying back what you promised is not always right.","premises":["Suppose you borrowed a friend's weapon and promised to return it whenever he asked.","He comes back out of his mind and demands it.","Everyone agrees you should not hand it over, so 'always give back what you owe' can't be what justice is."],"conclusion":"Keeping your word is not always the right thing to do.","plain":"You don't hand back the car keys you promised to return if your friend is too drunk to stand.","author":"plato","source":"Plato, Republic I, 331c; the same case appears in Cicero, De Officiis III.95","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"promise-conditions","q":"promise","side":"no","title":"Promises have built-in limits","claim":"In such cases the promise isn't broken; it never covered them in the first place.","premises":["Every promise is made against a background of shared understanding, which limits what it covers.","No one who lends a weapon means 'give it back even if I've gone mad'; the condition is understood, not stated.","So refusing to return it doesn't break the promise; it follows what was really promised."],"conclusion":"Properly understood, a genuine promise should always be kept; what looks like breaking one is recognizing its limits.","plain":"'I'll pick you up at eight' doesn't mean 'even if the road has been washed away'.","author":"anon","source":"Discussed in SEP, 'Promises and Agreements' (Allen Habib); compare Thomas Aquinas, Summa Theologiae II-II, q.110, a.3, ad 5","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-cephalus","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"promise-practice"}]},{"id":"promise-conditions-reply","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"Breaking by another name","claim":"The 'built-in limits' defense quietly concedes that promises may be set aside when enough is at stake.","premises":["If every promise silently includes 'unless something more important comes up', then Ross's view is true under another name.","To decide which unstated conditions a promise contains, we must ask which reasons are weighty enough to override it.","That is exactly the weighing of reasons the 'no' side said promises rule out."],"conclusion":"The defense gives up the debate: promises can rightly be set aside.","plain":"Calling it 'an unwritten exception' instead of 'breaking my word' changes the label, not the act.","author":"anon","source":"Reply drawing on W.D. Ross, The Right and the Good (1930), ch. 2; see SEP, 'Promises and Agreements' (Allen Habib)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-conditions","premise":3}]},{"id":"promise-hobbes-fear","q":"promise","side":"no","title":"Even a promise made in fear","claim":"A promise binds even when you made it out of fear, as when you promise a robber ransom for your life.","premises":["If I promise a robber money in return for my life, I have received what I bargained for: my life.","Fear is the motive behind many binding agreements, such as the peace treaties that end wars; if fear cancelled promises, they would all be void.","Only a law or an authority can release me from such a promise, not my own later regret."],"conclusion":"Even a promise extracted by fear must be kept, unless the law releases you from it.","plain":"If promises made under pressure didn't count, every surrender in history could be taken back the next morning.","author":"hobbes","source":"Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 14","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"promise-cicero-pirates","q":"promise","side":"yes","title":"No promises to pirates","claim":"A promise wrung from you by a criminal's threat need not be kept.","premises":["A pirate is not a lawful enemy but the common enemy of all, with whom no pledge of good faith is shared.","Promising works among people who live under shared rules of good faith; a promise extracted by a criminal's threat is outside that.","Even Hobbes admits that the law may release you from a promise made to a thief, which shows the promise has no force of its own."],"conclusion":"If you fail to pay a ransom you promised to pirates, you have done no wrong.","plain":"A 'deal' with a mugger is not the kind of deal honor asks you to keep.","author":"cicero","source":"Cicero, De Officiis (On Duties, 44 BCE), III.107; Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651), ch. 14","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"promise-hobbes-fear","premise":2}]},{"id":"aiart-intentional","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"Judge the work, not the maker","claim":"Whether something is good art depends on what is in the work, not on what went on in its maker's mind.","premises":["We judge a poem or a painting by what we find in it; the maker's private intentions are usually unavailable, and are not the standard of success anyway.","An AI-made image or poem can have all the features we prize in art: striking form, meanings readers discover, emotional power.","So nothing that matters to the work as art need be missing."],"conclusion":"AI-made works can be real art.","plain":"A poem found in an old drawer with no author's name can still make you cry.","author":"wimsatt","source":"W.K. Wimsatt and Monroe Beardsley, 'The Intentional Fallacy', Sewanee Review 54 (1946): 468–488 (applied here to AI)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"aiart-collingwood","q":"aiart","side":"no","title":"Art is expression","claim":"Art proper is a person expressing and clarifying their own emotions, so a system with no emotions can only produce craft.","premises":["Making a pleasing product to order, by a known technique, is craft; art proper is a person working out and expressing what they feel.","An AI system that predicts likely images or words from patterns in its training data has no feelings to express.","So whatever it produces is at most craft or 'amusement', however beautiful."],"conclusion":"AI cannot make art proper.","plain":"A greeting-card verse can rhyme perfectly and still say nothing anyone felt.","author":"collingwood","source":"R.G. Collingwood, The Principles of Art (1938), chs. 2 and 6 (applied here to AI)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-intentional","premise":3}]},{"id":"aiart-collingwood-reply","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"That rules out too much","claim":"If art required expressing the maker's own emotions, much great human art would not count either.","premises":["On Collingwood's test, whether a work is art proper depends on whether its maker was expressing their own feelings, which we usually can't know: many great portraits, church paintings and film scores were made to order, with a planned effect.","We don't wait to learn what the maker felt before treating such works as great art.","So expressing the maker's own emotion can't be what makes something art."],"conclusion":"Having no emotions doesn't stop an AI's output from being art.","plain":"Michelangelo painted the Sistine ceiling because the Pope ordered him to, and wrote a poem complaining about it.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection to expression theories; see SEP, 'Collingwood's Aesthetics' (Gary Kemp)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-collingwood","premise":3}]},{"id":"aiart-chiang","q":"aiart","side":"no","title":"Art is made of choices","claim":"Art comes from making a great many choices, and a prompt makes very few.","premises":["Art is something that results from making a lot of choices: writing a ten-thousand-word story involves something like ten thousand choices.","A short prompt makes very few choices; the program fills in the rest with something like an average of the choices others have made.","So the result carries little of anyone's intention, and tends toward the bland."],"conclusion":"Generative AI isn't going to make art.","plain":"Ordering 'a sad song about rain' is not the same as writing one.","author":"chiang","source":"Ted Chiang, 'Why A.I. Isn't Going to Make Art', The New Yorker (31 August 2024)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"aiart-camera","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"They said that about photography","claim":"Photography was once dismissed as mechanical copying, yet it became an art; AI can be used the same way.","premises":["When photography arrived, critics such as Baudelaire attacked it as a mechanical copy of nature and an enemy of art.","A photo can take a single click, yet choices of subject, moment, framing and selection made photography an art.","Artists working with AI likewise try many prompts, choose among many results and edit them, so how many choices go into the work depends on the user, not the tool."],"conclusion":"Works made with AI can be real art, as photographs are.","plain":"A camera does most of the work too, and nobody says there are no great photographers.","author":"hertzmann","source":"Aaron Hertzmann, 'Can Computers Create Art?', Arts 7 (2018): 18; Charles Baudelaire, 'The Salon of 1859', part II ('The Modern Public and Photography')","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-chiang","premise":2}]},{"id":"aiart-commission","q":"aiart","side":"no","title":"Prompting is commissioning","claim":"Typing prompts and picking results is more like commissioning a picture than making one.","premises":["A patron who describes what she wants and picks the best of several sketches is not the artist; the painter is.","With an AI tool, the user says what to depict, but the program decides how: the composition, the brushwork, nearly every detail.","A photographer controls the camera, the scene and the moment; the AI user controls far less, so the photography comparison fails."],"conclusion":"Prompting doesn't make you the artist, and the machine isn't one either.","plain":"Telling a tattoo artist 'a dragon, but cooler' doesn't make you the tattoo artist.","author":"anon","source":"US Copyright Office, 'Copyright Registration Guidance: Works Containing Material Generated by Artificial Intelligence', 88 Fed. Reg. 16190 (16 March 2023), likening prompts to instructions to a commissioned artist","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-camera","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"aiart-chiang"}]},{"id":"aiart-kelly","q":"aiart","side":"no","title":"Creativity is social","claim":"Being creative means changing how a community understands an art form, and standing behind the work; an AI does neither.","premises":["Producing something new is not enough for creativity: a toddler hitting new combinations of piano keys is not composing.","Creative work changes how a community understands its art, as Schoenberg's twelve-tone method did, and the artist must be able to stand behind it as worth doing.","AI systems that learn a style from data, like programs that write new 'Bach' chorales, recombine past patterns and stand behind nothing."],"conclusion":"An AI can't be an artist, though it can be an artist's tool.","plain":"A machine that writes a perfect fake Beatles song has learned the Beatles; it hasn't done what the Beatles did.","author":"kelly_sd","source":"Sean Dorrance Kelly, 'A Philosopher Argues That an AI Can't Be an Artist', MIT Technology Review (21 February 2019)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"aiart-boden","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"Three kinds of creativity","claim":"Most human creativity is the kind computers can already do.","premises":["Creativity means coming up with ideas that are new, surprising and valuable, and it comes in three kinds: combining familiar ideas, exploring the possibilities of a style, and transforming the style itself.","Most human artists do the first two kinds; very few ever transform their art form.","Computer programs already combine ideas and explore styles in ways that are new, surprising and valuable."],"conclusion":"Computers can be creative in the ways most human artists are.","plain":"When the program AlphaGo played its famous 'move 37' in 2016, top players first thought it was a mistake, then called it beautiful.","author":"anon","source":"Margaret Boden, The Creative Mind: Myths and Mechanisms (1990; 2nd ed. 2004)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-kelly","premise":3}]},{"id":"aiart-value","q":"aiart","side":"no","title":"Valuable to whom?","claim":"With AI art, humans do the valuing: they choose which outputs are good, so the creative judgment is theirs.","premises":["Boden's test of creativity includes value, and valuing has to be done by someone.","With AI art, people decide which outputs are worth keeping, discarding the rest.","So the judgment that turns output into art, rather than noise, is human."],"conclusion":"The machine generates; people create.","plain":"A slot machine that sometimes lines up three cherries isn't a designer; you're the one who notices the cherries.","author":"anon","source":"Objection discussed in Simon Colton, 'Creativity Versus the Perception of Creativity in Computational Systems', AAAI Spring Symposium on Creative Intelligent Systems (2008)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-boden","premise":3}]},{"id":"aiart-colton","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"Software that judges its own work","claim":"A program can be built to appreciate its own work, not just to produce it.","premises":["We call someone creative when they show skill, appreciation and imagination: a 'creative tripod'.","Programs can be built to judge their own output against their aims, reject failures and explain their choices; Colton designed his program The Painting Fool to do this.","If the program does the judging as well as the making, the objection that only humans do the valuing loses its grip."],"conclusion":"Machines can, in principle, be creative artists rather than mere tools.","plain":"An artist who throws away nine sketches and keeps the tenth is showing taste; a program can be built to do the same.","author":"colton","source":"Simon Colton, 'Creativity Versus the Perception of Creativity in Computational Systems' (2008); 'The Painting Fool: Stories from Building an Automated Painter', in McCormack and d'Inverno (eds.), Computers and Creativity (2012)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-value","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"aiart-boden"}]},{"id":"aiart-whose","q":"aiart","side":"neutral","title":"Art is made by social agents","claim":"AI-assisted works can be art, but the artists are the people using the AI, because art is a social activity.","premises":["Art is made by social agents: people who respond to an audience and to other artists, and who are held responsible for what they make.","Current AI systems are not social agents; they are tools, like cameras or paint programs, however clever.","So when an AI-assisted work is art, the credit belongs to the humans who used the tool."],"conclusion":"There is real art made with AI, but no AI artists, at least not yet.","plain":"Nobody gives the Oscar to the camera.","author":"hertzmann","source":"Aaron Hertzmann, 'Can Computers Create Art?', Arts 7 (2018): 18","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-colton","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"aiart-belamy","premise":3}]},{"id":"aiart-belamy","q":"aiart","side":"yes","title":"The art world has spoken","claim":"Something is art when the art world presents it for appreciation, and AI works are already being presented that way.","premises":["Something is a work of art when it is an artifact that has been presented, by people acting on behalf of the art world, as a candidate for appreciation.","AI-generated works are now shown in galleries and sold by major auction houses: in 2018 Christie's sold the AI-made 'Portrait of Edmond de Belamy' for $432,500.","So AI works already have the status that makes something art."],"conclusion":"AI art is real art.","plain":"Duchamp's urinal became art by being submitted to an art exhibition; AI pictures are getting the same treatment.","author":"dickie","source":"George Dickie, Art and the Aesthetic: An Institutional Analysis (1974), ch. 1; Christie's, New York, sale of 25 October 2018","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"children-asymmetry","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"The asymmetry","claim":"Never existing has an advantage over existing and no disadvantage, so coming into existence is always a harm.","premises":["The presence of pain is bad, and the presence of pleasure is good.","The absence of pain is good, even if there is no one to enjoy that good; but the absence of pleasure is not bad unless there is someone who is deprived of it.","So compared with existing, never existing has an advantage (no pain, which is good) and no disadvantage (the missing pleasures are bad for no one)."],"conclusion":"Coming into existence is always a harm, so it is wrong to have children.","plain":"You can't miss a party you were never invited to, but you can certainly be burned at one.","author":"benatar","source":"David Benatar, Better Never to Have Been: The Harm of Coming into Existence (2006), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"children-asymmetry-reply","q":"children","side":"no","title":"Good for no one?","claim":"If absent pain counts as good when there's no one to benefit, absent pleasure should count as bad; Benatar's pairing is lopsided.","premises":["Benatar says the absence of pain is 'good' even though there is no one for whom it is good.","By the same reasoning, the absence of pleasure should count as 'bad', or else neither absence should count at all.","Treated evenly, the two sides no longer show that never existing is always better."],"conclusion":"The asymmetry argument fails.","plain":"If an empty room gets credit for containing no toothache, it should also lose credit for containing no laughter.","author":"anon","source":"David DeGrazia, 'Is It Wrong to Impose the Harms of Human Life? A Reply to Benatar', Theoretical Medicine and Bioethics 31 (2010): 317–331; Ben Bradley, 'Benatar and the Logic of Betterness', Journal of Ethics and Social Philosophy (2010)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-asymmetry","premise":2}]},{"id":"children-asymmetry-defense","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"We already believe it","claim":"The asymmetry isn't a trick; it explains moral views almost everyone already holds.","premises":["Most people think there is a strong duty not to create a child who will suffer terribly, but no duty to create a child just because it would be happy.","We can regret, for a miserable child's own sake, that it was born; we never grieve for the happy people who were never born on an uninhabited island.","The asymmetry between absent pain and absent pleasure is the best explanation of these common views."],"conclusion":"The asymmetry is part of ordinary morality, so the argument stands.","plain":"No one feels sorry for the millions of happy people who don't exist on Mars.","author":"benatar","source":"David Benatar, Better Never to Have Been (2006), ch. 2","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-asymmetry-reply","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"children-asymmetry"}]},{"id":"children-narveson","q":"children","side":"no","title":"Making people happy, not happy people","claim":"Our duties concern people who exist or will exist, which explains the common views without making existence a harm.","premises":["We are in favor of making people happy, but neutral about making happy people: duties are owed to people, not to empty possibilities.","That explains why there is no duty to create a happy child: no one is wronged if they are never born.","It also explains the duty not to create a miserable child, since that child would exist and suffer, without implying that every life is a harm."],"conclusion":"The common views Benatar relies on don't need his asymmetry, so they give no support to anti-natalism.","plain":"You don't owe anyone a birthday party before they're born, but you do owe the guests you invite a decent party.","author":"narveson","source":"Jan Narveson, 'Moral Problems of Population', The Monist 57 (1973): 62–86","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-asymmetry-defense","premise":3}]},{"id":"children-goods","q":"children","side":"no","title":"A life worth living","claim":"Creating a person whose life will be well worth living does them no wrong and gives the world a new good.","premises":["Most people, asked whether their lives are worth living, say yes, and many lives contain great goods: love, friendship, discovery, joy.","Creating someone whose life is well worth living gives them something they will be glad to have.","Every human life brings some pain, but so does almost everything worth doing."],"conclusion":"Having children can be good, both for the child and for the world.","plain":"A hike has blisters and rain, and people still come home saying it was the best day of their year.","author":"anon","source":"Common view; defended against Benatar in David Benatar and David Wasserman, Debating Procreation: Is It Wrong to Reproduce? (2015), Part II (Wasserman)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"children-pollyanna","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"We overrate our lives","claim":"People judge their lives far better than they are, so 'most people are glad to be alive' proves little.","premises":["Well-documented biases make people rate their lives too highly: we remember the good more than the bad, adapt to hardship, and compare ourselves with those worse off.","Looked at honestly, every life contains a great deal of bad: hunger, tiredness, frustration, illness, grief, and finally death.","So even lives that seem good by ordinary standards are much worse than we think."],"conclusion":"Bringing anyone into existence imposes serious harm.","plain":"Holiday photos show the sunset, not the hours in the airport queue.","author":"benatar","source":"David Benatar, Better Never to Have Been (2006), ch. 3 (the 'Pollyanna principle')","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-goods","premise":1}]},{"id":"children-pollyanna-reply","q":"children","side":"no","title":"Measured against what?","claim":"Benatar measures lives against an impossible ideal; against realistic standards, many lives are well worth living.","premises":["How good a life is for the person living it depends partly on how they experience and value it, not only on an outsider's tally.","Benatar counts every hunger pang, every frustration and the very fact of ageing as a cost, a standard no possible human life could meet.","Measured against realistic standards, many lives are clearly worth living."],"conclusion":"The quality-of-life argument doesn't show that existence is always a serious harm.","plain":"If a meal counts as bad because you'll be hungry again tomorrow, no meal was ever good.","author":"anon","source":"David Benatar and David Wasserman, Debating Procreation: Is It Wrong to Reproduce? (2015), Part II (Wasserman); DeGrazia (2010)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-pollyanna","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"children-goods"}]},{"id":"children-consent","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"No one agreed to be born","claim":"Creating a person imposes serious burdens on someone who cannot consent, merely to give them benefits they didn't need.","premises":["It is generally wrong to impose serious harm on someone without their consent just to give them a benefit, even a larger one.","Creating a person imposes the risks and burdens of life on someone who cannot consent.","Life's goods are 'pure benefits': they don't save the child from any harm they would otherwise have suffered."],"conclusion":"Having children is morally troubling: parents impose great burdens without consent. (Shiffrin herself draws a weaker conclusion: that parents bear a heavy responsibility for these harms.)","plain":"A millionaire who drops gold bars from a plane has still wronged the person whose arm one breaks, even if the gold makes them rich.","author":"anon","source":"Seana Shiffrin, 'Wrongful Life, Procreative Responsibility, and the Significance of Harm', Legal Theory 5 (1999): 117–148","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"children-hypothetical-consent","q":"children","side":"no","title":"They would have agreed","claim":"When someone can't consent, we may act on what they would want, and almost everyone is glad to have been born.","premises":["When someone can't consent, we rightly act on what they would most likely want, as when doctors operate on an unconscious patient.","The great majority of people are glad to be alive and would choose to have been born.","So parents can reasonably presume their child's consent."],"conclusion":"The consent argument doesn't show that having children is wrong.","plain":"You can't ask a newborn whether it wants a name, but you're fairly sure it would want one.","author":"anon","source":"The 'hypothetical consent' reply; stated and criticized in Asheel Singh, 'The Hypothetical Consent Objection to Anti-Natalism', Ethical Theory and Moral Practice 21 (2018)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-consent","premise":2}]},{"id":"children-consent-rejoinder","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"Rescue isn't a gift","claim":"We presume consent to prevent a harm, not to hand out benefits, and a never-born person faces no harm.","premises":["We presume an unconscious patient's consent because surgery saves them from a greater harm.","A person who is never created faces no harm at all from not being created.","Presumed consent can justify harming someone to prevent something worse, but not to give them benefits they didn't need."],"conclusion":"Hypothetical consent can't license having children.","plain":"Pulling a stranger out of a fire without asking is fine; giving them a surprise tattoo because they'd probably like it is not.","author":"anon","source":"Seana Shiffrin, 'Wrongful Life, Procreative Responsibility, and the Significance of Harm', Legal Theory 5 (1999): 117–148","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"children-hypothetical-consent","premise":1},{"type":"replies","target":"children-consent"}]},{"id":"children-misanthropic","q":"children","side":"yes","title":"The harm we do","claim":"Each new human is very likely to cause great harm to others, which is a strong reason not to create one.","premises":["Humans cause vast suffering: wars, cruelty, pollution, and the deaths of tens of billions of farmed animals every year.","Each new person will very probably add to that harm over their lifetime.","There is a strong presumption against creating a being that is very likely to cause great harm."],"conclusion":"There is a strong presumption against having children.","plain":"If you knew a new factory would poison the river, you'd think hard before building it.","author":"benatar","source":"David Benatar and David Wasserman, Debating Procreation: Is It Wrong to Reproduce? (2015), Part I (Benatar, the 'misanthropic argument')","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"children-mbiti","q":"children","side":"no","title":"Keeping the chain alive","claim":"In many traditions, having children is not a harm done to the child but a duty that keeps a community, living and departed, in being.","premises":["A person exists as part of a community that stretches from the departed through the living to those yet to be born.","Children continue that community and keep the departed in living memory; in traditional African thought, having them is a duty to one's people, not a private choice.","A newborn enters that web of relationships as a member, receiving life as something given and handed on."],"conclusion":"Having children is not wrong; in many traditions it is a duty.","plain":"A relay runner who refuses to pass on the baton doesn't spare anyone; the race just ends.","author":"mbiti","source":"John S. Mbiti, African Religions and Philosophy (1969), the chapter 'Marriage and procreation'","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"torture-ticking","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"The ticking bomb","claim":"When torturing one guilty person is the only way to prevent far greater suffering, it is justified.","premises":["What matters morally is how much suffering is caused or prevented.","If a person holds information that would save many people from horrible deaths and refuses to give it, the pain needed to make him talk is far less than the suffering it prevents.","We already accept causing pain through punishment in order to prevent future harm."],"conclusion":"In such a case, torture is justified.","plain":"If hurting one guilty man for an hour stops a thousand innocent people dying, the sums aren't close.","author":"bentham","source":"Jeremy Bentham's manuscripts on torture, published in W.L. Twining and P.E. Twining, 'Bentham on Torture', Northern Ireland Legal Quarterly 24 (1973)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"torture-dreamland","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"Torture in dreamland","claim":"The ticking bomb story stacks up certainties that real interrogators never have.","premises":["The story assumes officials know there is a bomb, know they have the person who knows where it is, know torture will make him tell the truth in time, and know nothing else would work.","Real interrogators almost never know all of this; in practice people are tortured who know nothing, or give false information to make the pain stop. A US Senate committee's 2014 study concluded that the CIA's harsh interrogation methods were not an effective way of getting intelligence.","Rules must be judged for the real world they will be used in; as Shue puts it, artificial cases make bad ethics."],"conclusion":"The ticking bomb gives no reason to allow torture in the real world.","plain":"A plan that only works if every guess is right is a plan for a world we don't live in.","author":"anon","source":"Henry Shue, 'Torture', Philosophy & Public Affairs 7 (1978): 124–143, at p. 141; 'Torture in Dreamland: Disposing of the Ticking Bomb', Case Western Reserve Journal of International Law 37 (2006); US Senate Select Committee on Intelligence, Committee Study of the CIA's Detention and Interrogation Program (2014), Findings and Conclusions, no. 1","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-ticking","premise":2}]},{"id":"torture-mcmahan","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"In principle, not in law","claim":"Torture can be justified in principle, even if the law should still ban it.","premises":["'Can torture ever be justified?' is a question of principle, and an imagined case is a fair test of a principle, as in the trolley problem.","If torture would be right in the idealized case, the absolute moral ban is false, however rare such cases are.","Whether the law should allow torture is a separate question; since officials would abuse any exception, the law can rightly forbid it absolutely."],"conclusion":"Torture can be morally justified in rare cases, even though it should stay illegal.","plain":"Speed limits should have no 'unless it's urgent' clause, even though racing a dying friend to hospital might be right.","author":"mcmahan","source":"Jeff McMahan, 'Torture in Principle and in Practice', Public Affairs Quarterly 22 (2008): 111–128","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-dreamland","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"torture-ticking"}]},{"id":"torture-gafgen","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"The closest real case","claim":"Even the strongest real-life case looked nothing like the textbook one, which shows why principle and practice can't be split.","premises":["In 2002 Frankfurt police believed a kidnapped eleven-year-old boy might still be alive, and threatened the kidnapper with severe pain unless he said where the boy was.","The kidnapper talked, but the boy had been dead for days: the 'bomb' had never been ticking.","Officials always act under this kind of uncertainty, so a principle that allows torture 'in the clear case' will be used in unclear ones; the European Court of Human Rights ruled that even the threat broke the absolute ban."],"conclusion":"A moral permission for torture can't be kept apart from its abuse in practice; the ban should be absolute.","plain":"In the textbook, the clock is ticking and you have the right man; in real life, you rarely know either.","author":"anon","source":"Gäfgen v. Germany, European Court of Human Rights (Grand Chamber), application no. 22978/05, judgment of 1 June 2010","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-mcmahan","premise":3},{"type":"supports","target":"torture-dreamland"}]},{"id":"torture-absolute","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"An assault on the defenseless","claim":"Torture is always wrong, because it is an attack on someone who is completely helpless.","premises":["Even in war, the rules allow attacking only those who can still fight back; once a soldier surrenders, he may not be harmed.","A torture victim is completely in the torturer's power: tied down and defenseless, no longer a threat in the ordinary sense.","Some protections must be absolute, or they protect no one: once exceptions are allowed when 'enough' is at stake, every torturer will claim enough is at stake."],"conclusion":"Torture is always wrong. As the UN Convention Against Torture says, no exceptional circumstances whatsoever can justify it.","plain":"Hitting someone in a fair fight and hitting someone tied to a chair are different acts.","author":"anon","source":"Henry Shue, 'Torture', Philosophy & Public Affairs 7 (1978): 124–143 (on assault upon the defenseless); UN Convention Against Torture (1984), art. 2(2)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"torture-moore","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"When the heavens really fall","claim":"Torture is normally forbidden even for good results, but past a threshold of catastrophe the ban gives way.","premises":["Some acts, like torturing the innocent, are wrong even when they would produce somewhat better results; numbers alone don't license them.","But it's fanatical to hold that no catastrophe, however vast, could ever justify them: 'let justice be done though the heavens fall' is not wisdom when the heavens really are falling.","When the harm prevented is great enough, such as thousands of deaths, the threshold is crossed, especially when the person tortured is the guilty attacker."],"conclusion":"Torture can be justified in sufficiently extreme cases.","plain":"A rule against breaking down doors is a good rule, until the house is on fire with children inside.","author":"michael_moore","source":"Michael S. Moore, 'Torture and the Balance of Evils', Israel Law Review 23 (1989); see SEP, 'Deontological Ethics' (Larry Alexander and Michael Moore), on threshold deontology","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-absolute","premise":3}]},{"id":"torture-threshold","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"Where is the line?","claim":"A threshold view can't say where its threshold lies, and under pressure every official will think his case has crossed it.","premises":["Threshold views say torture is forbidden until the stakes pass some line, but not where: a thousand lives? a hundred? one child?","Officials under pressure will nearly always believe that their case crosses the line.","An absolute rule is the only line that holds under that pressure."],"conclusion":"Even if a threshold exists in theory, we must treat the ban on torture as absolute.","plain":"A sign saying 'No entry, unless it's really important' keeps nobody out.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see SEP, 'Deontological Ethics' (Larry Alexander and Michael Moore), on threshold deontology","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-moore","premise":3}]},{"id":"torture-vague","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"Vague lines are still lines","claim":"Not knowing exactly where a line falls doesn't show there is no line.","premises":["Many moral and legal lines are vague: when someone becomes an adult, or how much force is reasonable in self-defense.","We don't conclude that there is no difference between a child and an adult, or that self-defense is never allowed.","The hard question is where the threshold lies, not whether one exists; and the worry about abuse is a reason for strict rules, not for denying the moral truth."],"conclusion":"The threshold view survives: torture can still be justified in truly extreme cases.","plain":"You can't say which grain of sand turns a pile into a heap, but you can tell a heap from a single grain.","author":"anon","source":"Reply in the spirit of Michael S. Moore, 'Torture and the Balance of Evils', Israel Law Review 23 (1989); see SEP, 'Deontological Ethics' (Alexander and Moore)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-threshold","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"torture-moore"}]},{"id":"torture-sussman","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"Turning a person against himself","claim":"Torture is a special kind of wrong, not just a large amount of pain, because it makes the victim's own body and feelings into weapons against him.","premises":["Torture doesn't simply hurt; it uses the victim's own pain, fear and bodily reactions to break his will.","It forces him into betraying himself, so that he experiences himself as complicit in his own violation.","This attack on a person's very agency is a distinctive wrong, which can't simply be weighed against other harms."],"conclusion":"Torture is especially wrong, in a way that the 'balance of suffering' argument leaves out.","plain":"Torture isn't just being hit; it's being made to feel that your own body has joined the other side.","author":"anon","source":"David Sussman, 'What's Wrong with Torture?', Philosophy & Public Affairs 33 (2005): 1–33","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"torture-steinhoff","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"If you may shoot him...","claim":"If police may kill a terrorist to stop his attack, it's hard to see why they may not hurt him to stop it.","premises":["Police may shoot a terrorist who is about to set off a bomb, to protect the innocent.","A terrorist who has planted a bomb and refuses to reveal it is still carrying out his attack; he is an ongoing threat, not a helpless bystander.","Killing destroys a person's agency completely; if it is allowed in defense, the special wrong of torture can't make torture always forbidden."],"conclusion":"Torturing a guilty ticking-bomb terrorist can be justified as a form of defense.","plain":"If you may stop a burglar with a punch, it's strange to say you may stop him with a bullet but not a twisted arm.","author":"anon","source":"Uwe Steinhoff, 'Torture: The Case for Dirty Harry and against Alan Dershowitz', Journal of Applied Philosophy 23 (2006)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-sussman","premise":3},{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-absolute","premise":2}]},{"id":"torture-dershowitz","q":"torture","side":"yes","title":"Torture warrants","claim":"If officials will torture in a real emergency anyway, it's better that a judge must approve it openly.","premises":["In a real ticking-bomb case, officials will use torture whatever the law says.","Torture done secretly and off the books is more likely to be abused and to spread.","Requiring a judge's warrant in each case would make torture visible, rare and accountable."],"conclusion":"If torture is used at all, it should be controlled by 'torture warrants' rather than hidden.","plain":"If people are going to drive fast in emergencies anyway, better ambulances with sirens than secret racing.","author":"anon","source":"Alan Dershowitz, Why Terrorism Works (2002), ch. 4","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"torture-luban","q":"torture","side":"no","title":"Torture needs an institution","claim":"Legal torture would require trained torturers and an apparatus that would not stay limited to ticking bombs.","premises":["Torture that 'works' needs trained interrogators, doctors to keep victims alive and lawyers to approve methods: an institution, not a one-off act.","Institutions, once built, look for uses, and limits drift outward; harsh methods approved for a few prisoners after 2001 spread to places like Abu Ghraib.","So warrants would not keep torture rare; they would make it routine."],"conclusion":"Legalizing torture, even only for ticking bombs, is too dangerous.","plain":"Once you've built a torture chamber and trained the staff, it won't sit empty waiting for the perfect case.","author":"anon","source":"David Luban, 'Liberalism, Torture, and the Ticking Bomb', Virginia Law Review 91 (2005): 1425–1461","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"torture-dershowitz","premise":3}]},{"id":"patriotism-macintyre","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"Morality starts at home","claim":"We learn morality within particular communities, so loyalty to one's own nation is a central virtue.","premises":["We learn morality not as abstract rules for everyone but within a particular community, with its history, practices and shared goods.","So my moral life depends on the flourishing of my community, and I owe it a loyalty I don't owe to every other community.","Patriotism is this loyalty to one's own nation and its shared project, not a belief that it is better than others."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is a virtue, and on this view a central one.","plain":"A child learns kindness from a particular family, not from a rulebook written for all families everywhere.","author":"macintyre","source":"Alasdair MacIntyre, 'Is Patriotism a Virtue?', Lindley Lecture, University of Kansas (1984)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"patriotism-nussbaum","q":"patriotism","side":"no","title":"Citizen of the world","claim":"Our first loyalty should be to humanity as a whole, not to the accident of the country we were born in.","premises":["Where you were born is an accident; it doesn't change the worth of the people on either side of a border.","Our first allegiance should be to the worldwide community of human beings, as Diogenes meant when he called himself a 'citizen of the world'.","Teaching children that their own nation's people matter most invites them to shrug at suffering abroad."],"conclusion":"Patriotism, as a basic loyalty, is morally dangerous; we should be cosmopolitans first.","plain":"A child born a mile across the border is just as much a child.","author":"nussbaum","source":"Martha Nussbaum, 'Patriotism and Cosmopolitanism', Boston Review 19 (October/November 1994); Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers VI.63","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"patriotism-partiality","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"If family, why not country?","claim":"Loving your country more than others is no more a prejudice than loving your family more than strangers.","premises":["Nobody thinks it's a prejudice to love your own parents more than other people's parents, or to put your friend first.","Special attachments to particular people and places are part of a good human life; someone attached to nothing in particular would be cold, not admirably fair.","A country can be one such attachment: a shared home, language, history and political project."],"conclusion":"Patriotic love is a natural and good form of partiality, not a bias.","plain":"Cheering for your little sister at her school play isn't unfair to the other kids.","author":"david_miller","source":"David Miller, On Nationality (1995), ch. 3; compare Bernard Williams, 'Persons, Character and Morality', in Moral Luck (1981)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-nussbaum","premise":2}]},{"id":"patriotism-gomberg","q":"patriotism","side":"no","title":"Patriotism is like racism","claim":"Preferring people because they share your nation is as arbitrary as preferring them because they share your race.","premises":["Racism is preferring people because they share your race, an accident of birth.","Patriotism is preferring people because they share your nation, also an accident of birth.","Whatever makes the first a prejudice makes the second one too."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is not a virtue but a bias.","plain":"'They're one of us' is the same thought whether 'us' means a race or a nation.","author":"anon","source":"Paul Gomberg, 'Patriotism Is Like Racism', Ethics 101 (1990): 144–150","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-partiality","premise":3}]},{"id":"patriotism-gomberg-reply","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"Citizens share a project","claim":"Fellow citizens are bound by real shared institutions, which a race is not.","premises":["Fellow citizens make laws together, pay into shared systems and are jointly responsible for what their government does.","A race involves no such shared project; it is a category, not a relationship.","Special duties can arise from shared cooperative projects, as among teammates or coworkers."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is not like racism; it can be a fitting loyalty to a real community.","plain":"Owing more to your teammates than to random strangers isn't racism; it's being on a team.","author":"anon","source":"Standard reply; see Andrew Mason, 'Special Obligations to Compatriots', Ethics 107 (1997): 427–447, and SEP, 'Patriotism' (Igor Primoratz)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-gomberg","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"patriotism-partiality"}]},{"id":"patriotism-tolstoy","q":"patriotism","side":"no","title":"The feeling that makes war","claim":"Patriotism is the feeling governments use to make ordinary people kill each other.","premises":["Patriotism means preferring one's own state and people above all others.","Governments use this feeling to raise armies and persuade ordinary people to kill people of other nations with whom they have no quarrel.","A feeling that in practice makes war possible cannot be a virtue."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is a harmful feeling that humanity should outgrow.","plain":"Two farmers on either side of a border have no quarrel until someone hands them flags and rifles.","author":"tolstoy","source":"Leo Tolstoy, 'Christianity and Patriotism' (1894); 'Patriotism, or Peace?' (1896)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"patriotism-orwell","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"Patriotism is not nationalism","claim":"Tolstoy's charges hit nationalism, the hunger for national power, not patriotism, the love of a place and a way of life.","premises":["Patriotism is devotion to a particular place and way of life, which you believe is the best in the world but have no wish to force on other people; it is defensive.","Nationalism is the desire to win power and prestige for your nation over others.","Wars of conquest are driven by nationalism, not by patriotism in this sense."],"conclusion":"Patriotism, properly understood, is not the cause of war, and can be a virtue.","plain":"Loving your own garden is one thing; wanting to take over the neighbors' is another.","author":"anon","source":"George Orwell, 'Notes on Nationalism', Polemic (October 1945)","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-tolstoy","premise":1}]},{"id":"patriotism-keller","q":"patriotism","side":"no","title":"Patriotism as bad faith","claim":"Patriots love their country because it is theirs, which tempts them to believe flattering things about it regardless of the evidence.","premises":["A patriot loves her country because it is hers, and wants to believe it is good.","So she is disposed to believe flattering things about her country's history and conduct, not because of the evidence but because it is hers.","Holding beliefs you would drop if you looked at the evidence honestly is bad faith, a kind of self-deception and a vice."],"conclusion":"Patriotism tends to corrupt judgment, so it is a vice, not a virtue.","plain":"Every country's history textbooks somehow find that it was mostly in the right.","author":"anon","source":"Simon Keller, 'Patriotism as Bad Faith', Ethics 115 (2005): 563–592","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-orwell","premise":1}]},{"id":"patriotism-critical","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"Love that tells the truth","claim":"Patriotism can take the form of holding one's country to its own best ideals, which is the opposite of bad faith.","premises":["Loving someone doesn't require thinking they are flawless; often it means holding them to their best.","In his 1852 speech 'What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?', Frederick Douglass praised America's founding ideals and, in their name, fiercely condemned American slavery.","So patriotism can show itself in demanding honesty about one's country."],"conclusion":"Patriotism need not corrupt judgment; at its best it sharpens it.","plain":"The friend who tells you the truth about yourself may love you more than the one who only flatters.","author":"anon","source":"Frederick Douglass, 'What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?', speech at Rochester, New York (5 July 1852); on 'critical' patriotism, see SEP, 'Patriotism' (Igor Primoratz)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-keller","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"patriotism-orwell"}]},{"id":"patriotism-rousseau","q":"patriotism","side":"yes","title":"The love that makes citizens","claim":"A free society needs citizens who will sacrifice for the common good, and love of country is what moves them to.","premises":["A free republic depends on citizens who put the common good above private interest: who serve, vote, pay their share and defend one another.","Love of humanity in general is too thin to move most people to such sacrifices; Rousseau warns against cosmopolitans who love people far away so as to be excused from loving their neighbors.","Love of one's own country is strong enough to turn private individuals into public-spirited citizens."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is a civic virtue that a free society needs.","plain":"People will fill sandbags all night to save their own town in a flood, but rarely for 'towns in general'.","author":"rousseau","source":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Discourse on Political Economy (1755); Emile (1762), Book I; Considerations on the Government of Poland (1772), ch. 4","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"patriotism-kateb","q":"patriotism","side":"no","title":"Loyal to an abstraction","claim":"Patriotism is love of an abstraction, the nation, which in practice means readiness to kill and die for a state.","premises":["'My country' is an abstraction; in practice, patriotic loyalty is loyalty to a state and its government.","Love of such an abstraction easily becomes a readiness to kill and die for it, and to excuse wrongs done in its name.","Public spirit can be built instead on commitment to principles like justice and human rights, which don't divide humanity into us and them."],"conclusion":"Patriotism is a mistake; free societies should rest on loyalty to principles, not to countries.","plain":"A flag can't feel grateful when you die for it.","author":"anon","source":"George Kateb, 'Is Patriotism a Mistake?', Social Research 67 (2000): 901–924","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-rousseau","premise":2},{"type":"attacks","target":"patriotism-macintyre","premise":2}]},{"id":"experience-hedonism","q":"experience","side":"yes","title":"Only experience matters","claim":"Since what makes a life good is how it feels, the machine life would be better.","premises":["The only thing good in itself for a person is pleasure, in the broad sense of enjoyable experience, and the only thing bad in itself is pain.","The machine would give you far more enjoyable experience and far less pain than ordinary life.","So a life in the machine would be better for you."],"conclusion":"You should plug in.","plain":"If a meal tastes perfect, does it matter whether the chef was real?","author":"bentham","source":"Jeremy Bentham, An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), ch. 1; compare Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus 128–129","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"experience-nozick","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"The experience machine","claim":"We would not plug in, which shows that something matters to us besides how our lives feel from the inside.","premises":["We want to actually do certain things, not just have the experience of doing them.","We want to be a certain sort of person; someone floating in a tank is an indeterminate blob, neither brave nor kind nor funny.","Plugging in limits us to a man-made reality, cutting us off from contact with any deeper reality."],"conclusion":"Experience is not all that matters, so hedonism is false and we should not plug in.","plain":"Dreaming you climbed Everest is not the same as climbing it, however good the dream.","author":"nozick","source":"Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), pp. 42–45","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-hedonism","premise":1}]},{"id":"experience-debrigard","q":"experience","side":"yes","title":"The machine in reverse","claim":"Our refusal to plug in seems to come from reluctance to leave the life we know, not from a love of reality.","premises":["When students were told they had been in an experience machine all along and could return to their 'real' life, nearly half chose to stay plugged in; told their real life was in a maximum-security prison, only 13% chose to leave.","So people's answers track which life they already have, a familiar bias toward the status quo, rather than a preference for reality.","An intuition largely explained by a bias like this is weak evidence about what makes a life good."],"conclusion":"Nozick's thought experiment doesn't show that anything besides experience matters.","plain":"People turn down a better phone plan because switching feels like a hassle, not because their old plan is better.","author":"anon","source":"Felipe De Brigard, 'If You Like It, Does It Matter If It's Real?', Philosophical Psychology 23 (2010): 43–57; see also Dan Weijers, 'Nozick's Experience Machine Is Dead, Long Live the Experience Machine!', Philosophical Psychology 27 (2014): 513–535","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-nozick","premise":1}]},{"id":"experience-kagan","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"The deceived businessman","claim":"A man who is secretly betrayed by everyone has a worse life than one who is truly loved, even if their experiences are the same.","premises":["Imagine a businessman who dies believing he was loved by his family and respected by his colleagues, when in fact they all secretly mocked and deceived him.","His experiences were exactly those of a man truly loved, yet his life seems to have gone worse for him than that man's.","Here no status quo bias is at work: we are judging a stranger's whole life from outside."],"conclusion":"Something besides experience, like real love and real achievement, makes a life go well, so the machine life is worse.","plain":"Finding out your best friend only pretended to like you would not feel like learning about a harmless detail.","author":"kagan","source":"Shelly Kagan, 'Me and My Life', Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society 94 (1994): 309–324","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-debrigard","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"experience-nozick"}]},{"id":"experience-crisp","q":"experience","side":"yes","title":"Useful habits, not deep truths","claim":"We value truth and real relationships because, in ordinary life, they lead to good experiences, and our gut reacts even when they no longer do.","premises":["In ordinary life, people who care about truth, real friends and real achievement tend to have much better experiences in the long run.","So we have learned to value these things strongly, as rules of thumb, and we feel their absence even in odd cases where they make no difference to experience.","A rule-of-thumb reaction in an unreal case is weak evidence about what is ultimately good for us."],"conclusion":"The deceived businessman doesn't refute the view that only experience matters.","plain":"We flinch at a photo of a spider, though it can't bite; our alarms were built for real life.","author":"anon","source":"Roger Crisp, 'Hedonism Reconsidered', Philosophy and Phenomenological Research 73 (2006): 619–645","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-kagan","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"experience-hedonism"}]},{"id":"experience-philebus","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"The life of an oyster","claim":"A life of the greatest pleasure without thought or truth would not be a human good.","premises":["Imagine a life full of the greatest pleasures but with no mind: no memory, no knowledge, no true beliefs, not even awareness that you are enjoying yourself.","That would be the life of a jellyfish or an oyster, not of a human being, and no one would choose it.","So pleasure alone is not the good; a good life needs thought and truth too."],"conclusion":"The best life is not simply the most pleasant one.","plain":"A jellyfish drifting in warm water may feel fine all day, but you wouldn't swap lives with it.","author":"plato","source":"Plato, Philebus 21a–d","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"experience-mill","q":"experience","side":"yes","title":"Higher pleasures","claim":"A sensible hedonist values the pleasures of the mind most, and the machine can supply those too.","premises":["Pleasures differ in quality as well as amount; those who know both kinds prefer the pleasures of the intellect, feelings and imagination.","So a sensible hedonist doesn't recommend the oyster's life: it is better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied.","The machine, unlike the oyster's life, can give you these higher pleasures as well: the thrill of discovery, deep conversation, creative work."],"conclusion":"The oyster objection misses: the machine life could be rich in the pleasures that matter most.","plain":"Nobody said the machine only plays cartoons; it can give you the joy of solving a hard problem too.","author":"mill","source":"John Stuart Mill, Utilitarianism (1861), ch. 2","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-philebus","premise":3}]},{"id":"experience-fake-discovery","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"Discovery needs something discovered","claim":"The machine can't give you the higher pleasures as they really are, only imitations of them.","premises":["The pleasure of discovery is the pleasure of coming to know a truth; the pleasure of friendship is the pleasure of being understood by someone.","In the machine you would discover nothing and be understood by no one: your 'proof' was never checked, your 'friends' were never there.","So what the machine supplies are imitations of Mill's higher pleasures, not the real thing."],"conclusion":"Even Mill's refined hedonism points beyond experience, to contact with truth and with other people.","plain":"Solving a crossword where the answers are secretly changed to fit whatever you write isn't solving anything.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; compare Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), pp. 42–45, and Plato, Philebus 36c–41a (on false pleasures)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-mill","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"experience-philebus"}]},{"id":"experience-aristotle","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"Happiness is an activity","claim":"A good life consists in doing and being, not just in feeling, so a machine that replaces activity with its appearance can't supply it.","premises":["No one would choose to live their whole life with the mind of a child, however much they enjoyed what children enjoy.","So we value some things, like seeing, knowing and acting well, even if no pleasure came from them.","Happiness is an activity of the soul in accordance with virtue, carried out over a complete life."],"conclusion":"A good life needs real activity, which the machine replaces with the mere experience of it.","plain":"Watching a video of yourself scoring a goal you never scored doesn't make you a footballer.","author":"aristotle","source":"Aristotle, Nicomachean Ethics I.7 (1098a16–18) and X.3 (1174a1–8)","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"experience-chalmers","q":"experience","side":"yes","title":"Virtual is not fake","claim":"Virtual worlds are real worlds, and a life lived in a good one can be a good life.","premises":["Virtual objects are real digital objects, and what happens in a virtual world really happens; virtual reality is not an illusion.","In a virtual world shared with other people, you can have real relationships, make real choices and achieve real things.","So 'it isn't real' is not, by itself, a good objection to living in a machine-made world."],"conclusion":"A life in a well-made virtual world can be as good as a life outside one.","plain":"Friends you met in an online game are still your friends.","author":"chalmers","source":"David Chalmers, Reality+: Virtual Worlds and the Problems of Philosophy (2022), ch. 17","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"experience-not-shared","q":"experience","side":"no","title":"That's not Nozick's machine","claim":"A shared virtual world with real people is very different from a private machine that deceives you.","premises":["The case for virtual life depends on sharing a virtual world with other real people and making real choices there, knowing where you are.","Nozick's machine is different: it is programmed for you alone, the 'people' in it are props, and you are made to forget you plugged in.","So the argument shows at most that some virtual lives are good, not that the experience machine life is."],"conclusion":"The experience machine remains a bad bargain, even if virtual worlds can be good places to live.","plain":"Moving to a new city is one thing; being drugged so you think you live in a city that's really a film set is another.","author":"anon","source":"Distinction drawn in David Chalmers, Reality+ (2022), ch. 17; Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), pp. 42–45","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"experience-chalmers","premise":3}]},{"id":"timetravel-grandfather","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"The grandfather paradox","claim":"If you could travel into the past, you could prevent your own birth, which is a contradiction.","premises":["If you could travel to the past, you could do there anything an ordinary person can do, such as stop your young grandfather from meeting your grandmother.","If you did, you would never be born, so you could not have traveled back to do it.","Whatever leads to a contradiction is impossible."],"conclusion":"Travel into the past is impossible.","plain":"You can't erase the pencil that is doing the erasing.","author":"anon","source":"The 'grandfather paradox'; stated and discussed in David Lewis, 'The Paradoxes of Time Travel', American Philosophical Quarterly 13 (1976): 145–152; see SEP, 'Time Travel' (Nicholas J.J. Smith)","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"timetravel-lewis-can","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"Can and can't","claim":"A time traveler can kill his grandfather in one sense and can't in another, and that is no contradiction.","premises":["In one sense Tim can kill his grandfather: he has the gun, the skill and the opportunity, as much as any assassin.","In another sense he can't: given the fact that Grandfather lived to have children, Tim will somehow fail, perhaps by slipping on a banana peel or losing his nerve.","Saying Tim both 'can' and 'can't' just uses 'can' in two senses; what is impossible is changing the past, not traveling to it."],"conclusion":"The grandfather paradox shows that time travelers can't change the past, not that time travel is impossible.","plain":"You 'can' sing a song you know, though if you're being recorded singing something else, you won't.","author":"lewis","source":"David Lewis, 'The Paradoxes of Time Travel', American Philosophical Quarterly 13 (1976): 145–152","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-grandfather","premise":1}]},{"id":"timetravel-vihvelin","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"Bound to fail means can't","claim":"If a time traveler would fail however often he tried, then in the ordinary sense he simply can't do it.","premises":["Ordinarily, if it's true that you would fail no matter how hard or how often you tried, then you can't do the thing.","For Tim, it's true that if he tried to kill his grandfather, he would fail.","So Tim lacks an ability that any ordinary person in his place would have, and Lewis's 'one sense in which he can' doesn't survive."],"conclusion":"Time travelers would face strange limits on what they can do, a real cost for the view that time travel is possible.","plain":"If the door won't open whatever key, crowbar or shoulder you try, it's odd to insist you're able to open it.","author":"anon","source":"Kadri Vihvelin, 'What Time Travelers Cannot Do', Philosophical Studies 81 (1996): 315–330","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-lewis-can","premise":1}]},{"id":"timetravel-horwich","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"Too many banana peels","claim":"Time travel would need endless improbable coincidences, so we have strong reason to think it never happens.","premises":["If time travel were common, many travelers would try to harm their own past, and every attempt would have to fail.","Each failure needs some fluke, like a jammed gun, a slip or a sudden change of heart; a long run of such flukes would be wildly improbable.","Whatever would require wildly improbable coincidences is something we have strong reason to think won't happen."],"conclusion":"Even if time travel is logically possible, we have strong reason to believe it will never actually occur.","plain":"If every burglar who tried your door tripped over the same loose step, you'd suspect something odd was going on.","author":"anon","source":"Paul Horwich, Asymmetries in Time: Problems in the Philosophy of Science (1987), ch. 7","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-lewis-can","premise":2}]},{"id":"timetravel-smith","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"Not a fluke if it's already happened","claim":"Given what we already know about the past, the failure of every attempt to change it is certain, not improbable.","premises":["Coincidences look improbable only if we forget what we already know: that the grandfather lived.","Given that fact, the failure of every attempt on him is no fluke but a certainty, just as it's certain that no one will undo the fact that you ate breakfast this morning.","And if few people ever try to harm their own past, few odd failures are needed anyway."],"conclusion":"The coincidences argument doesn't show that time travel won't happen.","plain":"Every attempt to make 1066 not have happened is guaranteed to fail; that's not luck, it's history.","author":"anon","source":"Nicholas J.J. Smith, 'Bananas Enough for Time Travel?', British Journal for the Philosophy of Science 48 (1997): 363–389; Theodore Sider, 'Time Travel, Coincidences and Counterfactuals', Philosophical Studies 110 (2002): 115–138","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-horwich","premise":2},{"type":"replies","target":"timetravel-lewis-can"}]},{"id":"timetravel-novikov","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"The billiard ball test","claim":"Physicists who modeled paradox-like situations found that the laws of physics allow consistent histories every time.","premises":["Physicists asked what happens if a billiard ball goes into a time-traveling wormhole, comes out in the past and knocks its younger self off course, so that it never goes in.","For every starting setup they studied, they found consistent histories: for example, the older ball gives its younger self a glancing blow that sends it into the wormhole on just the right path.","This suggests the laws of physics only allow self-consistent histories, so true paradoxes never arise."],"conclusion":"Self-consistent travel into the past fits with the laws of physics.","plain":"Like a well-written time-travel film, the story always loops back to match itself.","author":"anon","source":"Fernando Echeverria, Gunnar Klinkhammer and Kip Thorne, 'Billiard Balls in Wormhole Spacetimes with Closed Timelike Curves: Classical Theory', Physical Review D 44 (1991): 1077–1099; John Friedman et al., 'Cauchy Problem in Spacetimes with Closed Timelike Curves', Physical Review D 42 (1990): 1915–1930","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"timetravel-branches","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"A different branch","claim":"On the many-worlds view of quantum physics, a time traveler arrives in another branch of history, where changing things causes no contradiction.","premises":["On the many-worlds view of quantum mechanics, reality contains many branching histories.","Deutsch argued that on this view, a traveler into the past would arrive in a different branch, where their own birth need not happen.","So they could stop their grandfather meeting their grandmother there without undoing their own existence."],"conclusion":"The grandfather paradox has a consistent resolution.","plain":"You change the ending, but in a different copy of the book from the one you started reading.","author":"anon","source":"David Deutsch, 'Quantum Mechanics near Closed Timelike Lines', Physical Review D 44 (1991): 3197–3217; David Deutsch and Michael Lockwood, 'The Quantum Physics of Time Travel', Scientific American 270 (March 1994): 68–74","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-grandfather","premise":2}]},{"id":"timetravel-not-past","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"That's not your past","claim":"Arriving in another branch is travel to a world like your past, not to your past.","premises":["In another branch, the man you stop is not the grandfather you descend from, only someone very like him.","Traveling to a world that merely resembles your past is not traveling into your own past, any more than visiting a perfect replica of 1950s London is visiting 1950.","So the branching escape avoids the paradox by changing the subject."],"conclusion":"Branching gives us travel between worlds, not travel into our own past.","plain":"Visiting your twin's house isn't the same as going home, however similar the furniture.","author":"anon","source":"Standard objection; see SEP, 'Time Travel' (Nicholas J.J. Smith), on time travel in branching time","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-branches","premise":3}]},{"id":"timetravel-godel","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"Einstein's equations allow it","claim":"Our best theory of space and time contains solutions in which travel into the past is possible.","premises":["Einstein's general theory of relativity is our best theory of space, time and gravity.","In 1949 Kurt Gödel found a solution to its equations, describing a rotating universe, in which a traveler could journey into their own past.","Later physicists found other solutions, such as certain wormholes, that also contain paths into the past."],"conclusion":"Our best physics does not rule out time travel.","plain":"The rulebook of the universe, read carefully, seems to leave a back door open.","author":"godel","source":"Kurt Gödel, 'An Example of a New Type of Cosmological Solutions of Einstein's Field Equations of Gravitation', Reviews of Modern Physics 21 (1949): 447–450; Michael Morris, Kip Thorne and Ulvi Yurtsever, 'Wormholes, Time Machines, and the Weak Energy Condition', Physical Review Letters 61 (1988): 1446–1449","status":"canonical","links":[]},{"id":"timetravel-hawking","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"Chronology protection","claim":"The laws of physics probably act to destroy any time machine before it can be used.","premises":["Calculations suggest that as a time machine was about to form, quantum effects would build up without limit and could destroy it.","Hawking proposed that the laws of physics always prevent travel into the past in this way: the 'chronology protection conjecture'.","He added, half jokingly, that there is strong evidence for it: we have not been invaded by hordes of tourists from the future."],"conclusion":"Physics probably forbids time machines.","plain":"The universe may be like a museum that locks the doors whenever anyone tries to take a shortcut through the past.","author":"anon","source":"Stephen Hawking, 'Chronology Protection Conjecture', Physical Review D 46 (1992): 603–611","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-godel","premise":3}]},{"id":"timetravel-tourists","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"No tourists yet","claim":"On the best-studied designs, you can't travel back to before the first time machine was built, so the lack of visitors proves nothing.","premises":["In the wormhole designs physicists have studied, you can only travel back as far as the moment the time machine was first switched on.","No time machine has been built yet, so no one from the future could visit us now.","So the lack of tourists from the future is just what we'd expect even if time travel is possible; and chronology protection itself remains an unproved conjecture."],"conclusion":"The tourists argument is no evidence against time travel.","plain":"Nobody can take a train to a station before the railway was built.","author":"anon","source":"Kip Thorne, Black Holes and Time Warps: Einstein's Outrageous Legacy (1994), ch. 14; Morris, Thorne and Yurtsever (1988)","status":"reconstruction","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-hawking","premise":3},{"type":"replies","target":"timetravel-godel"}]},{"id":"timetravel-bootstrap","q":"timetravel","side":"no","title":"Knowledge from nowhere","claim":"Travel into the past would let information appear with no one ever having created it, which seems impossible.","premises":["Suppose you travel back with a copy of Hamlet and give it to the young Shakespeare, who copies it out and publishes it; your copy was later printed from his.","Then no one ever wrote Hamlet: its words came from nowhere.","Knowledge can only come into the world through a creative process, like trial and error; it can't simply exist uncreated."],"conclusion":"Time travel of the ordinary, single-history kind would create knowledge from nothing, so it is ruled out.","plain":"A recipe that no cook ever invented, passed in a loop forever, is a free lunch the universe shouldn't serve.","author":"anon","source":"The 'knowledge paradox' of time travel; see David Deutsch and Michael Lockwood, 'The Quantum Physics of Time Travel', Scientific American 270 (March 1994): 68–74","status":"reconstruction","links":[]},{"id":"timetravel-lewis-loop","q":"timetravel","side":"yes","title":"Strange, but not impossible","claim":"A causal loop is inexplicable as a whole, but that makes it strange, not impossible.","premises":["In a causal loop, each event is caused by others in the loop, but the loop as a whole has no outside cause.","So asking where the information came from in the first place has no answer: the parts of the loop are explained, the whole is not.","That is strange, but not so different from other inexplicable facts we accept, like the existence of a universe at all."],"conclusion":"Causal loops are puzzling, not contradictory, so they don't rule out time travel.","plain":"Asking who first wrote the looping Hamlet is like asking what came before the very first moment.","author":"lewis","source":"David Lewis, 'The Paradoxes of Time Travel', American Philosophical Quarterly 13 (1976): 145–152, at p. 149","status":"canonical","links":[{"type":"attacks","target":"timetravel-bootstrap","premise":3}]}]};
